[{"body":"The completed independent preparation-comparison packets identify duplicate proposed preparations, source-continuity conflicts and unsupported claims of disjointness. This issue tracks only the bounded family decisions below. These are isolated proposals; no record merge, new namespace, scientific approval or export release is authorized by the review.\n\nAll three sealed packets were independently hash-checked: Worker01 878 files, Worker05 390, Worker10 526, with no hash mismatch or unlisted file in the ignored-inclusive inventories. Their assignments partition the 67 mechanical family leads (26/22/19); this means comparison coverage, not 67 resolved or approved identities. The exact 28 retained finding/obligation IDs are reduced to 26 rows in `.temp/all-unmatched-20261107/preflight/family-findings-triage-v1/findings-matrix-v2.json`. Original authors, skeptical reviews, proposal bytes, primary capture times/hashes, source cases and occurrences remain immutable.\n\nA source accession, filename, receiving dose, missing carrier, unknown pH or unspecified hydrate does not prove a distinct supplied preparation. Nor does it prove equality. Source-qualified partial proposals remain useful; overlapping candidates need a defined scope or the missing evidence before simultaneous installation. No unknown is filled by guessing. Source-batch water and final-volume instructions remain separate, and immediate-list completeness is independent of molecular grounding.\n\n| Findings / frozen entries | Sourced conclusion and bounded next action |\n|---|---|\n| W01-FPC-PA-001 + MIN-F03; 573/810, maintained ATCC_Wolfes_Vitamin_Mix | JCM197, NBRC837 and NBRC903 give the same ten nominal vitamins and amounts plus1L water. Prepare one combined nominal candidate preserving all source-specific processing. The maintained ATCC owner has authentic branded MD-VS history and a D-(+) calcium-pantothenate specification; JCM/NBRC only say calcium pantothenate. Preserve that distinction in what the evidence asserts: unspecified stereo proves neither sameness nor difference. Do not automatically reuse ATCC or install two source-number owners. Establish the applicability bridge or explicitly decide nominal-recipe versus supplied-product scope before co-installation. |\n| MIN-F01; 624/657 | DSM590/1144 Hutner tables and preparation match at nominal scope. Preserve CaCl2·2H2O3.335g versus3.34g rounding and both malformed molybdate strings as literal unresolved assertions; do not turn a typographical discrepancy into a different chemical. Prepare one source-union nominal recipe candidate. Both underlying Metals44 instructions are retained; the current automatically extracted portal is not a historical erratum. |\n| MIN-F02, W05-CR-F02; 381–384/624/657/705 | Wolfe JCM470/DSM792, modified Wolfe ATCC1190/1191 versus JCM221, and Hutner JCM900/979 comparisons have explicit unresolved hydrate/reagent or preparation details. Correct claims that missing qualifiers prove incompatibility. Do not infer JCM470's variable Mn hydrate as monohydrate, or transfer ATCC final-volume/form-specific instructions to JCM221. Preserve the positively different JCM149 MgSO4 quantity14.45g versus29.7g. |\n| W01-FPC-PA-003; 747 plus existing JCM403 B12 owner | NBRC1321 explicitly gives cyanocobalamin5mg with100ml water; JCM403 specifies generic B12 at matching nominal quantities. Preserve both levels of specificity. Missing JCM vitamer/preparation evidence is an unresolved relation, not a proved difference or an exact identity bridge. Coordinate the existing JCM403 promotion/pointer correction under1201/1227; do not create a competing owner by accession alone. |\n| W01-PREP-B-001; 619 | NBRC1507 names0.1M Tris/HCl pH7.5 stock but does not list free hydrochloric acid as a separate component. Remove that unsupported free-acid has-part claim. Preserve the literal named buffer, strength and pH with unknown complete composition; do not substitute dry Tris-HCl salt or invent solvent/neutralization. |\n| W01-PREP-B-002 + CHEM-COMP-003; 669 | JCM1479 positively specifies a5% L-cysteine-HCl-monohydrate stock atpH7. Unreported pH of other5% stocks does not establish a different substance. Correct the comparison wording, retain stock/solid separation and the unknown percentage basis/carrier/titrant. |\n| CHEM-COMP-002; 6/13 | NBRC1020 prints Na2WO4; JCM317 explicitly prints dihydrate, both at4mg with4mg selenite and1L water. Remove the claim that omission proves anhydrous NBRC1020 or disjointness. Preserve both literal tables and ask only for the missing supplied form if full chemical equivalence is needed. |\n| CHEM-COMP-004; 943/944/946/106 | Literal35%,32%,25% HCl and qualitative concentrated labels have unknown comparable bases; M2305/M2746 even call their25% input concentrated. Correct categorical disjointness without converting percentages or inventing a carrier. Retain source qualification; an explicit common partial-material scope or comparable formulation evidence is needed before consolidation. |\n| BIO-COMP-005; 328/333 with283/287/288/290 | Historical MJ and JCM1216 0.1x artificial-seawater formulations remain partly unspecified. Missing recipe/version is not evidence of a different formulation. Preserve the source-qualified preparations, positive known differences and exact missing historical MJ/1x-base specification; no guessed formula or wholesale seawater merge. |\n| BIO-COMP-001; 201/476 | CM0643 prepared broth and dry powder are different supplied forms. The source handles for entry201 use1000ml/10ml broth; entry476 weighs33g powder. Correct entry201's dry-powder representation and account for its authentic existing broth provenance. Retain powder separate from prepared broth, water and receiving supplements; the same catalogue does not erase the supplied-form boundary. MICRO:0002186 describes liquid, so any use requires whole-broth§3 scope review and must not identify powder. |\n| BIO-F02; 971 + Swine_Serum | The new DSM1076b heat-inactivated swine-serum proposal uses MediaDive960, already the creation source of Swine_Serum/UNMAPPED_0536. Reconcile or promote through that authentic preimage/history. Do not add a second same-source owner or label the separate generic Porcine_Serum/MD967 untreated. Heat schedule remains unknown. |\n| BIO-F03; 970 + Bovine_Calf_Serum | JCM1303 heat-inactivated calf serum uses MediaDive2061, already the import source of Bovine_Calf_Serum. Audit its broader/Gibco occurrences and partition their applicability before narrowing or adding an owner. Missing historical heat metadata is not an untreated claim. No wholesale repurposing or maintained-owner merge. |\n| BIO-F04; 893/908/914/916/922 | Withdraw unsupported serum exclusions: bovine does not exclude fetal; gamma irradiation does not exclude heat treatment; unspecified heat schedule need not differ from a specified one. Retain positive age/species/catalogue/process facts and actual30/45/60minute or active/inactivated differences. No forced product equivalence. |\n| BIO-F05; 904/906 | JCM946 supports56C30min heating and filtration before addition, but not their relative order. Clarify only that ordering claim. DSM1021 explicitly requires sterile fetal/newborn alternatives; preserve sterility for the selected newborn scope without inventing heat or filter treatment. |\n| BIO-F06; 300/921 | Oxoid CM0331 and TOGO M2224 disclose the same nominal dry base: special peptone23g, starch1g, NaCl5g, agar10g, pH7.3±0.2. Prepare one combined formulation candidate while retaining which source actually states the catalogue. Before installation, account for existing Columbia_Blood_Agar_Base's mixed Oxoid/Difco provenance; do not repurpose the entire owner. Preserve the distinct Difco/BBL/EH formulations. Blood and reconstitution water are not dry-base parts. |\n| BIO-F07; 980 | Replace a search-wrapper-as-primary assertion with the recovered2009 BD manufacturer manual capture, p158. Keep original wrapper, older manual edition and failed URLs as historical evidence. This verifies the nominal Difco formulation, not a historical lot or arbitrary vendor equivalence. |\n| BIO-F08; 919 | Current ATCC0988 is explicitly reformulated asMD2611 and specifies ATCC30-2020 plus55C treatment; frozen TOGO M2578 preserves older fetal/newborn alternatives and unknown schedule. Keep both versions and their exact applicability. Same URL is not same document; this is a version obligation, not a finding that the frozen transcription is false. |\n| CHEM-COMP-006; 35/36 | Reassess new claim-specific resolver dispositions. The fully specified0.01M FeSO4·7H2O in0.02N HCl name is not shown to be a non-name merely because a global alias was undesirable. Bicarbonate footnote pointers have different ambiguity. Preserve all existing rejected entries; do not blanket-reactivate or blanket-reject authentic labels. Under§3, any new rejection needs claim-specific evidence; owned-name suppression applies only when its ownership condition holds. Historical1173 lineage is not a new scientific decision. |\n\nAdditional reconciliation groups with positive evidence can proceed to isolated candidate preparation, with exact-byte independent review before application:\n\n- DAIGO395-01681 entries188/474: one named dry product, eight manufacturer components. Preserve the manufacturer/JCM peptone wording and exact hydrate, the3.15g listed sum versus3.2g preparation instruction, and both histories. Do not invent another constituent, agar, water, generic DSM1537 applicability or wholesale KG1402 routing. The local identifier is already shared; choose one readable destination by the product scope, not author order.\n- Mixed phosphate entries873/940: one nominal preparation from equal volumes of1M KH2PO4 and1M Na2HPO4. Preserve50+50ml versus10+10ml batches and PBBM-onlypH7.2. The immediate input list can be complete without claiming its input stocks' exact hydrate, solvent or neutralization chemistry.\n- HEPES-KOH1M entries449/450 (W05-CR-F01): both sources positively name the same nominal strength/neutralizer; receiving-mediumpH7.14/9.0 is not stockpH. Recommend an explicit common bounded nominal scope if that is the intended owner model; otherwise retain candidate holds for actual stockpH/carrier/neutralization evidence. Do not install two proved-distinct preparations based solely on source numbers.\n- 95% ethanol entries460–463 (W05-CR-F03): preserve proven within-protocol reuse and every occurrence. A shared bounded partial-material scope is an option, but neither four distinct complete formulations nor equality follows without percentage basis/carrier/denaturant/product evidence. Do not infer95%v/v aqueous NCIT identity. Missing composition is not permission to discard the supported partial material.\n\nExisting trackers are reused, not duplicated:1206 JCM852;1210 sodium-thiosulfate tetrahydrate;1202 Polypeptone;1212 Phytone/SoyPeptone;1217 W06 source/solvent provenance;1221 EBIOS;1204 rumen. For rumen the current sourced decision explicitly leaves the three dependent parents' literal rumen-fluid components UNMAPPED; the older comparison suggestion to redirect them to clarified fluid is superseded, not fresh evidence. The Tris-HCl parent-grade defect is filed separately because its exact record correction/native repro is distinct from these formulation decisions.\n\nPrimary source anchors: [JCM197](https://www.jcm.riken.jp/cgi-bin/jcm/jcm_grmd?GRMD=197), [NBRC903](https://www.nite.go.jp/nbrc/catalogue/NBRCMediumDetailServlet?NO=903), [ATCC2672](https://www.atcc.org/-/media/product-assets/documents/microbial-media-formulations/2/6/7/2/atcc-medium-2672.pdf?rev=7f27672230474579b9151e4c2ec9f20a), [DSM590](https://www.dsmz.de/microorganisms/medium/pdf/DSMZ_Medium590.pdf), [DSM1144](https://www.dsmz.de/microorganisms/medium/pdf/DSMZ_Medium1144.pdf), [JCM470](https://www.jcm.riken.jp/cgi-bin/jcm/jcm_grmd?GRMD=470), [NBRC1507](https://www.nite.go.jp/nbrc/catalogue/NBRCMediumDetailServlet?NO=1507), [DSM1076b](https://www.dsmz.de/microorganisms/medium/pdf/DSMZ_Medium1076b.pdf), [JCM1303](https://www.jcm.riken.jp/cgi-bin/jcm/jcm_grmd?GRMD=1303), [Oxoid CM0331 specification](https://documents.thermofisher.com/TFS-Assets/MBD/Specification-Sheets/PS-CM0331-V6.pdf), [TOGO M2224](https://togomedium.org/sparqlist/api/gmdb_medium_by_gmid?gm_id=M2224), [BD2009 manual](https://fsl.nmsu.edu/documents/difcobblmanual_2nded_lowres.pdf), [DAIGO manufacturer](https://labchem-wako.fujifilm.com/jp/product/detail/W01N0139-0168.html). Every capture's exact original URL/time/hash and case binding is retained in the triage indexes and source packets; links alone are not treated as immutable evidence.\n\nDo not apply the original frozen W06 payloads just because their historical paths still exist. Six selected W06 payloads and its new EBIOS proposal have advanced and need exact successor comparisons. W07's observed successor was metadata-only in the source comparison, but its actual selected bytes still require final intake readback. All source, role and release holds remain content-bound.\n\nExact frozen source origins retained for these findings (later successors require explicit bridges):\n\n| Entry | Original proposal | Source head | Exact payload SHA256 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 6 | `w01::NSV4-PROP-NBRC_1020_Se_W_Solution` | `26445f23b5a905ac26d864acfbd658570c88769c` | `235072e64c69a7dc51f9386f0664d69ffab234a5a0cca427cf4b4b18b37de0e7` |\n| 13 | `w01::NSV4-PROP-JCM_317_Se_W_Solution` | `26445f23b5a905ac26d864acfbd658570c88769c` | `1603001c438fc6e321f1f105cff5ebd5a9f2be169e5657493de1d8b49a4ace62` |\n| 35 | `w01::CS-PROP-JCM1430_FERROUS_SULFATE_HCL_STOCK` | `26445f23b5a905ac26d864acfbd658570c88769c` | `6e0ae3d8ec0984d7e29034b2fd41d3589f26fb7f236bdcc7a829d632837e0c59` |\n| 36 | `w01::CS-PROP-NBRC1321_CO2_BICARBONATE_STOCK` | `26445f23b5a905ac26d864acfbd658570c88769c` | `61c0a226a8a99d96b43657f6e00075e901ce051a698b01bd99f6ab6f1d88717c` |\n| 106 | `w02::a2-proposal-togo-m2746-m2305-hydrochloric-acid-25percent` | `0597d00417be4d7cdfb5a31970043eec9bbc88fe` | `70c016d0e74a8487668258f66ab8ae63722e4ecbf6cce3b4c7890caaa9054a51` |\n| 188 | `w03::b-proposal-daigo-r2a` | `1196d5bc3aaba7563b829d5cbe7e54722fccc35d` | `5603b8878ca3460906dcc26b33c29db602661773eb3a598ad293ae7cd525b22f` |\n| 201 | `w03::b-proposal-cm0643` | `1196d5bc3aaba7563b829d5cbe7e54722fccc35d` | `1646156e0153d035e585214dd25eaa6275a423bc41920aa34cc68a02992aa4e6` |\n| 283 | `w04::BIO-PROPOSAL-jcm_492_modified_mj_synthetic_seawater` | `548e7bdd0ce2a68b3ae0df6ad7a6150a4e5c9bb9` | `ddd35feefd2df9b1f2a3d721f5b3d89926988efc6e7210a1269e243d324d8f16` |\n| 287 | `w04::BIO-PROPOSAL-jcm_651_artificial_seawater_05x` | `548e7bdd0ce2a68b3ae0df6ad7a6150a4e5c9bb9` | `0adde845da6ee89a25d28a821e889727dd5136c6ae3015c4e6eb17781dc9095a` |\n| 288 | `w04::BIO-PROPOSAL-jcm_910_artificial_seawater_1x` | `548e7bdd0ce2a68b3ae0df6ad7a6150a4e5c9bb9` | `4557113d7ff49fdd8722ba409e1d5e1016d44b5f51f528a644c0048d9eeaa107` |\n| 290 | `w04::BIO-PROPOSAL-jcm_1269_artificial_seawater_4x` | `548e7bdd0ce2a68b3ae0df6ad7a6150a4e5c9bb9` | `71008fd600cde88d17a6315c0147b00ca48890df2cb53ce643c794853640899c` |\n| 300 | `w04::media-followup-oxoid_cm0331_columbia_blood_agar_base` | `548e7bdd0ce2a68b3ae0df6ad7a6150a4e5c9bb9` | `560e2e3301550c952b729956e1a04c87941304888130adbe52a254e747207e69` |\n| 328 | `w04::I1201-BIO-P-m3127_mj_synthetic_seawater` | `548e7bdd0ce2a68b3ae0df6ad7a6150a4e5c9bb9` | `fcf44fd32461fdd13b84b33eed72aee991591f78cdbde06433389d0ea104e7a1` |\n| 333 | `w04::I1201-BIO-P-jcm1216_artificial_seawater_01x` | `548e7bdd0ce2a68b3ae0df6ad7a6150a4e5c9bb9` | `227335e425995406a3e50df0ea4fe1a897a429756bd16acdee59057059a37104` |\n| 381 | `w05::R1193-P-wolfe470` | `43f7cf6e8681fa617b5d6d3529d94d537ca01f0d` | `59fdf6607b5ae8add60ff70972c6b2ac143e7fe0d5212b50896cb886315c2069` |\n| 382 | `w05::R1193-P-wolfe1190` | `43f7cf6e8681fa617b5d6d3529d94d537ca01f0d` | `8efc7361c2a14db7279703d3c824b7b43c36afdb8fbfe6ec0972e52d7405eade` |\n| 383 | `w05::R1193-P-wolfe221` | `43f7cf6e8681fa617b5d6d3529d94d537ca01f0d` | `ce7ea091fa3d6d935e7020dea064033aee56cbaee90603adab3e624ccbe7fb77` |\n| 384 | `w05::R1193-P-wolfe792` | `43f7cf6e8681fa617b5d6d3529d94d537ca01f0d` | `b8ccaea9515441fbee3edb15d5edf3951f2c4729278a82e3219ae751890e5ea4` |\n| 449 | `w05::CHEM-C1193-HEPES-1221` | `43f7cf6e8681fa617b5d6d3529d94d537ca01f0d` | `05cf4a1b09aff0fd1954ef0ac1aa563844414f4896261eaca48271323d9f8a46` |\n| 450 | `w05::CHEM-C1193-HEPES-1474` | `43f7cf6e8681fa617b5d6d3529d94d537ca01f0d` | `6ba28f7de8b9b6387fb38f44ec01ce9b9626a84bea256ea63b7d0df07b109fad` |\n| 460 | `w05::CHEM-C1193-ethanol95-atcc_1490` | `43f7cf6e8681fa617b5d6d3529d94d537ca01f0d` | `84691f529ce8d1e146d2dc2c37b1ecc87027e2cf52ccd60007d6908d8bb5fe14` |\n| 461 | `w05::CHEM-C1193-ethanol95-atcc_1257` | `43f7cf6e8681fa617b5d6d3529d94d537ca01f0d` | `fa52d73955f5d54be470eaa21c1de4ef59eda6b6fe95431d6a37cf9782975365` |\n| 462 | `w05::CHEM-C1193-ethanol95-atcc_1524` | `43f7cf6e8681fa617b5d6d3529d94d537ca01f0d` | `3da7ef96a6d1020c39ca9663c045a913acc0b68cc060b674304399fe62f576cc` |\n| 463 | `w05::CHEM-C1193-ethanol95-dsmz_78` | `43f7cf6e8681fa617b5d6d3529d94d537ca01f0d` | `5718e7de2922144c0e8b8bc519b6fddb637989975d99f0d7737dc4335ac96a9d` |\n| 474 | `w05::M1193-P-R2A-DAIGO` | `43f7cf6e8681fa617b5d6d3529d94d537ca01f0d` | `82a75f1ba268a903d32a8b62b2b8ee918ad6ac2ba7591b084f99a27702bd4dc7` |\n| 476 | `w05::M1193-P-CM0643` | `43f7cf6e8681fa617b5d6d3529d94d537ca01f0d` | `4c9ad4e62517234ca0cb7b4c58f6dbd33f4fe8319a2f1ba36d9e7752f05fc9b0` |\n| 573 | `w06::C-P-JCM197` | `1b3641853c7105b9091f81b5a9b19c99e8ed351e` | `de17dee3c912e05f2ac460ef85d296a8f6d0a20a175de173233a41cb522b8063` |\n| 619 | `w07::A-PROPOSAL-NBRC1507_Tris_HCl_01M_pH75_Buffer` | `fbfa032435ea1c399ae9b98bfedadb9ed639430d` | `fee1ee6d50153341ac16a466551a95edcf47ad2b3a532a72e283a7205837bbdd` |\n| 624 | `w07::A-PROPOSAL-DSM590_Hutner_Basal_Salts` | `fbfa032435ea1c399ae9b98bfedadb9ed639430d` | `51024df2e489d3a58ad0ed9d6d1fbe3f0928d64610edb295b03b4e25e5dc9f27` |\n| 657 | `w07::B-PROPOSAL-dsmz_1144_hutner_basal_salts` | `fbfa032435ea1c399ae9b98bfedadb9ed639430d` | `53c49c566103d5019f448a8b930fee2206b31f41a1769a325f467e808f21c4fe` |\n| 669 | `w07::B-PROPOSAL-jcm_1479_l_cysteine_hcl_monohydrate_5_percent_ph7` | `fbfa032435ea1c399ae9b98bfedadb9ed639430d` | `319ca5792df1c451e9174b686a18973f3ee912d388d96032dd97a3e82f5a23d0` |\n| 705 | `w07::W07-COMBINED-JCM900-JCM979-Hutner` | `fbfa032435ea1c399ae9b98bfedadb9ed639430d` | `86111586f96f9e38d02196b814304dd9458cc026bec53bd2240aec4ca351a9bd` |\n| 747 | `w08::vm-prop-new-31` | `b90a2fd4681d20e2586481b7b9b1114a78281b94` | `808564e03dd9d66dcabd795be5aa2d7609420a38b9aefbecc9d895ffbd2cacbe` |\n| 810 | `w08::vm1201-prop-nbrc903-vitamin` | `b90a2fd4681d20e2586481b7b9b1114a78281b94` | `fa908ae82f6299b49c9a0482a402949c660ab313fdd068cf65ada44c77b40006` |\n| 873 | `w10::buffers-phosphate-jcm822` | `468766d941e35a74eb2de3c95096660987277d9b` | `c2f559cedecb1270bce19cc07dac43ed19ed2f8efb53efc2efeb903204c73f8c` |\n| 893 | `w10::sv1-bio-gamma_irradiated_fetal_calf_serum_10109_155` | `468766d941e35a74eb2de3c95096660987277d9b` | `0f253626c7fac077f12cceea251a5d307fcf22d377610b30d8bef26f30cf559d` |\n| 904 | `w10::sv1-bio-filtered_rabbit_serum_56c_30min_jcm946` | `468766d941e35a74eb2de3c95096660987277d9b` | `942a42edce551c2377fe68662c95cbb21fb6b913319e7b41ea5132002e047962` |\n| 906 | `w10::sv1-bio-newborn_calf_serum_komodo1021` | `468766d941e35a74eb2de3c95096660987277d9b` | `be1e3746aa5ff9109e106749eaed43af63bf46fbbf9295c7ceaeccf564cbe43f` |\n| 908 | `w10::sv1-bio-sterile_bovine_serum_56c_45min_dsmz840` | `468766d941e35a74eb2de3c95096660987277d9b` | `cef9565281a570305c4b928642d8b59132631e5a1c415421ddb014de3f090f65` |\n| 914 | `w10::sv1-bio-heat_inactivated_fbs_atcc1827` | `468766d941e35a74eb2de3c95096660987277d9b` | `ffd332a7fa36db0467d91f7f5bd6a66ada2cd5f47c512a3b14e21af589f581a4` |\n| 916 | `w10::sv1-bio-horse_serum_56c_30min_atcc243` | `468766d941e35a74eb2de3c95096660987277d9b` | `dd5b85930b18b642234b61406489950847b07811358eb244c22e1c146132273a` |\n| 919 | `w10::sv1-bio-heat_inactivated_fbs_spiroplasma_m2578` | `468766d941e35a74eb2de3c95096660987277d9b` | `e095ee1af7fe44564fb8ad4bda0620a68beab2201749e5d95377fc90a32d7273` |\n| 921 | `w10::sv1-bio-columbia_blood_agar_oxoid_m2224` | `468766d941e35a74eb2de3c95096660987277d9b` | `814a2a4b8c196b50f0d14b6280f79c6ee3cdcb9372a15469fb8fa58d96fdf639` |\n| 922 | `w10::sv1-bio-fetal_bovine_serum_56c_60min_m2964` | `468766d941e35a74eb2de3c95096660987277d9b` | `3d85af4e8d854ce3f5c6a5a5588de2d1a788d00aa97a732a5711c0670f61a58b` |\n| 940 | `w10::sv1-buffers-phosphate_1m_ph7_2_pbbm_togo_m2760` | `468766d941e35a74eb2de3c95096660987277d9b` | `092dc9e4f5e24d02ade620ba3346988b7d4466501a779a2a11867512b99f7a61` |\n| 943 | `w10::sv1-buffers-hcl_nominal35percent_togo_m3216` | `468766d941e35a74eb2de3c95096660987277d9b` | `e7feec90fa5d94bc8a3003be08b94de71b9119a528787eb68784b3c5413ab55c` |\n| 944 | `w10::sv1-buffers-hcl_nominal32percent_jcm770` | `468766d941e35a74eb2de3c95096660987277d9b` | `4ca8faefe79f29c649c1e1b82468314bd454abd0abd41d732fe09b7b54ce17eb` |\n| 946 | `w10::sv1-buffers-hcl_nominal25percent_jcm431_899` | `468766d941e35a74eb2de3c95096660987277d9b` | `74e1276709349d844cc7c883b3d3554f8e8fce876d8798ef930f3e955c40b02c` |\n| 970 | `w10::sv1-kg-calf-serum1303` | `468766d941e35a74eb2de3c95096660987277d9b` | `c5095e48aa60fc9dde875bc49e1d1628f9869ab53d606ca10c42622de9b77a78` |\n| 971 | `w10::sv1-kg-swine-serum1076b` | `468766d941e35a74eb2de3c95096660987277d9b` | `855514ece2c881918f44112a0f3c52cb855b0e05642cf5af267235a1936a10c7` |\n| 980 | `w10::sv1-kg-difco-columbia1270` | `468766d941e35a74eb2de3c95096660987277d9b` | `07b3aa3689dbee44e18c5c4d416ebee0ba5920a5eb044cd7901cda9d7f8ab0e7` |\n","number":1237,"state":"open","title":"Reconcile source-bound preparation families before installing duplicate or unsupported owners","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1237"},{"body":"Worker05's source-bound review at frozen base `e000d66dd26ab053e9b17ce7bf8d3c873c3dde99` found `data/ingredients/mapped/Sea_Salts.yaml` still uses `NCIT:C75874` as its primary identity even though its own history describes only contextual evidence for a supplier-qualified commercial sea-salts preparation.\n\nThe captured [NCIT:C75874 term](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/api/ontologies/ncit/terms?iri=http%3A%2F%2Fpurl.obolibrary.org%2Fobo%2FNCIT_C75874) supplies a “Sea Salt” label but does not substantiate equivalence to the source product. [Sigma S9883](https://www.sigmaaldrich.com/US/en/product/sigma/s9883) illustrates why catalog-specific review matters: the supplier page contains both generic sea-salt prose and an artificial-seawater mixture description. It does not identify every generic Sea Salt occurrence or establish that this owner is catalog S9883. The review does not assert an unverified natural-versus-artificial NCIT definition.\n\nThe independently revised proposal uses a local registry identity and FALLBACK_REGISTRY mapping, withdraws the unsupported exact/close NCIT relationship, and preserves its historical provenance. It does not infer a catalog number, formula, component list, or merge with Artificial_Sea_Salt. All existing holds remain. The first draft's retained closeMatch was rejected during skeptical review and corrected before handoff.\n\nBefore-owner SHA256: `7d10257ca3847166f949d8e179aa068c6ebef128f932769b29ca064b76595c39`. Exact source cases, primary captures, proposal bytes and independent review are in `reports/all_unmatched_20261107/worker-05/materials/` and `chemicals/materials-independent-review.*` on `review/all-unmatched-w05-source-review-20261107`.\n\nFollow-up: reconcile shared-owner proposals and source-specific product boundaries, apply through guarded writers, withdraw inherited changed-payload support, and regenerate synchronized surfaces only during coordinator integration. Native release admission remains required (#1165). #811 records a missing generic Sea Salt index answer and #312 discussed Artificial_Sea_Salt; neither establishes this supplier owner's identity. Live issue/body/comment deduplication receipts are in the worker bundle. This review made no maintained-data or downstream-build changes.\n","number":1177,"state":"open","title":"Sea_Salts retains an unsupported NCIT primary identity for a supplier-scoped preparation","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1177"},{"body":"The unapplied W04 new-owner payload Oxoid_Wilkins_Chalgren_Agar_Plates.yaml asserts a supplier-prepared basal plate product across its material scope, supplied form and evidence. PMC3101863 reports growth on Oxoid Wilkins-Chalgren agar plates supplemented with blood and antibiotics. It does not establish that basal plates were purchased pre-poured or the order of plate preparation.\n\nExact reviewed scope:\n- Finding `MEDIA-SCI-01`; stable key `w04-independent-MEDIA-SCI-01`; immutable peer review `20261108T070033Z-w04-successor-science-v1`.\n- `I1201-MEDIA-P-oxoid_wilkins_chalgren_agar_plates`; owner `data/ingredients/mapped/Oxoid_Wilkins_Chalgren_Agar_Plates.yaml`; before `absent (new proposal; no maintained owner created)`; proposed SHA256 `0554c990d6eaacdc7dfef4977d87fbb138998dd93eeb0b6fd509d15b793adc11`.\n  [Exact published proposal](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/blob/548e7bdd0ce2a68b3ae0df6ad7a6150a4e5c9bb9/reports/all_unmatched_20261107/worker-04/successors/issue-1201-v1/proposals/media/Oxoid_Wilkins_Chalgren_Agar_Plates.yaml).\n\nCorrect only the unsupported procurement/preparation claim in every affected field, including preferred material scope, supplied form, notes, mapping evidence, curation history and recipe reference. Represent the source-supported agar/plate preparation with honest supplements and remaining limitations. Do not infer the opposite purchase form from a current powder catalogue comparator or treat blood/antibiotics as part of an unqualified basal supplier product. Preserve the old payload/evidence as a historical draft and publish a fully bound successor.\n\nEvidence:\n\n- [Primary/ontology source](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3101863/); capture SHA256 `0d6b310e7e41ca1501369512ae6b0958becc068381fbd72dccaa4166fb78c04d`; Methods: Bacterial Growth Conditions\n- [Primary/ontology source](https://www.thermofisher.com/order/catalog/product/CM0619B); capture SHA256 `4421686b2cbcab2344e98871108f7b71c26959c892fbfa7455212a106607e5a3`; ProductCM0619B current powder comparator, not historical procurement\n\nDeduplication: Fresh all-state Wilkins search found no exact tracker. #1201 is related source-completion context; this dedicated finding concerns an affirmative unsupported new-payload claim.\n\nReview context: W04 published head `548e7bdd0ce2a68b3ae0df6ad7a6150a4e5c9bb9`, frozen campaign base `e000d66dd26ab053e9b17ce7bf8d3c873c3dde99`. Main as observed at `0dac278466699129a083343a921c2e0ca18a9ab4` has the same eighteen affected inherited-owner blobs as the frozen baseline; this is not a report of already-applied campaign curation. The full independent assembly covers 188 source cases and 115 proposals and remains `needs_curation`. Original peer review and evidence are immutable. Agent-authored new mapping evidence must satisfy #1226 without relabeling historical human evidence. The fix must use existing native guarded proposal validation and independent re-review; it grants no maintained-owner or export approval and requires no new gate, threshold, or synonym-selection rule.\n\n<!-- mim-review-round:20261108T071043Z-w04-final-scientific-issue-plan -->\n<!-- mim-review-bundle:wilkins-preparation -->\n<!-- mim-fingerprints:w04-independent-MEDIA-SCI-01 -->\n","number":1234,"state":"open","title":"Remove unsupported supplier-prepoured scope from the Wilkins-Chalgren proposal","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1234"},{"body":"Three unpublished Worker 1 stock proposals turn a storage or autoclaving atmosphere into an asserted material component:\n\n- `CS-PROP-NBRC361_NEUTRALIZED_SULFIDE_STOCK`: Nitrogen is listed as a component based on autoclaving under N2 and sealed storage.\n- `CS-PROP-NBRC1321_SULFIDE_STOCK`: Nitrogen is listed as a component based on storage under N2 after filtration.\n- `KG26-PROP-186-IDENTITY` / `DSMZ863_Sodium_Carbonate_Stock`: Carbon dioxide is listed as a component based on autoclaving sealed vessels under CO2.\n\nThe captured [NBRC361](https://www.nite.go.jp/nbrc/catalogue/NBRCMediumDetailServlet?NO=361), [NBRC1321](https://www.nite.go.jp/nbrc/catalogue/NBRCMediumDetailServlet?NO=1321) and [DSMZ863](https://www.dsmz.de/microorganisms/medium/pdf/DSMZ_Medium863.pdf) instructions establish those preparation conditions. They do not specify the gas as a material ingredient or provide a dissolved-gas specification for these stocks. `MAPPING_SEMANTICS.md` section 6 defines `components` as material has-part, not every participant in a preparation procedure.\n\nRetain the atmosphere conditions and exact source provenance in notes/evidence. Remove unsupported gas-component assertions, or supply direct applicable evidence for material inclusion. Recheck component completeness against the source's actual material inputs. Do not invent gas amounts or final dissolved speciation.\n\nKeep the explicit CO2 saturation of the separate bicarbonate preparations distinct: there the source does describe introducing CO2 into the solution, without establishing a numerical concentration. This finding does not remove that sourced distinction or merge any owners.\n\nIndependent finding: `.temp/all-unmatched-20261107/preflight/w01-shard-coordinator-review-v2/finding-004-atmosphere-partonomy.json`, SHA256 `34b1123989cef7421dc05cf926f2001c18d0fac12a43463da83eabba8dc48d71`. It binds exact frozen payloads, source captures and case IDs. A coordinator check of the current proposals still found all three gas components. Current issue deduplication and the worker's artifact-finding/issue-plan files showed no existing tracker for this specific defect.\n\nCorrect only proposal artifacts through the guarded writer, preserve earlier versions and obtain independent re-review. No maintained data, scientific hold or release disposition has changed for this finding.\n","number":1167,"state":"open","title":"Keep storage and autoclave atmospheres out of unsupported stock material components","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1167"},{"body":"The frozen Worker 1 shard proposes four new owners for two equivalent preparations. This concerns unpublished proposals; no maintained owners have been merged or changed.\n\n| Preparation | Proposals | Primary evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| CO2-saturated bicarbonate | `NSC-PROP-NBRC_1257_Bicarbonate_Solution` and `CS-PROP-NBRC1321_CO2_BICARBONATE_STOCK` | Both sources specify 8.4 g NaHCO3 plus 100 mL distilled water, CO2 saturation, sealed CO2 bottles and autoclaving. |\n| Sulfide from nonahydrate | `NSC-PROP-NBRC_1257_Sulfide_Solution` and `CS-PROP-NBRC1321_SULFIDE_STOCK` | NBRC1257 uses 12 g Na2S·9H2O plus 50 mL water; NBRC1321 uses 24 g plus 100 mL. Both are nominally 1 M, filter sterilized and stored under N2. The batch scales differ by two; the evidence does not establish distinct preparations. |\n\nPrimary pages: [NBRC1257](https://www.nite.go.jp/nbrc/catalogue/NBRCMediumDetailServlet?NO=1257) and [NBRC1321](https://www.nite.go.jp/nbrc/catalogue/NBRCMediumDetailServlet?NO=1321). Exact captures and payloads are bound in the finding below.\n\nReconcile each pair to one proposed owner while preserving both source cases, every occurrence, raw labels, original batch scales and receiving-medium amounts. Source numbers alone do not define distinct substances. This does not establish equivalence with generic 84 g/L bicarbonate, neutralized sulfide or 5% sulfide. Preserve added-water amounts without asserting measured final volume; keep generic source-local labels non-resolving. The author/coordinator must select the retained proposal; this issue selects no identifier and authorizes no maintained-owner merge.\n\nThe same-source phenanthrene and Metal44 pairs were already reported by the worker and remain separate reconciliation items. Current open-issue deduplication found no tracker for these two cross-source pairs; #1163 concerns the earlier stock-completeness gap.\n\nEvidence under `.temp/all-unmatched-20261107/preflight/w01-shard-coordinator-review-v2/`: `finding-003-duplicate-preparations.json`, SHA256 `8395d68231535cb509d7fef4b1d1ad3405498c50fc71d9e9ce259c5219749261`; peer `duplicate-addendum-v1/comparison.json`, SHA256 `1efe59deafe86709301536500ac9a885ab9b2d793144591c1b3ab8c92a573a26`. Frozen proposal manifest SHA256 `eb456ecef3f68b587a2f0281336f88b33679eb8d65a38f842661457ec05866f0`.\n\nRequire native validation and independent re-review of the reconciled final payloads. No role, component, mapping-release or whole-record approval follows from this issue.\n","number":1166,"state":"open","title":"Reconcile duplicate NBRC1257 and NBRC1321 stock proposals before integration","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1166"},{"body":"The existing `Clarified_rumen_fluid` and `Sludge_fluid` owners carry exact MicrO identities with narrower origin claims than the source contexts establish. Worker05's independent correction review confirmed this while reassessing its assigned source cases; source ownership and all release holds remain unchanged.\n\n| Existing owner | Confirmed scope problem |\n| --- | --- |\n| `data/ingredients/mapped/Clarified_rumen_fluid.yaml`, `MICRO:0000520` | The ontology definition requires cow rumen. Official MediaDive DSMZ161/212 explicitly allow **cow or sheep**, while JCM266/355/1201 describe the preparation without specifying animal species. |\n| `data/ingredients/mapped/Sludge_fluid.yaml`, `MICRO:0000521` | The ontology definition requires municipal sludge. DSMZ119/JCM872 specify anaerobic-digester sludge without that origin; another actual owner context, TOGO M2596, supplies a wastewater origin and a distinct processing sequence. A generic label does not establish municipal origin or make the different preparations equivalent. |\n\nPrimary evidence: [MicrO clarified rumen fluid](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/api/ontologies/micro/terms?obo_id=MICRO:0000520), [MicrO sludge fluid](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/api/ontologies/micro/terms?obo_id=MICRO:0000521), [DSMZ161 and its clarified-fluid definition](https://mediadive.dsmz.de/rest/medium/161), [DSMZ212](https://mediadive.dsmz.de/rest/medium/212), [JCM266](https://www.jcm.riken.jp/cgi-bin/jcm/jcm_grmd?GRMD=266), [DSMZ119](https://www.dsmz.de/microorganisms/medium/pdf/DSMZ_Medium119.pdf), [JCM872](https://www.jcm.riken.jp/cgi-bin/jcm/jcm_grmd?GRMD=872), [TOGO M2596 original context](https://togomedium.org/sparqlist/api/gmdb_medium_by_gmid?gm_id=M2596).\n\nThe isolated worker05 proposals reuse the existing owners under local registry identities, classify them as undefined biological mixtures and preserve distinct supplied preparation descriptions. They do not create duplicate generic owners, equate raw sludge input with the yeast-incubated supernatant, equate raw rumen contents with clarified fluid, or apply one source recipe to every occurrence. No new ambiguous resolving alias is added. Original owner hashes, original ontology evidence, complete occurrence comparisons and first rejected candidate versions remain preserved.\n\nExact evidence and proposed payloads are in `reports/all_unmatched_20261107/worker-05/correction-1193-v1/root/`, with author-separated review in `materials/root-independent-review.json` (SHA256 `4d70637b408acd320aac22441f131b72d8a77911017f1dd515bb2fa787bd7acd`). Findings F03 and its corrected payloads are retained separately. The source case IDs remain bound to the unchanged worker05 shard.\n\nCoordinator follow-up: reconcile overlapping proposals, review the complete integrated owner payload, withdraw unsupported inherited exact MicrO rows if applying the registry correction, and run native synchronization/publication gates. Existing source-specific, owner and export holds remain; the correction review does not grant release approval.\n\nDeduplication: live all-state searches for clarified rumen/sludge found historical #204 (term existence/label verification) and #513 (missing ingredient classification). Neither covers this narrower-origin mismatch. #1193 tracks the worker05 completeness correction; this issue tracks the distinct maintained-owner identity concern. No historical issue is closed or superseded here.\n","number":1204,"state":"open","title":"Reconcile narrower cow and municipal-origin identities on generic fluid owners","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1204"},{"body":"Final coordinator review of two published source-review shards found that several no-change outcomes defer already-authorized curation rather than identify a missing scientific fact. Worker04 head `0d2c73e5aee5adb88782537755472d9ca0c5c3cd`; worker08 head `7efd3f8b98f9e822431dcb510c2c242a2569cfc7`; both depend on #1158 at `e000d66`.\n\nConcrete examples:\n\n- Worker08 case `CM_NAME:b9a945a3356d0c885eea986a2f6223e78c8e7d7163ee95629061a640294cbbe8` (H2O deionized) correctly distinguishes deionization from distillation, but its remaining requirement is to perform native owner lookup and use or create the appropriately scoped owner. [DSMZ533](https://www.dsmz.de/microorganisms/medium/pdf/DSMZ_Medium533.pdf) explicitly identifies deionized water in both main and trace stock. No proposal or completed lookup disposition accompanies that deferred step.\n- Worker08 case `CM_NAME:e69128522cfe555f63dd51d4a23ece86363290b3839180386b090f368b28d1cc` (glycine-NaOH buffer) preserves a source-defined concentration and pH, and correctly finds a receiving-medium volume transcription error. [JCM906](https://www.jcm.riken.jp/cgi-bin/jcm/jcm_grmd?GRMD=906) specifies the separately prepared buffer. The decision defers representing the stock without explaining which missing fact prevents a bounded preparation identity. It must not be equated to pure glycine or assigned invented titrant amounts.\n- Worker04 case `CM_NAME:2f91fab8feab468a33e48d01e2f0a1363b9ad70816766c69a1e8d68c0ed6f8d5` describes the [NBRC15](https://www.nite.go.jp/nbrc/catalogue/NBRCMediumDetailServlet?NO=15) treated, clarified V-8 preparation but defers representing it until later curation. Lack of a molecular mapping or extraction yield does not by itself establish that a source-qualified preparation cannot be represented with an honest partial assertion.\n\nRelated outcomes defer normality-qualified NaOH stocks, source-specific top layers, C9322 supplied-form review, and malted wheat meal because an exact molecular/ontology mapping or later complete-owner review is absent. These are leads for reassessment, not predetermined identity approvals. Genuine solvent, formulation, provenance or supplied-form uncertainties may remain after primary research.\n\nReassess the full no-change cohorts against MAPPING_SEMANTICS sections3/6 and add-media. Complete supported proposal work, or state the precise scientific fact still missing and the actual primary-source searches. Keep receiving-medium dose/unit repair separate from ingredient identity. Never invent concentrations, solvent, chemistry, product equivalence, component completeness, or exact ontology matches; preserve all native holds and old review bytes. Require separate skeptical review of successor evidence/payloads and ordinary commits/pushes on the existing worker branches. No maintained data or export approval is requested from workers.\n\nThis extends the same class of completeness problem seen in #1163 and #1193 to distinct final shards; those earlier findings remain preserved. Exact frozen decisions and capture hashes are retained in the coordinator's immutable `w04-w08-deferred-curation-v1/review-inputs.json` and will accompany the campaign evidence. This is a review-completeness finding, not a statement that every listed source must map.\n","number":1201,"state":"open","title":"Complete deferred source-qualified curation in final unmatched review shards","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1201"},{"body":"Worker05's published source-review head `b44430c9230e62f9d9d73154484311b77442e73e` declares the shard complete, but at least one no-change disposition defers curation that the campaign already authorizes rather than documenting an unavailable scientific prerequisite.\n\nAffected case: `KG_CASE:b58f2778ba8c8905faf5cddf634ff4d5059978baa1b2489af2eb4db7f7e9fef1`, MediaDive ingredient1382, Grace's Insect Medium. Existing source owner: `data/ingredients/unmapped/Graces_Insect_Medium.yaml`, SHA256 `98d818debe40f93d28b7c08b2a7437b6a3c8566e12c857aa7f42fd3c1cdafa3e`.\n\nThe [committed decision](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/blob/b44430c9230e62f9d9d73154484311b77442e73e/reports/all_unmatched_20261107/worker-05/media/decisions.json) explicitly binds the source to Sigma G8142 and cites the manufacturer's complete formula. Its no-change and missing-requirement fields say to curate that formula and review component identities. There is no proposal, and no distinct missing evidence for the whole supplied product is identified.\n\nThe [manufacturer formula](https://www.sigmaaldrich.cn/deepweb/assets/sigmaaldrich/product/documents/172/423/g8142for.pdf) distinguishes G8142 liquid1x from G9771 powder, including bicarbonate treatment. Worker evidence `MEDIA-SIGMA-G8142-FORMULA` retains the PDF at SHA256 `7a07a208abd686522d82c3d61bc682af4e377ebd226bdbe786c2f9e84727b140`. This is a lead for supplied-product curation, not approval of any generic medium equivalence or every component identity.\n\nMAPPING_SEMANTICS sections3 and6 and the add-media workflow distinguish whole preparation identity from component grounding. Named ungrounded parts may remain explicit; a missing component CURIE is not by itself proof that an evidence-defined preparation must stay entirely uncurated. The campaign requires justified curation, not only a list of future curation tasks.\n\nPlease reassess this case and the other worker05 no-change dispositions whose stated blocker is simply to curate, define or reconcile an already identified formulation. For each, either prepare a concrete evidence-backed proposal through the existing proposal workflow, or record the exact unresolved scientific fact and the primary-source searches actually attempted. Preserve historical/source scope, competing-owner boundaries, raw labels, prior holds, and separate full-payload/release review. Do not invent a catalog variant, infer a complete formula from a source name, merge owners, treat all product names as resolved, or approve roles.\n\nFor G8142 specifically, verify the source-to-product attribution and all current owner occurrences before choosing whether the existing owner can be specialized or a separate source-qualified preparation is needed. Transcribe only supported formulation claims; preserve any uncertainty in individual component form or quantity explicitly. Independent re-review must cover the revised disposition/proposal and final head.\n\nDeduplication: the complete652-visible-issue all-state snapshot and relevant Grace/G8142/worker05 title/body matches contained no exact completeness finding. #1176 and #1177 cover separate peptone/Sea Salts defects. This issue does not reopen those or supersede a scientific hold.\n","number":1193,"state":"open","title":"Complete source-defined worker05 formulation curation before final shard acceptance","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1193"},{"body":"The worker07 campaign proposal at `be2f4c36d3d4ed79aabe29401c555ae3fd65d7b0` closes all 17 occurrences of `Mg(SO4) x 7 H2O` as `existing_identity_or_alias` and adds a resolving RAW_TEXT alias to the pure magnesium sulfate heptahydrate owner. CHEBI:31795 is correct for the solute. The problem is supplied-stock applicability.\n\nAt least three frozen CultureMech root occurrences (CultureMech:014409, :014591 and :014959; MediaDive solutions5518,5736,6270) are volumetric stock additions. The [DSMZ1592 recipe](https://www.dsmz.de/microorganisms/medium/pdf/DSMZ_Medium1592.pdf), [DSMZ1593 recipe](https://www.dsmz.de/microorganisms/medium/pdf/DSMZ_Medium1593.pdf) and [MediaDive6270 primary record](https://mediadive.dsmz.de/rest/solution/6270) specify10mL of7.5g/L magnesium sulfate heptahydrate stock. A global resolving synonym on the solute does not distinguish that preparation from its component. The frozen CultureMech percent-volume annotation is retained as source evidence, not accepted as a corrected dose.\n\nBefore integration, review all17 source occurrences and separate true solute cases from stock-input cases. Preserve valid hydrate chemistry. Where evidence defines the preparation, prepare a source-qualified stock proposal with exact concentration/source context; otherwise document the precise remaining evidence. Keep raw labels and occurrence provenance without global stock-to-solute equivalence. Re-review the corrected exact payloads independently and regenerate the worker native report. Published prior evidence stays immutable.\n\nExact finding: `.temp/all-unmatched-20261107/preflight/cross-shard-reconciliation-v1/finding-g12-mg-stock-scope-v1.json`, SHA256 `37771803c93ebe38644785034a54b9c50e8ddf5d610821053c75bfb4df0c612a`. Case `CM_NAME:9dd78dd62f6762ad602b4126b067042b0cc68ba65bd212f9a10b07ec20b33e8e`; proposal `B-PROPOSAL-Mgso4_X_7_H2o`, payload SHA256 `e898e16739bc1bb152cffc32fe6903ea3b64c668cdab626860009770acb97149`. The coordinator verified current head, proposal/ledger hashes and primary captures. Deduplication inspected662 current issues and found no exact existing stock-scope finding.\n\nThis is a source-case and alias-scope correction under MAPPING_SEMANTICS.md sections3 and6; it is not an authorization to merge owners, edit CultureMech, approve roles or transfer release support.\n","number":1203,"state":"open","title":"Separate MgSO4 heptahydrate stock occurrences from the resolving solute alias","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1203"},{"body":"Independent adversarial review of #1174 reproduced false producer provenance in the provisional source operator. No maintained ingredient or campaign data was applied.\n\nAffected source: `src/mediaingredientmech/curate/campaign_owner_apply.py`, reviewed SHA256 `74a448602d8d512a1b12037d88d0eba0a9ac9e1ea55c89e6bd3051afb23f0a99`, `_load` producer verification.\n\nAt preparation time the operator reads producer bytes from the package directory and compares them to the application root. Both reads inspect the current disk bytes. A long-lived process that imported version A can therefore accept a plan bound to later version B while still executing A.\n\nThe private deterministic probe imports the frozen implementation, appends a harmless identifying comment to that private source file, and builds the test plan from the changed file. `prepare` and dry-run application accept even though the known imported source hash differs from the accepted producer hash. The unchanged positive remains valid. The probe modifies only a private fixture and performs no owner application.\n\nBind the loaded implementation and relevant imported dependency bytes and file identities at import time. Compare accepted producer pins to that actual loaded snapshot at preparation and late boundaries; refuse stale imports or substitutions. Add regression coverage for the reproduced stale-import case and an unchanged positive. Preserve existing plan, receipt and scientific-approval restrictions; obtain independent re-review.\n\nFinding `IR-CAMPAIGN-OPERATORS-2`; finding SHA256 `4b4063a784418cfc8ff3f159c83f143396b15b3571b46be04b7263381aba8e47`; probe SHA256 `b0941a51cc8169d904bf52872b5221115f6ffb0cd151c7eca405e3e9281f90d6`; result SHA256 `30e6a03adcb0a4c7acd048d4b75a61b7ca5664b8954b65330dbc6290169508a2`. Evidence accompanies the implementation PR.\n\nDeduplication: the live all-state loaded/producer search returned no issue. #1174 is the parent capability task; #1194 concerns late catalog membership, a separate reproduced boundary. This is false provenance acceptance, not a claim of actual scientific-data corruption or a production exploit.\n","number":1195,"state":"open","title":"Bind campaign source producer pins to the actually loaded implementation","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1195"},{"body":"`data/ingredients/mapped/Indochrome.yaml` is classified `SINGLE_INGREDIENT` under `kgmicrobe.compound:indochrome`, with a placeholder mapping and no congener-specific source identity. The unsuffixed metatraits and metatraits_gtdb cases support a named pigment material, not a selected molecule. This finding is bound to maintained baseline `e000d66dd26ab053e9b17ce7bf8d3c873c3dde99`.\n\nA read-only GitHub `main` check at 2026-11-08T06:11:28.616136+00:00 found the relevant owner bytes identical to this frozen baseline. Exact decoded-body hashes and API captures are retained in `reports/all_unmatched_20261107/worker-10/successor-v1/issues/current-main-owner-check.json`.\n\nFinger and colleagues describe indochrome pigments as containing multiple isomers. Their particular Pseudarthrobacter preparation resolves four major blue fractions. That preparation is evidence against treating the unsuffixed material as a known single compound; it does not establish four constituents or their proportions for every metatraits occurrence. Primary reference: [Purification and characterization of indochrome type blue pigment](https://academic.oup.com/jimb/article/46/1/101/5996787).\n\nThe isolated proposal moves the named material to `kgmicrobe.ingredient:indochrome` with `UNDEFINED_MIXTURE` and a fallback registry identity, retaining the previous source identifier as provenance. It does not mint a precise molecule, assign a CAS, copy another pigment's structure, or fabricate components. Exact case/evidence/proposal bindings are in `reports/all_unmatched_20261107/worker-10/successor-v1/kg/{decisions,evidence,proposals}.json`: cases168 and170; proposal `sv1-kg-indochrome-mixture`.\n\nThis is a concrete curation item under [#718](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/718). Read-only all-state issue searches for indochrome found no dedicated existing item. [#1188](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1188) contains a related but distinct halomicin-mixture correction and does not cover this owner.\n\nBefore integration, reconcile current owner bytes and downstream references, preserve all source/native holds, withdraw inherited support for changed content, and validate namespace/registry rows plus maintained-surface synchronization. This proposal establishes a bounded named-material identity; congener composition, role claims and complete publication approval remain separate.\n","number":1213,"state":"open","title":"Represent unsuffixed indochrome as an undefined pigment material","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1213"},{"body":"The bare source name `geomycin` cannot be assigned to oxytetracycline from its MeSH trade-name synonym alone. The current `data/ingredients/mapped/Geomycin.yaml` uses `mesh:D010118` / Oxytetracycline, with an OLS exact-synonym lookup as the promotion evidence. Its existing native mapping disposition is already WITHHOLD. Preserve that hold.\n\nPrimary literature establishes a competing historical sense: [Brockmann and Musso, 1954, Geomycin II](https://doi.org/10.1002/cber.19540871128), abstract, describes a Streptomyces xanthophaeus antibiotic preparation with separable A/B/C/D components. This is positive evidence of name ambiguity, not a reason to choose one component or identify the source with that preparation. [MeSH D010118](https://id.nlm.nih.gov/mesh/D010118) supplies the oxytetracycline sense. Both must be considered.\n\nThe frozen campaign contains two distinct cases for `kgmicrobe.compound:geomycin`, from metatraits and gtdb-metatraits. Both retain only the bare name in their graph-node source record, with one incident METPO:2000202 edge each. Neither exposed source object gives a CAS, structure, catalogue product, strain-specific reference or paper that selects a sense. Their case IDs end `3b4ae00ee5` and `b9d93cf1`; exact objects are preserved in worker09's assigned-case and decision artifacts.\n\nVerified against remote MIM main `0dac278466699129a083343a921c2e0ca18a9ab4`: Geomycin.yaml SHA256 `fa269060e6ebc77f8c8bdf474dbae2432967d180e859387780789a8268d19bda`. The campaign baseline includes unmerged #1158; this issue proposes no change to its exports. Evidence lives under `reports/all_unmatched_20261107/worker-09/antibiotics/`, including primary-06.json and term-mesh-D010118.json captures; independent worker09 review follows before integration.\n\nFollow-up: recover the original phenotype/publication evidence for each source case, decide the actual material scope, then review the current owner's primary identifier, mapping grade and any source-registry representation under MAPPING_SEMANTICS section 3. Do not infer A/B/C identity from spelling, remove the hold, or approve roles from this mapping review. All-state issue bodies and relevant prior review decisions were searched for Geomycin/oxytetracycline; no dedicated issue was found in that bounded search.\n","number":1171,"state":"open","title":"Disambiguate Geomycin source cases before accepting the oxytetracycline identity","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1171"},{"body":"# Worker01: complete source-defined stocks need bounded proposals or explicit blockers\n\nThe provisional 188-case shard contains nine decisions (seven source-qualified preparations) with recovered primary formulations, `proposal_ids: []`, and `missing_requirement: null`. They share a generic future-integration deferral. Preserving stocks separately from dry solutes is correct; it does not explain why a source-qualified proposal cannot be prepared.\n\nThis finding is bounded to the frozen decisions below, not all stock cases or a final-head approval. No source, mapping, role, or release change is approved by this issue.\n\n| Case index | Source label | Verified source scope |\n|---|---|---|\n| 17 | CaCl2 solution (0.1 M)*** | [CS-P-NBRC-1339](https://www.nite.go.jp/nbrc/catalogue/NBRCMediumDetailServlet?NO=1339): 0.1 M CaCl2 aqueous stock; distilled water and autoclaving are explicit. |\n| 92 | CaCl2 solution | [CS-P-NBRC-1339](https://www.nite.go.jp/nbrc/catalogue/NBRCMediumDetailServlet?NO=1339): The short label is locally resolved to that 0.1 M stock in M9-Phenanthrene, without global synonym promotion. |\n| 101 | MgSO4 solution (1 M)*** | [CS-P-NBRC-1339](https://www.nite.go.jp/nbrc/catalogue/NBRCMediumDetailServlet?NO=1339): 1 M MgSO4 aqueous stock; distilled water and autoclaving are explicit; no dry hydrate identity is inferred. |\n| 66 | MgSO4 solution | [CS-P-NBRC-1339](https://www.nite.go.jp/nbrc/catalogue/NBRCMediumDetailServlet?NO=1339): The short label is locally resolved to that 1 M stock in M9-Phenanthrene, without global synonym promotion. |\n| 68 | Phenanthrene solution (250 mM) in dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO)**** | [CS-P-NBRC-1339](https://www.nite.go.jp/nbrc/catalogue/NBRCMediumDetailServlet?NO=1339): 250 mM phenanthrene in DMSO, filtered with DMSO-safe filters; solvent is explicitly supplied. |\n| 116 | Tween 80 solution** | [CS-P-NBRC-881](https://www.nite.go.jp/nbrc/catalogue/NBRCMediumDetailServlet?NO=881): Preparation lists Tween 80 50 g and distilled water 1 L; preserve added-water amount rather than assume measured final volume. |\n| 107 | Bicarbonate solution (1.0 M)*** | [CS-P-NBRC-1321](https://www.nite.go.jp/nbrc/catalogue/NBRCMediumDetailServlet?NO=1321): NaHCO3 8.4 g and distilled water 100 mL; CO2 saturation, sealed CO2 atmosphere and autoclaving are explicit. Existing 84 g/L overlap requires bounded ownership resolution. |\n| 114 | Sulfide solution (1.0 M)******* | [CS-P-NBRC-1321](https://www.nite.go.jp/nbrc/catalogue/NBRCMediumDetailServlet?NO=1321): Na2S·9H2O 24 g and distilled water 100 mL; filtration and N2 storage are explicit. |\n| 128 | FeCl3 solution | [CS-P-JCM-1036](https://www.jcm.riken.jp/cgi-bin/jcm/jcm_grmd?GRMD=1036): FeCl3·6H2O 0.15 g and distilled water 300 mL, freshly prepared; source-qualified nested Solution B occurrences only. |\n\nRequired resolution: prepare source-qualified native proposals or document a concrete remaining scientific/ownership blocker for each case. Bind the long/short CaCl2 and MgSO4 labels only to their exact source occurrences. Resolve existing-owner/cross-worker overlap before integration. Preserve added-water amounts rather than assume final volume; do not guess hydration, merge stocks by their solute, or approve roles. Require native validation and independent review before publication.\n\nFrozen decisions SHA-256: `5dbbf63b1736fbac74bc5f6457be48fb3613b3bd38a3ab4d4e37a279ce3ce704`. Frozen proposals SHA-256: `eb27d6de8b6a3403b131cb9ec1e21c65d300a2a977fa2acabdf6b463dcda1354`. Full case IDs, occurrence paths, source captures and original retrieval times are in `.temp/all-unmatched-20261107/preflight/w01-shard-coordinator-review-v1/finding-001-concentration-completeness.json`.\n\nPrimary capture SHA-256 bindings:\n- `CS-P-NBRC-1339`: `a731a4c601ae5def7009b9d6600c516810d08a122976fd28a62720ffab122837`.\n- `CS-P-NBRC-881`: `6bf300eade6e04eb3e0047615fbafeb7d8ce795758966df1d562c8462eba2565`.\n- `CS-P-NBRC-1321`: `471a2ea8c7daff318ea0b79a4b7508d5be43f5b823d728d957bdeef3c3ad6e4b`.\n- `CS-P-JCM-1036`: `b50c448cb435eda7dd35bb52c171e56db259c40f097332035990ec931d8ea22d`.\n\nThis is distinct from the previously corrected canary artifact findings in #1159. This reviewer has not performed live issue deduplication or posted an issue.\n\nCoordinator deduplication: current open issues were inspected before filing. #1159 concerns the accepted canary artifacts; #811 and #868 concern other label-index/identity problems. None covers these nine provisional full-shard completeness decisions. This finding belongs to the source-review evidence being prepared for #1160; dependency #1158 remains unchanged.\n","number":1163,"state":"open","title":"Complete worker01 source-defined stock proposals or document specific blockers","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1163"},{"body":"The campaign currently proposes competing owners for the Asahi EBIOS tablet family. Reconcile their source scopes before integration; the evidence below does not establish identical historical excipients or authorize a maintained-owner merge.\n\n- Worker01 `KG26-PROP-172-IDENTITY` promotes existing `data/ingredients/unmapped/EBIOS.yaml` from `UNMAPPED_0380` to `kgmicrobe.ingredient:ebios`. Its payload SHA256 is `ad84636fcfdfa606ce97d7655145ed968b3c0d36e2387b5ccad1850245ffe91b`.\n- Worker02 `a2-proposal-nbrc1132-asahi-ebios-tablets` proposes a new `kgmicrobe.ingredient:nbrc1132_asahi_ebios_tablets` owner. Its payload SHA256 is `3275012aad49e7d7e353507d106a3a26c8b2313fad80e5e587ade3bec857bc3c`. Its rationale treats the existing EBIOS owner as an unresolved EBIOS/P2G record.\n- The existing owner's before hash is `130f1d9a20d6e16b9a10939a7251242d290bc8b13eb2a140be697e7b86cf87a4`. Its creation history identifies MediaDive ingredient1953. That exact frozen source record explicitly says dried yeast tablets, 10g. P2G occurs in inherited research prose and does not replace this source continuity.\n\n[JCM969](https://www.jcm.riken.jp/cgi-bin/jcm/jcm_grmd?GRMD=969) identifies EBIOS dried yeast tablets, 10g, with an Asahi Food & Healthcare footnote. Worker06 case92/TOGOM1019 is this same source context. The frozen NBRC1132 context in worker02 identifies Asahi Breweries tablets distributed by Tanabe. These facts require a reviewed product-family/source boundary decision; different recipe identifiers alone do not establish distinct product identities, and different historical supplier wording does not prove equal formulations.\n\nThe worker06 bounded finding receipt is `reports/all_unmatched_20261107/worker-06/successor-v3/research-b/ebios-cross-shard-finding.json`, SHA256 `0fa69e99e546e64f0f7577efd9bef50114db34a7c705231c513479079311e9fd`. It preserves both exact proposal objects and payloads, all three frozen source cases, original owner history and JCM969 capture provenance. The original JCM969 capture SHA256 is `e0a5b7970d48f4ec47c071f9f21eb039465e95b58ca05efb3a4ed9a69508a1ca`, retrieved `2026-11-08T05:07:06.324562+00:00`.\n\nRequired outcome: check the established owner first; reconcile the uninstalled proposals with all author lineages and exact source links retained. Keep tablet form separate from P2G/yeast extract, preserve unknown historical excipients and doses, and avoid a global bare-brand alias. Worker06 can independently propose its exact JCM969/tablet scope against the existing owner, with this cross-shard dependency explicit. The coordinator must independently review the eventual combined payload and source applicability before application or release.\n\nThis was found during issue1217's authorized bounded EBIOS work and is filed before the worker06 EBIOS correction. An all-state read of 679 issue titles/bodies found EBIOS only in issue1217, which does not resolve this cross-worker owner conflict. No other worker was edited; no maintained change or merge has occurred.\n","number":1221,"state":"open","title":"Reconcile competing Asahi EBIOS tablet proposal owners before campaign integration","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1221"},{"body":"At the campaign's frozen base `e000d66dd26ab053e9b17ce7bf8d3c873c3dde99`, `data/ingredients/mapped/Fatty_Acid_Mixture_See_Medium_No_266.yaml` uses CHEBI:35366 as its identity and carries fatty-acid molecular-class aliases/structure. The source is a prepared stock, not that class.\n\n[JCM medium266](https://www.jcm.riken.jp/cgi-bin/jcm/jcm_grmd?GRMD=266), “Fatty acid mixture”, specifies valeric, isovaleric, alpha-methylbutyric and isobutyric acids, 0.5g each, 20ml distilled water, and concentrated NaOH to pH7.5. [CHEBI:35366](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/api/ontologies/chebi/terms?iri=http%3A%2F%2Fpurl.obolibrary.org%2Fobo%2FCHEBI_35366) is the fatty-acid molecular class. Its whole-stock exact identity is unsupported. No final solution volume or stereochemistry can be inferred from these recipe inputs.\n\nThe reviewed isolated proposal uses `kgmicrobe.ingredient:fatty_acid_mixture_see_medium_no_266`, removes inherited single-molecule properties, rejects the eight incompatible molecular-class aliases while preserving provenance, and transcribes six named inputs with UNMAPPED component references. Two generic historical mixture labels remain unapproved. No functional role is newly assigned. Existing WITHHOLD remains; this proposal is not publication approval.\n\nExact before-owner SHA256: `00aa192a2ce2f4acb1b752d3a478ca4e661c8e15e2f0039c9b22b608a10a7e6c`. JCM capture SHA256: `0054a5cdd60634511ec40a28bc6743000104c8152118dfc019e987afb8886321`. Evidence/proposed payload and separate skeptical review are under `reports/all_unmatched_20261107/worker-05/root/` and `reviews/` on branch `review/all-unmatched-w05-source-review-20261107`.\n\nFollow-up: reconcile cross-shard owner dependencies, integrate through guarded writers, review complete changed-owner claims, synchronize maintained surfaces and SSSOM, and retain release holds until native admission is supported (#1165). Do not merge with the different JCM1130 five-acid VFA stock. Live all-state searches for the exact owner, fatty acid and 35366 found no dedicated issue; #718 remains the broader excluded-claim backlog. No maintained data or downstream build was changed by this review.\n","number":1180,"state":"open","title":"JCM266 fatty-acid stock is incorrectly identified as the CHEBI fatty-acid molecular class","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1180"},{"body":"Worker04 found four source defects that need upstream reconciliation before source-specific matching can be released. This MIM tracking issue records the integration blockers; it does not authorize changing CultureMech from this worker.\n\nAll four current CultureMech `main` files were read through GitHub on 2026-11-08 at 05:13–05:21 UTC and matched the frozen case bytes. Captures, hashes and primary references are retained in `reports/all_unmatched_20261107/worker-04/captures/current-issue-owners/` on branch `review/all-unmatched-w04-source-review-20261107`.\n\n| Source record | Observed conflict | Required follow-up |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `data/normalized_yaml/algae/merds_my75s.yaml` | Ingredient `Thoroughly disperse the agar in cold`, with75%w/v, is a preparation-sentence fragment. The [CCAP MErds/MY75S sheet](https://www.ccap.ac.uk/wp-content/uploads/MR_MErds_MY75S.pdf) describes a biphasic medium with separate recipes and stock bases. The normalized two-ingredient record is not a faithful reconstruction. | Remove the phantom token while reconstructing the entire phase/stock structure and preparation instructions from the source; do not treat75% seawater as75% agar or just delete one row. |\n| `data/normalized_yaml/bacterial/isosphaera_medium.yaml` | `Agar, Noble (BD214050), if needed` combines Noble grade with a catalog number that [BD identifies as Bacto Agar](https://www.bdbiosciences.com/en-ca/products/reagents/dehydrated-culture-media/agars/agar-bottle-100g.214050?tab=product_details). [Noble Agar214220](https://www.bd.com/en-us/products-and-solutions/products/product-page.214220) is a different product. | Recover the original ATCC recipe/version or procurement evidence and decide which field was wrong. Retain the contradictory raw label until resolved. |\n| `data/normalized_yaml/bacterial/JCM_J1429_M1H_NAG_ASW.yaml` | Free N-acetyl-D-glucosamine is annotated CHEBI:8006 (peptidoglycan context), while [JCM1429](https://www.jcm.riken.jp/cgi-bin/jcm/jcm_grmd?GRMD=1429) lists the free ingredient, consistent with CHEBI:506227. | Correct the upstream monomer grounding with primary recipe and ontology evidence. The MIM spelling proposal must not conceal this upstream error. |\n| `data/normalized_yaml/bacterial/aam.yaml` | The record calls its source AAM-minus but includes D-glutamate1.71mM. [Lee2009](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2698402/) Table4 row13 gives L-glutamic acid250mg for AAM; the Methods explicitly omit L-glutamic acid from AAM-minus. | Reconstruct the intended AAM/AAM-minus variant and stereochemistry, rather than changing D to L alone. Do not extend this correction to the separate Klein1979 occurrences without their original methods. |\n\nRelated, but different, work: CultureMech#408 concerns TOGO volume-to-g/L normalization; the closed CultureMech#273 parsing correction concerned SBBM, not this CCAP MErds/MY75S record. MIM#587 concerns KG instructional labels; its comments retract blanket parenthetical filtering. These four findings need source-specific fixes, not generic label stripping.\n\nAcceptance: each corrected source cites its original evidence, preserves recipe variants and supplied forms, gets independent source review, and is then reconciled with frozen MIM case IDs and holds during the coordinator's integration. No maintained file or export has been changed by worker04. Campaign dependency #1158 and publication holds #1157 remain active.\n\nDeduplication searched the complete retrieved MIM and CultureMech issue/PR title/body inventories plus relevant prior comments. No exact open task for these four records was found within that bounded search; this is not a claim that every historical comment was searched.\n","number":1192,"state":"open","title":"Track four primary-source conflicts blocking worker04 source-specific matches","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1192"},{"body":"The kg-microbe #1123 name-disagreement review exposed an invalid Lysozyme CAS identifier. An arithmetic check of the current supported MIM candidate found three invalid CAS RNs:\n\n| Subject | Published target | Calculated check digit | Stored digit |\n| --- | --- | ---: | ---: |\n| MIM:Izalpinin | cas:480-14-4 | 8 | 4 |\n| MIM:Lysozyme | cas:2650-88-3 | 6 | 3 |\n| MIM:Methyl-2-Hydroxy-Phenylproprionate | cas:89471-28-0 | 3 | 0 |\n\nChecked against main `5019d6871fe0b0f47eccd50f9d0c169c98c40484` and the proposed #752 mapping-only follow-up. [CAS documents its check-digit method](https://www.cas.org/es-es/training/documentation/chemical-substances/checkdig): multiply the digits before the final check digit from right to left by 1, 2, ... and take the sum modulo 10. For Lysozyme, `8*1+8*2+0*3+5*4+6*5+2*6 = 86`, giving 6 rather than 3.\n\nA valid checksum does not establish chemical identity. Do not repair these by substituting the calculated digit: a missing or substituted earlier digit is also possible. Lysozyme needs source/form review, not forced equivalence with FOODON:03413135 merely to clear a downstream disagreement.\n\nImmediate containment: add exact row/owner-bound WITHHOLD decisions for these three CAS assertions while preserving the complete source payloads and unrelated local-identifier assertions. Historical/published release files stay immutable.\n\nRemaining acceptance: inspect original source records, independently verify the intended compounds/materials and replacement identifiers, correct YAML/SSSOM via the existing mapping-change receipt chain, and explicitly re-review corrected rows. Add a supported-release CAS check-digit gate to prevent recurrence without confusing syntactic validity with identity approval. This issue remains open until source correction and the permanent gate are complete.\n\nRelated: #752 and https://github.com/Knowledge-Graph-Hub/kg-microbe/issues/1123.\n","number":753,"state":"open","title":"Correct three supported CAS assertions with invalid check digits","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/753"},{"body":"Independent #1174 review reproduced a minor preflight/receipt roundtrip defect in provisional `campaign_owner_apply.py` SHA256 `74a448602d8d512a1b12037d88d0eba0a9ac9e1ea55c89e6bd3051afb23f0a99`.\n\nTop-level plan and acceptance arguments are made absolute but their saved repository-relative spelling is not checked with the same canonical-path rule used by `verify_completed`. The CLI passes the spelling through.\n\nIn a private actual native fixture, `intake/../plan.json` and `intake/../acceptance.json` refer to existing regular files inside the same root. Preparation accepts them; application changes owners and returns complete. Its completed verifier then rejects its own saved acceptance reference as `Noncanonical relative path`. No symlink, outside-root target, real campaign data or author-file mutation is involved.\n\nReject noncanonical top-level root/plan/acceptance/receipt spellings before rendering, output reservation or source writes, consistently with the completed verifier. Preserve alias protections instead of resolving symlinks away. Add regression refusals proving source/receipt bytes remain unchanged and a canonical positive roundtrip.\n\nFinding `IR-CAMPAIGN-OPERATORS-4`, SHA256 `1eb8040969565287cb758048ef9e015f85b4eab9fd385d3b04fcdf2b37911d85`. Probe SHA256 `31e6f57c1272e3f259f91392778d8909c8f03b5435caba258231f9cae9008ea4`; result SHA256 `e5eadc31942aa1f5a9d47fc46ab9ffb7e809e29b79b6ef48f7d3960bbcec14e2`. Evidence will accompany the implementation PR.\n\nLive all-state campaign/path search was checked. #1161 is a separate accounting-observer path-role defect; #1194 covers late membership, #1195 loaded code, and #1197 chain continuity. This is an early argument-validation omission, not a scientific identity finding or a claim of silent publication.\n","number":1198,"state":"open","title":"Reject noncanonical campaign proof paths before source application","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1198"},{"body":"The supported MIM release preserves unsupported scientific claims for further curation. SSSOM finalization in #731 completes the disposition review for all **3,018 mapping rows**: **1,763 supported** and **1,255 withheld**. The complete source graph remains semantically unapproved.\n\nCurrent mapping snapshot: [mim-sssom-2026-09-21](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/releases/tag/mim-sssom-2026-09-21). Its separate `withheld_mappings.sssom.tsv` preserves every withheld source row; `mapping-dispositions.tsv` records all 3,018 decisions, reasons and evidence references. Missing support does not necessarily establish that a claim is false. Non-exact mapping relations, weak exact-match evidence, unresolved aliases and identity conflicts remain withheld.\n\nThe trait-synonym source cleanup in #703 is now complete: 46 activity phrases across 39 records are retained as rejected provenance. The original negative #724 decision remains archived; the corrected Aromatic Compound row has a separate evidence-bound review. Adversarial review #730 fixes publication and integrity defects by source correction, precise withholding and regression checks. Closing #730 does not declare the withheld source claims scientifically corrected.\n\nThe [earlier KGX snapshot](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/releases/tag/mim-supported-2026-09-21) remains historical: 4,587 nodes, 2,112 edges, 1,603 mappings and 2,411 excluded assertions. The new SSSOM supersedes that snapshot's mapping selection; a corresponding new KGX publication is still pending. Current graph review ledgers account for 2,251 excluded assertions and preserve 1,735 open major/blocking historical findings. These counts overlap and are not additive.\n\nPriority evidence gaps in the current source corpus:\n\n- The 1,255 withheld mapping rows in the new lossless SSSOM backlog.\n- 662 prediction-only roles and 11 roles without evidence.\n- 24 additional unverified role promotions: generic mineral labels had become trace-element, iron, phosphate, or sulfur requirements, and a generic growth-factor label had become a vitamin claim. Review #720 records the affected assertions.\n- 43 component assertions requiring the original source preparation, including the unresolved PYGS abbreviation and TYGVS formulation differences.\n- The flattened CMC/PY/horse-serum expression remains AMBIGUOUS after withdrawal of its false single-mixture identity.\n- Other record-level findings remain explicit in the full disposition ledger.\n\nResolve each claim from its exact source identity, organism and preparation; add content-bound scientific dispositions and regenerate the affected artifacts. Do not bulk approve from chemical ancestry, name patterns, citation presence or structural validation. New evidence may move claims into the supported release only after review.\n\nSee [standalone SSSOM reproduction instructions](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/blob/main/docs/SSSOM_RELEASE.md) and [mapping review evidence](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/tree/main/reports/sssom_completion_20260921). This issue remains open for scientific curation after #731.\n","number":718,"state":"open","title":"Curate the claims excluded from the reviewed MIM release","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/718"},{"body":"Umbrella for #321, #334, #344, #251, #256, #257, #258, #259, #225 and the formula-as-label residual of #317. Filed because these are being worked as nine separate issues and they are **one cohort** — fixes to any one of them move the others' counts.\n\n## Current state, measured on `main` 2026-08-24 via `just report-hydrate-grounding`\n\n199 records whose `preferred_term` carries hydrate notation:\n\n| n | verdict |\n|---:|---|\n| 99 | `OK_HYDRATE_TERM` |\n| **46** | `CAS_MISSING_ANCHOR_ROWS` |\n| **39** | `HYDRATE_ON_ANHYDROUS_TERM` — MAPPING_SEMANTICS §3 violations |\n| 13 | `OK_OWN_CAS_ID` |\n| 2 | `UNKNOWN_NO_FORMULA` |\n\nPlus **45** mapped records carrying a hydrate *synonym* their own term does not account for (16 on an anhydrous term, 29 naming a different hydration state) — that is #251 — and **12** unmapped records with hydrate notation.\n\nThe §3 violations are the ones that assert something false: `CuSO4 x 2 H2O` and `CuSO4 x 4 H2O` both → `CHEBI:23414 copper(II) sulfate [Cu.O4S]`, a formula with no water. `CoCl2 x 2 H2O` and `CoCl2 x 4 H2O` both → `CHEBI:35696`. `FeCl2 x 6 H2O` and `FeCl2 x 7 H2O` both → `CHEBI:30812`. Two distinct substances collapsing onto one anhydrous term is #321's \"10 terms absorb multiple distinct hydrates\".\n\n## Why the formula-as-label records belong here\n\n**173 mapped records have a formula as their `preferred_term`** (`AlCl3`, `Al2(SO4)3 x 18 H2O`, `AlK(SO4)2`, `BaCl2 x 2 H2O`). **95 of them also carry hydrate notation** — so more than half the formula cohort *is* the hydrate cohort.\n\nThat overlap is why #334 (\"formula-notation labels shadow correctly-mapped siblings\") and #317's formula residual are the same problem seen from two sides. It also explains why the synonym-based re-grade in #317 can never clear them: the \"name\" is a formula, so it matches no ChEBI string by construction, and no synonym rule will ever fire.\n\nThey are also the `capitalize()` scars from #147 — `Alcl3`, `Cacl2_X_2_H2o`, `Al2_So43_X_18_H2o` — which ties this to #443. Note **the stems cannot be fixed independently**: 88 of those CURIEs are persisted bare in kg-microbe node xrefs (see #443's audit), so a rename is cross-repo.\n\n## Why one unit\n\n- Fixing a §3 violation by moving a record to a hydrate-specific term changes whether it still counts as a duplicate under #334.\n- `water_multiplicity` bugs (#256 hyphen-separated, #257 no sanity ceiling) change which records the report classifies at all, so #321's and #344's counts are downstream of them.\n- #258/#259 are defects in the *report* the other issues are measured with — they should be fixed **first**, or every count here is being read off an instrument with known faults.\n- #251's synonym-hidden notation is invisible to all of the above, so any \"done\" claim that ignores it is premature.\n\n## Suggested order\n\n1. **#258, #259, #256, #257** — repair the instrument. Small, and everything else is measured with it.\n2. **Re-measure.** The counts above were taken with the faulty instrument and should be expected to move.\n3. **#321 / §3 violations** — the 39 that assert a hydrate is an anhydrous compound. Highest-value: these are wrong, not merely imprecise.\n4. **#334 / #225 duplicates** — after 3, because grounding changes alter what is a duplicate.\n5. **#251** — synonym-hidden notation, which needs its own sweep.\n6. **#344**, then the formula-label cleanup, which is gated on #443's cross-repo rename decision.\n\nExisting tooling, none of it currently chained: `report_hydrate_grounding.py`, `check_hydrate_water.py`, `fix_hydrate_collapse.py`, `fix_hydrate_terms_and_twins.py`, `merge_minted_hydrate_duplicates.py`, `regrade_hydrate_to_close_match.py`, `add_water_of_hydration.py`, `anchor_cas_hydrate_records.py`, `fix_scrambled_hydrate_cas.py`.","number":459,"state":"open","title":"Work the hydrate cohort as one unit: 199 hydrate records + 173 formula-as-label records overlap by 95","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/459"},{"body":"Independent full-shard review of worker06 at `d5a7613c931d45d057eaca7b5ec15280aca76c9e` found source-scope, component-provenance and completeness defects that must be resolved before campaign integration. The review covers all188 cases and100 selected payloads with three separate scientific reviewers and an independent final assembly review. It grants no owner, role or export approval.\n\nThe current ordinary successor is `1b3641853c7105b9091f81b5a9b19c99e8ed351e`, verified live. It addresses the known duplicate stock under #1207 and reports a separate Merck component-label correction. Re-review those exact successor bytes before retiring their findings. The remaining findings below need an immutable successor preserving published captures, payloads and native reviews.\n\n| Finding | Defect | Required bounded outcome |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| W06-IR-002 | Contextual numbered pointers and generic labels resolve globally | Preserve each exact non-identifying label and provenance as REJECTED_LABEL; qualify preferred/ontology labels for JCM151/187/284 and R-P-MODIFIED-VITAMINS (JCM1158); retain explicit occurrence routing. Do not reject established named stocks or source-qualified names merely because they contain a number or see-below text. |\n| W06-IR-003 | Merck108339 glucose component fails native reference-label validation | Use active component_name D-glucose for CHEBI:17634, preserve supplier spelling D(+)Glucose in assertion evidence, and rerun partonomy/exact-byte review. |\n| W06-IR-004 | JCM977 stock Water is asserted as literal source input | Remove Water from literal transcription or explicitly represent and evidence the aqueous-solvent inference using method CURATED_INTERPRETATION and truthful curator/LLM attribution. Preserve unknown solvent detail, stock volume and glucose form; basal790mL water is not stock water. |\n| W06-IR-005 | Acknowledged Water inferences use literal-transcription assertion method | Distinguish source-transcribed solute/concentration from inferred solvent. Use CURATED_INTERPRETATION with precisely attributed inference evidence and LLM provenance if retained, or omit untranscribed Water. MANUAL_CURATION requires actual human-supplied evidence and is not prescribed automatically. |\n| W06-IR-006 | Positive JCM381-to379 evidence is misclassified as failed search | Bind A-E-CAPTURE-121 to the successful exact primary381 body with original timestamp/hash, retain381→379 context and index provenance, then refresh evidence bindings. D-glucose identity needs no correction. |\n| W06-IR-007 | Bounded authorized product/stock curation is deferred as missing science | Provide bounded source/manufacturer-qualified proposals retaining UNKNOWN excipients/lot/SKU/doses/forms and explicit source discrepancies. EBIOS must remain tablets; TypeB must remain distinct from TypeA/pure casein; C.difficile BHI branch is Oxoid and must not inherit current SR0096E doses; RM may transcribe narrative molybdate while documenting parsed-table omission, unknown acid strength and solvent/final-volume details. Do not clear source/owner/export holds. |\n| W06-IR-008 | Four failed receiving pages are classified as positive primary evidence | Relabel the four empty responses as unsuccessful_search, preserve original captures/access times, and enumerate receiving occurrences lacking an applicable recovered primary page. Preserve valid197 formulation/support and source-dose holds. Recover exact alternatives only if needed; do not use primary metadata as proof. |\n| W06-IR-009 | TSY broth reasoning misstates frozen units | Correct the reasoning to the actual1000 ML_PER_L frozen value; retain the genuine unknown whole-broth formulation and do not infer constituents from its name. |\n| W06-IR-010 | MediaDive1886 lacks a source bridge to JCM187 | Hold this KG source applicability unresolved; require an exact original collection/medium/recipe occurrence linking compound1886 toJCM187. Preserve independently supported CultureMech JCM187 contexts and their component evidence; remove or qualify unsupported KG backlink until then. |\n| W06-IR-011 | YNB J240 quantity is misstated | Correct only J240 occurrence quantity in reasoning/evidence summaries to1g; keep exactSKU/grade unresolved. |\n| W06-IR-012 | N-Z-Amine requirement overlooks known Sheffield typeA contexts | State known SheffieldtypeA subset explicitly and identify remaining uncertainty as catalogue/historical composition and unspecified other occurrences. Either perform authorized bounded source-qualified curation/review for that subset or mark that as deferred authorized curation; do not label already captured supplier/grade facts unavailable. Do not inferSigmaC7290 or universalN-Z-Amine equivalence. |\n\nPrimary examples: [JCM977](https://www.jcm.riken.jp/cgi-bin/jcm/jcm_grmd?GRMD=977) names20%w/v glucose without an explicit stock-solvent preparation; its790mL distilled water belongs to the basal medium. [JCM381](https://www.jcm.riken.jp/cgi-bin/jcm/jcm_grmd?GRMD=381) successfully links379; its original body must not be labelled a failed search. Failed receiving-page captures for JCM737/876/887/955 contain only “Nothing found”; the valid JCM197 formulation remains separate. [JCM969](https://www.jcm.riken.jp/cgi-bin/jcm/jcm_grmd?GRMD=969) and captured TOGO M3181/M2811/M2815 supply bounded product or stock facts while historical formulation details remain unknown.\n\nThe frozen TSY quantity is1000ML_PER_L, and the exact MediaDive J240 YNB occurrence is1g. The other inspected YNB recipes use6.7g. Captured compound1608 receiving contexts already identify Sheffield typeA; this does not establish SigmaC7290 or universal N-Z-Amine equivalence. A providerless “see Medium No.” pointer needs an actual collection/recipe bridge, not a matching number alone.\n\nPreserve raw labels as provenance; retire resolving entries only where claim-specific evidence supports their non-identifying role. If a solvent inference is retained, use truthful CURATED_INTERPRETATION and actual LLM attribution, not fabricated human curation or literal recipe transcription. Keep genuine salt/form/SKU/dose/titrant/final-volume uncertainties and all88 downstream holds. Complete bounded authorized curation where evidence already settles its scope; do not force unresolved identities or collapse preparation differences.\n\nEvidence: `.temp/all-unmatched-20261107/workers/w03/worktree/.temp/w06-published-head-independent-review-v1/`. Findings SHA256 `4626520a16f0b5e3729f6ffc630fba623e579742b3361cd68667de9c2598dd27`; assessment `ea688186e9507893bf98720bcaeaf3f86112600c18a390b411499db9c06c0a9c`; complete manifest `ee57931deef8638f9cb13a4f8bbc657bf5a1ef938eeb7e0a173775a2e8135a66`. The coordinator verified all5038 manifest files and read the exact grouped findings, reproductions and bounded fixes. The source evidence/case IDs/affected payload digests are enumerated in that ledger and must accompany the published successor.\n\nLive all-state deduplication inspected675 issues and a fresh exact W06-IR search. #1207 remains the duplicate-stock tracker; #1201 is related completeness work, not a replacement for these named findings. Require per-finding dispositions, guarded isolated payload checks, complete case/occurrence accounting, native schema/partonomy and fresh independent final-head review. No maintained application, gate weakening, merge or downstream rebuild is authorized by this issue.\n","number":1217,"state":"open","title":"Resolve worker06 independent source-scope, component-provenance and completeness findings","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1217"},{"body":"The worker-10 successor review found distinct plant-derived material scopes collapsed into generic owners. The inspected maintained baseline is `e000d66dd26ab053e9b17ce7bf8d3c873c3dde99`; source-specific proposals are available for Phytone and corn starch, with complete-owner publication still withheld.\n\nA read-only GitHub `main` check at 2026-11-08T06:11:28.616136+00:00 found the relevant owner bytes identical to this frozen baseline. Exact decoded-body hashes and API captures are retained in `reports/all_unmatched_20261107/worker-10/successor-v1/issues/current-main-owner-check.json`.\n\n`data/ingredients/mapped/Phytone.yaml` shares the primary identifier FOODON:03315720 (hydrolyzed vegetable protein) with soy-peptone records. The source case explicitly names BBL Phytone. MicrO0001795 names Phytone peptone, and manufacturer guidance describes the papaic soybean digest while distinguishing Phytone UF and other soy products. Use the specific Phytone material identity rather than a shared generic parent, and separately reconcile Phytone aliases carried by `data/ingredients/mapped/Soy_Peptone.yaml`; do not silently merge different soy products. Primary references: [TOGO M2989](https://togomedium.org/medium/M2989), [MicrO0001795](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/ontologies/micro/classes/http%253A%252F%252Fpurl.obolibrary.org%252Fobo%252FMICRO_0001795), [manufacturer technical guide](https://documents.thermofisher.com/TFS-Assets/BPD/brochures/peptones-supplements-feeds-technical-reference-guide.pdf). MicrO's contradictory botanical parenthetical is not evidence for barley origin and must not be copied into the ingredient.\n\n`data/ingredients/mapped/Starch.yaml` carries an active exact `Corn Starch` alias on generic CHEBI:28017. TOGO M1812 explicitly identifies Sigma corn-origin starch, and FOODON:00005791 denotes starch powder derived from maize grain. A distinct corn-starch proposal is supported; owned-name/alias reconciliation must accompany integration so the generic owner does not continue claiming the specific name. This finding does not approve an automatic move of every rice, soluble or supplier-labelled starch alias. Primary references: [TOGO M1812](https://togomedium.org/medium/M1812), [FoodOn00005791](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/ontologies/foodon/classes/http%253A%252F%252Fpurl.obolibrary.org%252Fobo%252FFOODON_00005791).\n\nExact proposal/evidence references are `reports/all_unmatched_20261107/worker-10/successor-v1/bio/{decisions,evidence,proposals}.json`: cases69 and78; proposals `sv1-bio-phytone_specific_identity` and `sv1-bio-corn_starch`. The ledger binds before-owner and proposed-payload hashes.\n\nThis is a source-backed refinement of the broad withholding queue [#718](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/718). Closed [#504](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/504#issuecomment-5536067334) recorded the shared Phytone/soy identifier family and called it a benign duplicate set; the exact Phytone ontology/manufacturer evidence now supports a more specific material distinction. Closed [#114](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/114), [#170](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/170) and [#148](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/148) do not resolve these current source-specific identity/alias claims. [#1188](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1188) addresses the different ISP4 whole-medium versus salt-parent defect.\n\nRequired follow-up: reconcile exact current owner bytes and duplicate/alias ownership, preserve source-specific products and existing rejected labels, apply only supported identity deltas through guarded writers, synchronize maintained surfaces and validate registry/SSSOM/lookup output. No product composition, species, process or whole-owner approval may be inferred from a generic hydrolysate or starch parent.\n","number":1212,"state":"open","title":"Resolve specific Phytone identity and corn-starch alias ownership","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1212"},{"body":"# W06 proposes separate NBRC1308 and NBRC1356 owners for the same fully enumerated trace-element preparation.\n\nBoth primary tables give NTA12.8g, FeCl3·6H2O1.35g, MnCl2·4H2O0.1g, CoCl2·6H2O0.024g, CaCl2·2H2O0.1g, ZnCl2 0.1g, CuCl2·2H2O0.025g, H3BO3 0.01g, Na2MoO4·2H2O0.024g, NaCl1g, NiCl2·6H2O0.12g, Na2SeO4 0.004g, Na2WO4 0.004g and1L distilled water. Both first dissolve NTA, adjust pH6.5 with NaOH, add minerals and state finalpH7.0. The unquantified NaOH uses different metadata encodings, not distinct chemistry. Retain unknown titrant quantity and case applicability; no winner or owner merge is chosen.\n\n- Worker06: `A-P-NBRC1308-TRACE`, `data/ingredients/mapped/nbrc_1308_trace_elements_solution.yaml`, `kgmicrobe.ingredient:nbrc_1308_trace_elements_solution`; payload SHA256 `6a8f4ebc4562e888207270f5fce1dfdc425c581494d9183bd6b8aed5e896a2aa`.\n- Worker06: `C-P-NBRC1356-TRACE`, `data/ingredients/mapped/nbrc_1356_trace_elements_stock.yaml`, `kgmicrobe.ingredient:nbrc_1356_trace_elements_stock`; payload SHA256 `5894ab52e5e649cee09bcd3d149d3723ed42cca6661848dcceaf30078bc170cb`.\n\nPrimary sources: [1308](https://www.nite.go.jp/nbrc/catalogue/NBRCMediumDetailServlet?NO=1308), [1356](https://www.nite.go.jp/nbrc/catalogue/NBRCMediumDetailServlet?NO=1356).\n\nReconcile the proposed stock identity before integration, preserving every source occurrence, raw-label restriction, original proposal and review lineage. This does not authorize merging maintained owners. Require one source-backed reconciled payload and re-review of all affected references.\n\n\nExact independent supplement: `.temp/all-unmatched-20261107/preflight/new-owner-semantic-duplicate-review-v1/confirmed-findings-supplement-v1.json`, SHA256 `e309179928a0130bf05fd34f95f501bb5339bc44e4fbfd6f05ac2d6ebc11345a`. The coordinator independently verified the primary capture hashes and read both complete formulation tables. Live issue deduplication inspected664 issues with no matching finding. Worker06 payloads have now been verified unchanged in published commit `d5a7613c931d45d057eaca7b5ec15280aca76c9e`; original pre-publication snapshot provenance is retained.\n","number":1207,"state":"open","title":"Reconcile duplicate NBRC1308 and NBRC1356 trace-element stock proposals","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1207"},{"body":"Fresh primary-source review finds an unsupported quantity in the historically approved BSA occurrence. The native companion verifier correctly refuses to publish the fresh WITHHOLD decision while the stored claim still says SUPPORTED.\n\nAffected claim: `MIM.occurrence:8ef56b6e356d256d79632b07d5dd457b18c7d9c99c5d75a540e11c9b34cfc67f`, owned by `data/ingredients/mapped/Bovine_Serum_Albumin.yaml`, source `CultureMech:012955` / MediaDive solution 3888 / JCM220. Current immutable claims source: `reports/ingredient_bundle_20260924/claims.json` (SHA256 `52c5c0e9938725524ed9680c736e1015b74dba1c603c2b76da62b7cc17175a8b`). The complete claim envelope SHA256 is `154a541e80b5e6dcd08c1583795dcb6ae821dc5e6605f42c17d6bf31a2792991`.\n\nThe claim stores `quantity={value: \"9.652509652509652\", unit: \"PERCENT_V_V\"}` and `review_status: SUPPORTED`. Its archived source payload repeats that normalized concentration. [The official JCM220 recipe](https://www.jcm.riken.jp/cgi-bin/jcm/jcm_grmd?GRMD=220) instead specifies 10 mL of 20% bovine serum albumin, fraction V, with 1 L water and separate filtration. [The original MediaDive3888 response](https://mediadive.dsmz.de/rest/solution/3888) captured during the fresh review independently retains amount 10, unit ml, attribute 20%, condition fraction V, `g_l: null`, and reported volume 1036 mL. This supports a source-qualified stock addition; it does not support the current percentage claim or establish a corrected final protein concentration. Do not guess the percentage basis, replacement quantity, product identity or lot.\n\nThe fresh W09 review covers all 20 owners and 51 claims: 49 SUPPORTED, two WITHHOLD. An independent read-only replay verified all 51 native claim proofs, all 20 scoped mapping proofs and the exact source captures, then reproduced the sole mismatch: `Companion claim status disagrees with reviewed disposition`. The existing original review still passes its historical native contract; that deterministic pass is not fresh scientific support for the quantity. The defect is the stored occurrence disposition, not BSA owner identity or a broken native gate.\n\nProposed bounded resolution, after this issue is filed:\n\n- Preserve the historical claims, companion reviews and source captures byte-for-byte. Create a new immutable full 51-claim successor through a guarded, reviewed publication operation; change only this occurrence's `payload.review_status` from SUPPORTED to WITHHOLD. Preserve its stable ID, owner, original quantity, preparation, source payload, evidence references and all other 50 claims. Append a truthful change/provenance receipt outside the historical claims.\n- Review the complete new claim envelope explicitly and bind fresh evidence and its decision to the actual new hash. Do not merely replace a hash in the old approval. The fresh full 20-owner/51-claim companion must bind the final complete canonical review and current owner bytes; the current private 20-row diagnostic base is not a release base.\n- Use existing native `validate_payload`, `validate_review_decision`, `reviewed_products`, the reserved exclusive-output `publish_new_files` primitive, and final `export_bundle`/bundle validation. Recheck all input bytes, role aliases and output reservations. The unchanged-cohort refresher is inapplicable because a scientific claim disposition changes; its guards must remain intact.\n- Preserve the invalid-CAS Lysozyme identifier WITHHOLD, Acriflavine's three chemical-property WITHHOLD findings, and Rifamycin SV's SMILES WITHHOLD finding. Do not drop an owner or claim, transfer historical mapping authority, or turn the source discrepancy into a new chemical identity decision.\n- Test the exact one-field change and preserved 20/51 cohort/IDs; original SUPPORTED payload plus fresh WITHHOLD decision must still fail. Changed owner/proof hashes, omitted or duplicate claims, invalid status, stale inputs and aliased/pre-existing outputs must fail. Verify that the new native bundle retains the withheld observation rather than omitting it.\n\nSource-transform correction in CultureMech, if pursued, is a separate upstream task. This repair neither changes its data nor asserts that a corrected final concentration has been established.\n\nRelated decisions: [#770](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/770) requires preservation of all seven BSA recipe memberships and their preparation context; [#1008](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1008) permits unchanged-cohort refresh only when all selected scientific claims remain identical; [#1057](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1057) preserves the fresh-review boundary; [#753](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/753) retains the unresolved Lysozyme raw-CAS concern.\n\nLocal exact evidence: `.temp/all-unmatched-20261107/preflight/fresh-companion-prerequisite-v1/verification-v1.json`, current-claim copy, live issue/comment captures, official-source capture references and immutable W09 review manifest. No claim, source, owner, gate or published review has been changed. The coordinator checked 679 current open and closed issues, the linked issue comments and exact occurrence/finding searches before filing this issue.\n","number":1222,"state":"open","title":"Withhold unsupported JCM220 BSA occurrence quantity through a fresh complete companion","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1222"},{"body":"# Bounded MediaDive source/form holds\n\nFive targeted cases remain on hold; no further alias additions or mapping approvals are proposed. The sodium acetate1315 pilot is documented separately.\n\n| Source | Exact raw name | Decision | Evidence and next step |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| mediadive.ingredient:331 | Tris(hydroxymethyl)methylamine | UPSTREAM_LABEL_NORMALIZATION_AND_EXISTING_OWNER_REVIEW | The admitted raw name Tris(hydroxymethyl)methylamine already occurs as an accepted EXACT_SYNONYM on Tris_Base; the transformed label removes parentheses. MediaDive identifies CHEBI9754/CAS77-86-1. That supports retaining the exact raw source spelling, not adding the damaged transformed form or approving this owner's whole row. The owner also carries Tris hydrochloride, Tris HCl, Tris buffer1M and nitrilotriacetic acid trisodium salt as accepted aliases: their salt/preparation/substance scopes require separate review before any full-payload approval. Preserve source-to-transformed provenance and the current native release hold. Review the existing Tris alias payload as a separate bounded curation; do not promote an entire row from this one raw-label agreement. [Source](https://mediadive.dsmz.de/download/ingredient/331/json). |\n| mediadive.ingredient:1512 | Calcium D-(+)-pantothenate | UPSTREAM_LABEL_NORMALIZATION_AND_STEREOCHEMISTRY_HOLD | The raw Calcium D-(+)-pantothenate spelling already exists as accepted EXACT on Calcium_Pantothenate; the transformed spelling drops parentheses. MediaDive gives CAS137-08-6. The current owner mixes D and DL aliases and also carries unrelated-looking vanillylamide aliases recovered from SSSOM. This bounded source spelling comparison does not settle all those alias identities or permit a complete-row release. Keep the raw spelling and source CAS witness. Audit D versus DL and unrelated alias provenance before considering this owner's row; do not turn punctuation normalization into identity approval. [Source](https://mediadive.dsmz.de/download/ingredient/1512/json). |\n| mediadive.ingredient:1930 | Nitrilotriacetic acid trisodium salt monohydrate | DISTINCT_HYDRATE_REQUIRES_SEPARATE_IDENTITY_REVIEW | MediaDive explicitly names nitrilotriacetic acid trisodium salt monohydrate and CAS18662-53-8. The nearby maintained trisodium nitrilotriacetate owner is CHEBI132766/CAS5064-31-3 with anhydrous structure. Section3 forbids treating the supplied hydrate as an exact alias of that different form; source hydration must remain explicit. Keep unresolved in this batch. A future exact-form review must resolve a hydrate-specific ontology identity locally or establish the documented CAS/local identity plus asymmetric parent. Do not guess hydration or import the source formula uncritically. [Source](https://mediadive.dsmz.de/download/ingredient/1930/json). |\n| mediadive.ingredient:2160 | Na2SiO3 x 5 H2O | REJECTED_HYDRATE_ALIAS_AND_STOCK_CONTEXT_HOLD | MediaDive2160 names Na2SiO3 x5H2O and CAS10213-79-3. Its admitted raw occurrence says21.2g/l stock solution,1ml. The exact pentahydrate label is REJECTED_LABEL on Na-silicate; neither that generic/anhydrous scope nor the separate nonahydrate is the exact supplied hydrate. The source solution attribute also must survive. Preserve the rejected label and preparation context. Review the pentahydrate substance and the named stock separately if later curation is authorized; do not resurrect this alias on a different hydrate or generic owner. [Source](https://mediadive.dsmz.de/download/ingredient/2160/json). |\n| mediadive.ingredient:776 | Maltose x H2O | CONTRADICTORY_SOURCE_HYDRATION_HOLD | The source label Maltose xH2O names a hydrate, but live MediaDive metadata gives anhydrous CAS69-79-4, CHEBI17306 and formulaC12H22O11. The exact label is already REJECTED_LABEL on Maltose. The general Maltose Hydrate local owner does not establish water number/anomer; the separate D-Maltose Monohydrate NCITC80300/CAS6363-53-7 owner cannot be selected from this conflicting source label alone. Retain the unresolved source label and its contradictory metadata; obtain original recipe/product evidence to decide hydrate and stereochemistry. No alias addition to anhydrous maltose and no automatic choice of the known monohydrate owner. [Source](https://mediadive.dsmz.de/download/ingredient/776/json). |\n\nAll current owner bytes, source captures/hashes, raw records and exact existing native dispositions are retained in `targeted-mediadive-holds-v1.json`. These are bounded triage conclusions, not whole-record or release approvals.\n\nHistorical #251 and broader open #811/#868 remain distinct from these exact new source observations. No issue is closed or claimed solved by this report.\n\nFollow-up from #1149. Preserve all current WITHHOLD and REJECTED_LABEL decisions. This is a source-bound follow-up to the broader #718/#811/#868 queues, not authorization to map all five rows. Options are: verify exact supplied form/product from original recipe evidence and curate the correct separate owner; or preserve source-scoped unresolved status. Recommendation: keep the five holds until those specific identity checks are complete. No hydration, stereochemistry, or mixture inference from normalized names.\n","number":1157,"state":"open","title":"Resolve five source-bound MediaDive form and alias holds from the fresh chemical queue","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1157"},{"body":"The Edison literature sweep surfaced two records whose reports identify a\nconfident ontology term while explicitly refusing to assert an identity mapping:\n\n| record | term | report's words |\n|---|---|---|\n| `Calf brains` | `UBERON:0000955` *brain* | \"high-confidence anatomical-source annotation\" but \"do **not** assert `skos:exactMatch` or `skos:closeMatch`\" |\n| `Cow manure` | `ENVO:00003031` *animal manure* | recommends retaining UNMAPPED |\n\nBoth terms resolve correctly against the local builds, and both prefixes are\nalready in `promote_resolved_unmapped.py`'s `ONTOLOGY_DB`.\n\nThe reports forbid `exactMatch` and `closeMatch` — the two predicates that\nlicense node substitution. They do not mention `skos:narrowMatch`, which is\nexactly the predicate that says the subject is *narrower than* the object\nwithout claiming they are interchangeable, and which MAPPING_SEMANTICS.md\nSection 3 already uses for hydrates (`NaH2PO4*H2O` narrowMatch the anhydrous\n`CHEBI:37585`).\n\nSo the question is a policy one, not a lookup one: **is Section 3's\nidentifier + narrowMatch + Rule B1 registry row pattern meant only for\nhydrate/salt variants of a parent compound, or does it also cover\nsource-qualified materials** — calf brains as a narrower thing than brain, cow\nmanure than animal manure, larchwood xylan than xylan?\n\nArguments each way:\n\n- **For extending it.** These records would gain a real, correct, non-substituting\n  edge into UBERON/ENVO, which is more than the current UNMAPPED conveys. The\n  narrowMatch semantics are literally true.\n- **Against.** \"Calf brains\" as a medium ingredient is a *preparation*, and\n  narrowMatch on a preparation-to-anatomy axis is a different relation from\n  narrowMatch on a hydrate-to-anhydrous axis. Conflating them makes the\n  predicate mean two things, and kg-microbe consumers cannot tell which.\n  UBERON:0000955 as an *anatomical source* — which is what the report actually\n  recommends — is arguably a schema slot, not an SSSOM row.\n\nNothing was applied; the records stay UNMAPPED and the CURIEs are recorded in\ntheir curation history. Raising this because the same question governs a\nrecurring class of records (extracts, tissue preparations, source-qualified\npolymers), and deciding it once is better than deciding it per record.\n\nBlocked on a curator's call; see also #236 on subject/file identity.","number":294,"state":"open","title":"Does the Section 3 narrowMatch pattern extend to source-material records?","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/294"},{"body":"At frozen campaign base `e000d66dd26ab053e9b17ce7bf8d3c873c3dde99`, `data/ingredients/mapped/Polypeptone.yaml` treats Hipolypepton as a casein-plus-soy blend and includes three Hipolypepton labels in the same owner. The official Wako catalog instead identifies unsuffixed Hipolypepton as enzymatically degraded casein, while Hipolypepton S/N/NS are soybean products. The existing equivalence can therefore collapse distinct supplied materials.\n\nPrimary evidence: [Wako official catalog](https://labchem-wako.fujifilm.com/us/catalog/pdf/catalog_0005.pdf), HIPOLYPEPTON Series section (catalog392-02115,500g); [JCM181 recipe](https://www.jcm.riken.jp/cgi-bin/jcm/jcm_grmd?GRMD=181) expressly specifies Hipolypepton(FUJIFILMWako). The catalog is product-family evidence, not historical lot equivalence or a quantitative peptide formula.\n\nWorker06 prepared isolated proposals `A-P-WAKO-HIPOLYPEPTON` and `A-P-POLYPEPTONE-BOUNDARY`: a distinct casein-hydrolysate product and three provenance-preserving REJECTED_LABEL corrections. Full owner/publication review and synchronized native integration remain required; no maintainedYAML,SSSOM or exports were changed by these proposals.\n\nThe baseline conflation originates in the2026-04-18 creation/mapping event; an August raw-alias backfill extended it. Exact source captures, baseline owner bytes and proposed bytes are bound under `reports/all_unmatched_20261107/worker-06/research-a/`. No matching issue was found in the coordinator’s628-issue live capture using case-insensitive Hipolypepton/Polypeptone/casein-soy searches.\n\nA fresh all-state GitHub search for Hipolypepton in issue titles and bodies immediately before filing returned no matches. This finding describes the explicit frozen campaign base, not an unverified claim about current main.\n","number":1202,"state":"open","title":"Separate casein-derived Hipolypepton from the Polypeptone owner","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1202"},{"body":"The approved #669 rollout requires form review before merging `Nh42ni_So42` and `Nh42ni_So42_X_6_H2o`. Related: historical #334.\n\nBoth records currently use CHEBI:86149, the hexahydrate. The bare owner's CAS 56110-30-6 and structure were enriched from the selected mapping; the explicit owner uses 7785-20-8. The source claims need to be kept distinct:\n\n- [PubChem's 56110-30-6 lookup](https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/rest/pug/compound/name/56110-30-6/property/MolecularFormula,MolecularWeight,InChI,InChIKey,IUPACName/JSON) resolves to CID 182663, H20N2NiO14S2, a hexahydrate. Its depositor synonym list includes that CAS string.\n- The [PubChem CAS annotation](https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/rest/pug_view/data/compound/182663/JSON?heading=CAS) assigns 7785-20-8 through FDA GSRS Q5LM426K64. [ChEBI:86149](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/api/ontologies/chebi/terms?obo_id=CHEBI:86149) and [Sigma 574988](https://www.sigmaaldrich.com/US/en/product/aldrich/574988) explicitly identify the hexahydrate with 7785-20-8.\n- CAS Common Chemistry API access returned 401, so there is no direct registry response verifying 56110-30-6. The old claim that it necessarily denotes anhydrous material remains unverified.\n\nArchived MediaDive concentrations for some bare-label sources fit roughly 394.99 g/mol. [NBRC 1199](https://www.nite.go.jp/nbrc/catalogue/NBRCMediumDetailServlet?NO=1199), however, gives only the bare nickel formula, with no hydration, CAS or catalogue. Omitted water does not prove anhydrous identity. Supplier/NBRC evidence is inspected web extraction; the failed MediaDive compound endpoint responses provide no identity evidence.\n\nFor this rollout, SUPPRESS the explicit hexahydrate's preferred name from the bare owner's published `other`, with unchanged records and a sidecar. Preserve the explicit owner's existing rejected bare-formula alias; do not silently reverse it or invent a replacement CAS.\n\nOptions are an explicit evidence-backed convention that the generic-label cohort denotes the hexahydrate, permitting a guarded merge, or distinct forms if original preparation/product evidence supports them. Recommendation: retain suppression and leave this issue open until registry/depositor provenance and source scope are reconciled. A future merge must preserve history, negative decisions and union overlapping memberships; current shared IDs are not independent form evidence.\n","number":1050,"state":"open","title":"Resolve bare ammonium nickel sulfate form and conflicting CAS evidence before merging","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1050"},{"body":"# Worker01: pH 7 filtered tomato juice proposal has the wrong parent-mapping grade\n\nFrozen proposal `RS-COMPLETE-FILTERED_TOMATO_JUICE_PH7` intends a distinct local preparation identity with `skos:broadMatch` to `MICRO:0000556` (filtered tomato juice). Its YAML instead sets `mapping_quality: BROAD_MATCH`.\n\nThe actual pinned native producer emits `skos:closeMatch` for that ontology row, plus the expected exact local registry row. An isolated in-memory control changing only the grade to `NARROW_MATCH` emits the intended `skos:broadMatch` and preserves the registry identity. No maintained or proposal bytes were changed.\n\n`MAPPING_SEMANTICS.md` says the MIM record is narrower and its target broader, and names `NARROW_MATCH` as the corresponding grade. The schema enum prose currently says the opposite; the repository precedence rule gives mapping semantics priority. This explains the trap but does not make the proposed row correct.\n\nFix the proposal grade and review generated predicates for all new proposals before integration. Keep distinct pH-qualified identity, no invented titrant, no role or release approval. A sodium-selenate-decahydrate draft also contains `BROAD_MATCH` and should be checked if incorporated; it was not part of the frozen 17 assembled proposals. Deduplicate the existing schema-description discrepancy separately rather than changing predicate behavior to accommodate this draft.\n\nFrozen assembled proposals SHA-256: `eb27d6de8b6a3403b131cb9ec1e21c65d300a2a977fa2acabdf6b463dcda1354`.\nNative reproduction: `.temp/all-unmatched-20261107/preflight/w01-shard-coordinator-review-v1/finding-002-native-predicate-reproduction.json`, SHA-256 `8eb2844955c2cde108d34eb05e97e1c3f071b4ba5bd88574136daab2ca013785`.\n\nThis is a provisional artifact finding, not a final-head review or full scientific approval. No live issue deduplication or GitHub mutation was performed by this reviewer.\n\nCoordinator deduplication: the live open-issue inventory confirms the general schema-description discrepancy is already tracked in #884 and the generic predicate question in #696. This issue tracks the distinct new provisional Worker 1 payload in #1160. Correct its grade under current semantics; do not change producer behavior or broaden this fix to those policy issues.\n","number":1164,"state":"open","title":"Correct the parent-mapping grade in the pH 7 filtered tomato juice proposal","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1164"},{"body":"The fresh 2026-11-07 KG-Microbe MediaDive node export retains `mediadive.ingredient:1315` with the label `Na acetate x 3 H2O`. MIM already has the exact sodium acetate trihydrate identity, `CHEBI:32138`, in `data/ingredients/mapped/Sodium_Acetate_Trihydrate.yaml`, but its accepted hydrate alias is `Na-acetate x 3 H2O` and the space spelling is absent.\n\nPrimary evidence inspected on 2026-11-08 UTC (2026-11-07 local):\n- [MediaDive ingredient 1315](https://mediadive.dsmz.de/download/ingredient/1315/json) explicitly pairs the exact source label with CAS 6131-90-4.\n- [ChEBI CHEBI:32138](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/CHEBI:32138), inspected through its OLS API record, is sodium acetate trihydrate with CAS 6131-90-4 and three water components in its formula and InChI.\n- [PubChem CID 23665404](https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/23665404), inspected through PUG REST, corroborates the trihydrate CAS and name.\n\nThis resolves the earlier `hydrate_review` requirement for source confirmation for this exact raw spelling. It does not release other raw-form holds, preparations, anhydrous sodium acetate or variable hydrates. The MediaDive formula has an extra hydrogen relative to ChEBI; retain that source discrepancy as provenance and do not copy it into MIM chemistry.\n\nThe source checkout is `kg-microbe-mim-1788-20261107`, read-only, at f10fb3909ed32898a54176ca77e509a950d16b84. Its consumed reviewed MIM export is already from MIM 0dac278466699129a083343a921c2e0ca18a9ab4, so this is an absent source spelling, not an unconsumed old mapping. Current MIM canonical position 2556 is supported; any owner-byte change must withdraw inherited support through the native receipt chain. A fresh mapping-only assessment must bind the exact new row, all six alias tokens, current owner and evidence before support may return. No role or whole-record scientific approval is implied.\n\nAcceptance: guarded source-alias write and history; synchronized YAML and generated surfaces; native SSSOM generation and explicit scoped publication; immutable row/owner receipt; complete supported/withheld candidate exports and companion provenance; full source-bound triage ledger including rare MicrobeDecoder compounds; required validation and independent final review. The KG-Microbe inputs/build remain unchanged; downstream adoption requires its own review and no merge or rebuild is part of this task.\n","number":1149,"state":"open","title":"Curate the verified MediaDive sodium acetate trihydrate spelling and preserve export review boundaries","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1149"},{"body":"Five retained aliases need preparation-stage scope review. `Fe(III)-EDTA (0.66% [wt/vol] in water)` explicitly names an aqueous formulation, while four other concentration-qualified strings may denote a stock or a receiving-medium dose. The latter four are not asserted to be proven wrong solely because they contain a concentration.\n\nExact reviewed scope:\n- Finding `CH-06`; stable key `w04-independent-CH-06`; immutable peer review `20261108T070033Z-w04-successor-science-v1`.\n- `w04-chem-proposal-d-biotin-spellings`; owner `data/ingredients/mapped/Biotin.yaml`; before `26056efadc5e57f081cde6e4ce0c2e25c03530c6d564ef0df25da3bcacb59c30`; proposed SHA256 `f5e754785ccf5509bc133fb413dddba09ff6e4f1767b8997b89294bb3eaf54d9`.\n  [Exact published proposal](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/blob/548e7bdd0ce2a68b3ae0df6ad7a6150a4e5c9bb9/reports/all_unmatched_20261107/worker-04/proposals/chemistry/Biotin.after.yaml).\n- `w04-chem-proposal-folate-b9`; owner `data/ingredients/mapped/Folic_Acid.yaml`; before `df85938271ec8f32a6d756f5213ba9661c9a7530822f333d2e02493b9f92e615`; proposed SHA256 `79b9b94956ba546897ca69172654b62bc1ff4ecd894a828da51dbe91cf4d2e2a`.\n  [Exact published proposal](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/blob/548e7bdd0ce2a68b3ae0df6ad7a6150a4e5c9bb9/reports/all_unmatched_20261107/worker-04/proposals/chemistry/Folic_Acid.after.yaml).\n- `w04-chem-proposal-ferric-edta`; owner `data/ingredients/mapped/Fe_Iii-edta.yaml`; before `583095cb1c81bca99e71bd4be2d6d2c876b1ff8923b77820107e6968d10078a2`; proposed SHA256 `f6166de4951a66032986107f6dac307cf264b15cd555f2d935c4cd3cd22d80bc`.\n  [Exact published proposal](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/blob/548e7bdd0ce2a68b3ae0df6ad7a6150a4e5c9bb9/reports/all_unmatched_20261107/worker-04/proposals/chemistry/Fe_Iii-edta.after.yaml).\n- `w04-chem-proposal-tungstate-malformed-alias`; owner `data/ingredients/mapped/Na2wo4_X_2_H2o.yaml`; before `b66951aba30cde0c3efe3b5ae2080a8d3445aebdffbd04b748e554f28358ffa9`; proposed SHA256 `ee4ddb16dcd7db924f74c49b55dbfb563d9da489a7a2adb9a0b03ee575a9da57`.\n  [Exact published proposal](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/blob/548e7bdd0ce2a68b3ae0df6ad7a6150a4e5c9bb9/reports/all_unmatched_20261107/worker-04/proposals/chemistry/Na2wo4_X_2_H2o.after.yaml).\n- `I1201-CHEM-P-ketoglutamate-acid-alias-rejection`; owner `data/ingredients/mapped/Alpha-ketoglutaric_Acid.yaml`; before `cc852c586e35a3ffa025ce9446da05dc235a6df8a7c0e7c2fa4c4d6ee18d371d`; proposed SHA256 `81b84944ab1121ee8c5abc5f78183d208baf1624db3844a8d0dac935a2fdca91`.\n  [Exact published proposal](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/blob/548e7bdd0ce2a68b3ae0df6ad7a6150a4e5c9bb9/reports/all_unmatched_20261107/worker-04/successors/issue-1201-v1/proposals/chemistry/Alpha-ketoglutaric_Acid.after.yaml).\n\nKeep the explicit aqueous FeIII-EDTA formulation distinct from the pure-solute owner. Recover the exact original stock-versus-dose context for the other four labels before deciding whether each may resolve. Preserve text, occurrence lineage and supported new aliases. Do not create a stock from concentration text alone, infer titrant/solvent amounts, reject every parenthetical concentration, or change the native synonym policy. The already-filtered bare parenthetical solution string is outside this defect.\n\n| Owner | Exact field | Literal | Current type |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `Biotin.yaml` | `synonyms/22` | `Biotin (1 μg/μL)` | `RAW_TEXT` |\n| `Folic_Acid.yaml` | `synonyms/23` | `Folic acid (5 μg/μL)` | `RAW_TEXT` |\n| `Fe_Iii-edta.yaml` | `synonyms/11` | `Fe(III)-EDTA (0.66% [wt/vol] in water)` | `RAW_TEXT` |\n| `Na2wo4_X_2_H2o.yaml` | `synonyms/2` | `Na2WO4 x 2 H2O (0.1% w/v)` | `HYDRATE_FORM` |\n| `Alpha-ketoglutaric_Acid.yaml` | `synonyms/6` | `alpha-ketoglutaric acid (100 mM).` | `RAW_TEXT` |\n\nThe exact field/value hashes, original source tags and complete occurrence-evidence pointers are preserved in `ACTIONABLE-MATRIX.json` CH-06. The listed proposal links expose the retained literals; no supplier page is being presented as proof of a source recipe’s stock stage.\n\nDeduplication: #1189 tracks four different W10 owners and is not exact coverage for these five literals. #1201 may track resulting source completion; it does not establish stock identity. No matching stock/owner tracker was found in the captured all-state bodies and relevant indexed search/comments.\n\nReview context: W04 published head `548e7bdd0ce2a68b3ae0df6ad7a6150a4e5c9bb9`, frozen campaign base `e000d66dd26ab053e9b17ce7bf8d3c873c3dde99`. Main as observed at `0dac278466699129a083343a921c2e0ca18a9ab4` has the same eighteen affected inherited-owner blobs as the frozen baseline; this is not a report of already-applied campaign curation. The full independent assembly covers 188 source cases and 115 proposals and remains `needs_curation`. Original peer review and evidence are immutable. Agent-authored new mapping evidence must satisfy #1226 without relabeling historical human evidence. The fix must use existing native guarded proposal validation and independent re-review; it grants no maintained-owner or export approval and requires no new gate, threshold, or synonym-selection rule.\n\n<!-- mim-review-round:20261108T071043Z-w04-final-scientific-issue-plan -->\n<!-- mim-review-bundle:concentration-alias-scope -->\n<!-- mim-fingerprints:w04-independent-CH-06 -->\n","number":1230,"state":"open","title":"Separate explicit stock and unresolved concentration aliases from pure-solute identity","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1230"},{"body":"Worker-02 continuation of #1201. These are isolated review proposals against the campaign's unchanged maintained owner bytes, not applied data changes. All artifact paths are repository relative. The published predecessor at `58f885fef05f8ff6e8b9b56fcb2479fef23b55c8` remains unchanged.\n\nFour current pure-substance owners accept concentration-qualified or hydration-erasing strings as resolving aliases. The proposed corrections preserve each exact token and original source as `REJECTED_LABEL`; they do not invent stock recipes or change the established solute identities.\n\n| Owner | Tokens made non-resolving | Bounded reason |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `Fe_Nh42_So42_X_6_H2o.yaml` | `Fe(NH4)2(SO4)2 x 6 H2O (0.01% w/v)`, `Fe(NH4)2(SO4)2 x 6 H2O (0.1% w/v)`, `Fe(NH4)2(SO4)2 x 6 H2O (2% v/v)` | Concentration-qualified preparation labels do not unconditionally identify neat Mohr hexahydrate. |\n| `Na-pyruvate.yaml` | `1 M Sodium pyruvate*` | The explicit 1 M preparation is not the neat sodium-pyruvate substance. |\n| `Voso4_X_5_H2o.yaml` | `VOSO4 x 5 H2O (0.01% w/v)`, `VOSO4` | One is preparation-qualified; the other omits the five waters required by the exact hydrate. |\n| `Methanol.yaml` | `Methanol (10%)` | The original stock-strength versus receiving-medium-dose interpretation is unestablished. Either interpretation is unsuitable as an unrestricted molecular-identity alias. |\n\nChEBI 76181, 50144 and 132758 establish the compared solute scopes. They are not evidence for an unstated solvent or a complete formulation. The vanadyl source's CAS12439-96-2 and owner's CAS14708-82-8 differ as annotations; the inspected ChEBI132758 response lists both, so their difference alone is not a chemical contradiction. Preserve both facts rather than silently replacing either.\n\nATCC2883 separately specifies filter-sterilized methanol, 3 mL added after autoclaving. The exact methanol proposal records that source-qualified treatment in notes and supplied_form on the existing molecular owner. It does not create a new mixture from filtration alone or infer assay, dilution, solvent or filter pore size.\n\n\n- Proposal `w02-v2-mohr-hexahydrate-composed`. Current campaign-baseline owner: `data/ingredients/mapped/Fe_Nh42_So42_X_6_H2o.yaml` (SHA256 `ba589668a7e9ed687122766204f51e531d64c54b2177759b555f97be6d7a916f`).\n- Exact proposed payload: `reports/all_unmatched_20261107/worker-02/continuation-1201-v1/integration/after/Fe_Nh42_So42_X_6_H2o.yaml` (SHA256 `a27f84e2768830f06b85fae4d31f8cc561bcc92467b0070630bafbeefde1b61d`).\n\n- Proposal `c2-proposal-pyruvate-stock-alias`. Current campaign-baseline owner: `data/ingredients/mapped/Na-pyruvate.yaml` (SHA256 `9d0af2a7f4568b68d50c3f766b255892f1e50cd1424236ab96447777ea8da4e4`).\n- Exact proposed payload: `reports/all_unmatched_20261107/worker-02/continuation-1201-v1/c/proposed-owners/c2-proposal-pyruvate-stock-alias.yaml` (SHA256 `a0dd95f327ce01bdcdd37747eb6e1ed75d311f570ed2e6283ecbbb21690ded11`).\n\n- Proposal `c2-proposal-vanadyl-stock-and-bare-aliases`. Current campaign-baseline owner: `data/ingredients/mapped/Voso4_X_5_H2o.yaml` (SHA256 `2903645ba0ba6c7c0455dc876db9b88bb355d72e0f8a818e269fdfb39800b192`).\n- Exact proposed payload: `reports/all_unmatched_20261107/worker-02/continuation-1201-v1/c/proposed-owners/c2-proposal-vanadyl-stock-and-bare-aliases.yaml` (SHA256 `3fa275792b65030133f2f375a6e325d08cad3e2a0442f2531ffbd42c5ce5aa08`).\n\n- Proposal `a2-proposal-methanol-source-filtration-provenance`. Current campaign-baseline owner: `data/ingredients/mapped/Methanol.yaml` (SHA256 `a8f09fa01565b9ba9a9caca7a5a78d2ea919bb6c54b26a170a64390caad46a7e`).\n- Exact proposed payload: `reports/all_unmatched_20261107/worker-02/continuation-1201-v1/a/after-v6/Methanol.yaml` (SHA256 `de3969545ff64b75aa8ded4c783eb37c5db2307348bd09bed3dec7d579f34d08`).\n\n- `CM_NAME:04fc9a1465f3d659efe08a5ae5dad64549020bcf412b8f9ccb55584876555c2c`; source `Fe(SO4)2(NH4)2 . 6H2O`; raw source label `Fe(SO4)2(NH4)2 . 6H2O`.\n- `CM_NAME:12f11ea24b8277a93aa7239dc2e5d98a1ede7a288ecdc58381d46cc339ac5435`; source `Methanol(filter sterilized)`; raw source label `Methanol(filter sterilized)`.\n- `CM_NAME:1bd8d675450108231a5a447707552676245f24c975891b217f1caa5f8170262b`; source `(NH4)2Fe(SO4)2·6H2O`; raw source label `(NH4)2Fe(SO4)2·6H2O`.\n- `CM_NAME:664ea49b4baffce477446f17556f93e233f5535d0abfdec29fbb8f7384caf80c`; source `Fe(SO4)2(NH4)2·6H2O`; raw source label `Fe(SO4)2(NH4)2·6H2O`.\n- `CM_NAME:904d8b9b6ef754a946575436e8d68a149ec7891b40a2e40eb24351912f196097`; source `FeSO4(NH4)2SO4.6H2O`; raw source label `FeSO4(NH4)2SO4.6H2O`.\n- `CM_NAME:a0904f210b0689887db38c10d22989b237ba0a2a60d27f2801a85077d9ee6b6c`; source `(NH4)2SO4FeSO4 × 6H2O`; raw source label `(NH4)2SO4FeSO4 × 6H2O`.\n- `CM_NAME:ae541eb6dc54dff13a0406bbea554e9ed353683fe05493deb6192a5da62596f7`; source `(NH4)2Fe(SO4)2・6H2O`; raw source label `(NH4)2Fe(SO4)2・6H2O`.\n- `KG_CASE:43cc527abd97db8f30e73215d6cf922734ae7eec0fa7b9303c3bb4227558a0ea`; source `mediadive.ingredient:113`; raw source label `Na-pyruvate`.\n- `KG_CASE:4b7e86a2a10425a7d3777449e0375b761cb3821b26155e1e41bf69bf571e5e76`; source `mediadive.ingredient:106`; raw source label `Fe(NH4)2(SO4)2`.\n- `KG_CASE:a5de5680f410f37548a4708f6c56d5137e9140044007527774d011ae38029d60`; source `mediadive.ingredient:762`; raw source label `Na pyruvate`.\n- `KG_CASE:fa582b7326d5d9051fe2a53c1ed88df58acce880cd09ed3c2661153cf247fc15`; source `mediadive.ingredient:277`; raw source label `VOSO4 x 5 H2O`.\n\n- `c-chebi-76181`: [https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/api/ontologies/chebi/terms?obo_id=CHEBI:76181](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/api/ontologies/chebi/terms?obo_id=CHEBI:76181); accessed `2026-11-08T05:07:38.350964+00:00`; `reports/all_unmatched_20261107/worker-02/c/captures/chebi-76181.json` (SHA256 `7c66c3f2971ef73a26f7ac0530c5cb9e7fd9c279d28e0fb955d0e89b73260a45`).\n- `c-chebi-50144`: [https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/api/ontologies/chebi/terms?obo_id=CHEBI:50144](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/api/ontologies/chebi/terms?obo_id=CHEBI:50144); accessed `2026-11-08T05:07:40.864927+00:00`; `reports/all_unmatched_20261107/worker-02/c/captures/chebi-50144.json` (SHA256 `0d96d95af7783f3b4842a2c748e6e09e3e638436988b096aa19f377587f5d82c`).\n- `c-chebi-132758`: [https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/api/ontologies/chebi/terms?obo_id=CHEBI:132758](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/api/ontologies/chebi/terms?obo_id=CHEBI:132758); accessed `2026-11-08T05:07:38.489048+00:00`; `reports/all_unmatched_20261107/worker-02/c/captures/chebi-132758.json` (SHA256 `fb200a93b215d6811e2bd540688a2d217377cd502c9d84d7aaaf14a78f3b5b01`).\n- `c-chebi-87020`: [https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/api/ontologies/chebi/terms?obo_id=CHEBI:87020](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/api/ontologies/chebi/terms?obo_id=CHEBI:87020); accessed `2026-11-08T05:07:37.863257+00:00`; `reports/all_unmatched_20261107/worker-02/c/captures/chebi-87020.json` (SHA256 `fbf850a3ccae6ed0e62d3e78d0da4bd252a51af85a59c3d70294801eef4adaeb`).\n- `a-primary-atcc-2883`: [https://www.atcc.org/~/media/E4D6EB3B1C80408996E0DD53A0D258A1.ashx](https://www.atcc.org/~/media/E4D6EB3B1C80408996E0DD53A0D258A1.ashx); accessed `2026-11-08T05:25:43.510566+00:00`; `reports/all_unmatched_20261107/worker-02/a/captures/atcc-2883-primary.pdf` (SHA256 `78179650fdbf7dcb07f6d07d0263470c64b732bfb44580478a5f9e67209448d9`).\n\nIndependent reviewer `/root/final_reviewer` approved the exact composed Mohr payload and its seven scoped cases in `reports/all_unmatched_20261107/worker-02/continuation-1201-v1/d/independent-review-mohr-v2.json` (SHA256 `9b22001a7400cf1c979dc26ba526ade6155245d79502e564b46ed35bf98cbdab`). This is not a whole-owner, merge or publication approval.\nIndependent reviewer `/root/research_a` approved the exact pyruvate and vanadyl payloads within the declared C-review scope in `reports/all_unmatched_20261107/worker-02/continuation-1201-v1/a/review-c/independent-review.json` (SHA256 `37577be2e1881e5e32fb430f68ffd15a7dedc86994b135c9bd5764f3b3116a71`). This is not a whole-owner, merge or publication approval.\nIndependent reviewer `/root/research_b` approved the exact methanol payload within the declared A-review scope in `reports/all_unmatched_20261107/worker-02/continuation-1201-v1/b/independent-review-a-binder-v1.json` (SHA256 `f4521c3947dc1a79cde4b5ac94bae8770470cd4c2b83faeab6171b1c931a4206`). This is not a whole-owner, merge or publication approval.\n\nThe Mohr receipt binds the final composed successor, including all six preserved earlier source aliases and the three new rejections. Approval of C's isolated fragment alone was not treated as approval of that composition. Bare-formula, generic-hydrate, role-like and other full-owner obligations remain held.\n\nVanadyl requires cross-shard reconciliation: the captured coordinator observation records worker10 `chem-proposal-113` at head `dbaf4629f3b93d3bd8a78028e243abfe90c7a922`, payload `reports/all_unmatched_20261107/worker-10/chemicals/proposed/Voso4_X_5_H2o.yaml`, SHA256 `7b38dec9fd6f28a46639ef4dd0f14d7c2bcd4db6c5bfca1f33f9dfe76fcbeef4`, against the same baseline owner. It proposes a source formula-ordering alias from TOGO M2262. That is a distinct delta from worker02's two rejections. Preserve both proposal histories and require independent review of the exact combined bytes before integration; this observation does not attest worker10's scientific review, choose a winner or approve worker10's source cases. Observation: `reports/all_unmatched_20261107/worker-02/continuation-1201-v1/validation/cross-shard-overlaps-v1.json` (SHA256 `5ff9da544c2b1bfa043712df04eedc85fda2d4942adb0a7b749f9692d9a280b3`).\n\nExisting #1189 covers glyoxylate, vanadate, AQDS and carbonate, not these four owners. #1203 concerns magnesium-sulfate stock occurrences. #702 mentions Na-pyruvate for a historical CAS-to-ChEBI import defect, and #752 mentions methanol within hydrogen-gas aliases; neither covers these exact tokens. This is a bounded related bundle, not a closure of those issues or #718's release umbrella. Keep the recorded Mohr, sodium-pyruvate and vanadyl WITHHOLD reasons and all other source/owner/release obligations until separately assessed.\n\nBefore applying any accepted delta, compare the then-current owner bytes and reconcile concurrent proposals. Preserve every raw label, original source reference, existing REJECTED_LABEL, WITHHOLD, source-quantity and downstream hold. Use guarded curation with history and the required maintained-surface/SSSOM synchronization and native validation. This draft requests no merge, downstream rebuild, automatic owner release or closure of broader issues.\n\n<!-- mim-review-round: worker02-continuation1201-v1 -->\n<!-- mim-review-bundle: new-pure-owner-qualified-aliases -->\n<!-- mim-fingerprints: 0e9bcfb4f0eaf50beefb3c1acbacc9f2a174b6d99ba22ed2738f6ee5f7ecbf3c,88defd96a502824a9c55ced34e60ef406af757f1033f33b506bfcaadd31be184,fd47fc3cb6f1875603d90fd0e81a0c2e8375b99de057d45cb1c1ead7a331d7d3,70913314a90cf18234d8d93a51d79bfe3cb41ade002718376845f2c8091cfc50 -->\n","number":1220,"state":"open","title":"Preserve stock and supplied-form boundaries in Mohr, pyruvate, vanadyl and methanol aliases","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1220"},{"body":"Worker 1's full-shard evidence at `1894d28d94df4e654ebce9752c23a2321aeb21c5` assigns `IR-SHARD-ARTIFACT-1` through `IR-SHARD-ARTIFACT-12` to #1159 in `reports/all_unmatched_20261107/worker-01/shard/independent_review/final-188-scientific-review-v1.json`. Its active shard report repeats that attribution. The live #1159 body covers only the three earlier canary findings (`IR-DRAFT-1`, `IR-DRAFT-2`, `IR-VALIDATION-1`) and has no comments expanding its scope.\n\nThis issue records the distinct full-shard finding set and the publication provenance correction `W01-PUBLISHED-001`. Preserve #1159's canary identity. The full-shard review reports these corrections in isolated proposals, independently re-reviewed before publication:\n\n| Finding | Defect and corrected scope |\n| --- | --- |\n| IR-SHARD-ARTIFACT-1 | Distinguish the unsuccessful JCM956 retrieval from the explicit JCM574 fallback and actual MDS context. |\n| IR-SHARD-ARTIFACT-2 | Remove the false discrepancy between frozen `10 MILLIMOLAR` and `10 mM` for case105; retain the actual reagent-form uncertainty. |\n| IR-SHARD-ARTIFACT-3 | Correct named case104's receiving dose from 1 to 2 mL. |\n| IR-SHARD-ARTIFACT-4 | Distinguish case138's current recipe1188 direct reference to852 from the frozen1187 inheritance narrative. |\n| IR-SHARD-ARTIFACT-5 | Correct case103's receiving dose from 5 to10 mL. |\n| IR-SHARD-ARTIFACT-6 | Correct case112's receiving dose from 1 to1.5 mL. |\n| IR-SHARD-ARTIFACT-7 | Remove root40's unsupported aqueous assertion; retain the actual solvent evidence gap. |\n| IR-SHARD-ARTIFACT-8 | Keep Middlebrook7H10 dry base distinct from the NCIT enriched finished medium. |\n| IR-SHARD-ARTIFACT-9 | Retire source-local/generic aliases that would resolve globally, including bare numbered pointers; qualify the JCM431 stock name and mapping label. |\n| IR-SHARD-ARTIFACT-10 | Reconcile four duplicate proposal families while preserving each source-case/occurrence lineage. The NBRC1257/1321 bicarbonate and sulfide pairs are also tracked by #1166; NBRC1339 phenanthrene and NBRC943 Metal44 are the other two families. |\n| IR-SHARD-ARTIFACT-11 | Preserve added-water quantities without treating them as final solution volumes or inferred final concentrations; retain explicit preparation and comparison bases. |\n| IR-SHARD-ARTIFACT-12 | Correct false or unrelated missing-evidence requirements for cases38/64 and34/59/66, preserving the source-supported remaining gaps. |\n\nThe separate coordinator findings remain on #1163, #1164, #1166, #1167 and #1168. This tracker does not replace them or grant maintained-record, mapping, role, component or release approval.\n\nAppend an immutable provenance correction or successor, update active report/issue references and the PR description, and preserve old primary captures, proposal payloads, native saved reviews and canary files byte-for-byte. Do not rewrite the historical review merely to conceal its mistaken issue reference. The published correction must bind the exact prior review hash and all twelve finding IDs. Obtain independent final-head re-review of the new references and confirm the scientific payloads are unchanged.\n\nIndependent evidence: `.temp/all-unmatched-20261107/preflight/w01-published-head-review-v1/finding-001-tracker-scope.json`; prior full-shard review SHA256 `02b2be72b448d35b573ad992a3d2c98afcd3f16430709e10d6837564b595edcf`; active report SHA256 `762ba8d961cfdfc856c724d2a4b232cc776bbc43861e34a0f7d28af70b06f1df`. A live all-state GitHub issue search for `IR-SHARD-ARTIFACT` found no existing dedicated tracker. The local evidence search included ignored files. No scientific payload defect was found in the independent 188-case/76-proposal reassessment.\n","number":1173,"state":"open","title":"Track Worker 1 full-shard findings separately from the canary provenance","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1173"},{"body":"Found in the second review of PR #881. This predates #881.\n\nWhen a mapping-change receipt touches a row, the assembler marks it WITHHOLD with the receipt as its basis, which is correct. But two kinds of reason, keyed by the row's baseline position, replace the receipt's reason:\n- concern-cohort reasons (`reports/sssom_completion_20260921/assemble_review.py`, around lines 391-397);\n- explicit holds (around lines 495-498).\n\n`build_review.py` copies that text into `assertion-dispositions.tsv`, and `reviewed_sssom.py` exports `review_reason` into the released `mapping-dispositions.tsv`. A row can therefore ship with a reason that describes a defect the receipt fixed.\n\n**Examples on #881's head (54e09be6):**\n\n| row | record | published reason says | actual state |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 2021 | `Na2so4_X_10_H2o` | \"final other has an anhydrous label\" | #881 removed it |\n| 1608 | `L-cysteine_Hcl_X_H2o` | \"final SSSOM exports concentration/no-water tokens\" | #881 removed the concentration token |\n| 1869 | `Mgso4_X_7_H2o` | holds on the cross-record baseline | #881 deleted that baseline pair, and the token is gone from the row |\n| 1256 (on main) | Furaltadone | \"still emits it as a narrow match\" | it is an exact match now |\n\nSemantic-review finding SEM:ae352c84b5456ae95a4a stays OPEN with the same outdated text (`finding-dispositions.tsv`).\n\n**Effect:** dispositions stay conservatively WITHHOLD, so nothing is wrongly released. But the published reasons misstate the current rows, and a reviewer reading them would chase defects that are already fixed.\n\n**Fix:**\n- When a receipt has touched a row, label the carried reason as recorded against the pre-receipt payload, or put the receipt's reason first.\n- Reconcile the fixed findings with the reconcile-review-records skill.\n","number":902,"state":"open","title":"Review reasons carried past a mapping-change receipt still describe defects the receipt fixed","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/902"},{"body":"Four owners accept source labels that overstate sodium stoichiometry, hydration, counterion identity or equivalence between a stock and its solute. The worker-10 source review supplies exact bounded corrections against campaign baseline `e000d66dd26ab053e9b17ce7bf8d3c873c3dde99`. Raw labels remain preserved as provenance; the changes do not authorize publication of each complete owner payload.\n\n## Findings and primary evidence\n\n### Na2-glyoxalate overstates sodium count\n\nOwner: `data/ingredients/mapped/Na2-glyoxalate.yaml`. Proposal: `kg-glyoxylate-label`.\n\nMediaDive ingredient 361 gives formula C2HNaO3 and molecular mass 96.017, consistent with CHEBI:91251 sodium glyoxylate. Its CAS-RN field is null. Correct the preferred label to sodium glyoxylate and preserve the literal Na2-glyoxalate as REJECTED_LABEL. Recipe concentration arithmetic is only internally consistent derived metadata, not independent experimental evidence. No disodium identity is established.\n\nPrimary references: [kg-md361](https://mediadive.dsmz.de/rest/ingredient/361); [kg-medium_298b](https://mediadive.dsmz.de/rest/medium/298b); [kg-CHEBI_91251](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/api/ontologies/chebi/terms?iri=http%3A%2F%2Fpurl.obolibrary.org%2Fobo%2FCHEBI_91251).\n\n### Variable-water vanadate is not a monohydrate synonym\n\nOwner: `data/ingredients/mapped/Navo3_X_H2o.yaml`. Proposal: `kg-vanadate-variable-hydrate`.\n\nThe Navo3_X_H2o owner has exact CHEBI:132095 monohydrate identity but accepts `NaVO3 x n H2O` as HYDRATE_FORM. MediaDive 700 preserves variable hydration, while ingredient 182 explicitly identifies the monohydrate. Retype the variable-n label as REJECTED_LABEL and retain its unresolved source-form requirement; leave the independently specific monohydrate case unchanged. Other inherited unqualified sodium vanadate/metavanadate hydrate aliases still require full-owner review.\n\nPrimary references: [kg-md700](https://mediadive.dsmz.de/rest/ingredient/700); [kg-md182](https://mediadive.dsmz.de/rest/ingredient/182); [kg-CHEBI_132095](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/api/ontologies/chebi/terms?iri=http%3A%2F%2Fpurl.obolibrary.org%2Fobo%2FCHEBI_132095).\n\n### AQDS salt owner accepts the bare dianion name\n\nOwner: `data/ingredients/mapped/AQDS.yaml`. Proposal: `kg-aqds-dianion-alias`.\n\nThe source salt is CAS 853-68-9, and MediaDive 521/PubChem CID 70070 support disodium C14H6Na2O8S2. The bare anthraquinone-2,6-disulfonate denotes the dianion represented by CHEBI:85112. Retype that bare alias as REJECTED_LABEL on the disodium owner; retain qualified Na2 wording and the supplied salt identity. This does not merge the salt with its active ion.\n\nPrimary references: [kg-md521](https://mediadive.dsmz.de/rest/ingredient/521); [kg-pc_aqds](https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/rest/pug/compound/cid/70070/property/InChI,InChIKey,IUPACName,MolecularFormula/JSON); [kg-CHEBI_85112](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/api/ontologies/chebi/terms?iri=http%3A%2F%2Fpurl.obolibrary.org%2Fobo%2FCHEBI_85112).\n\n### Na2CO3-CO2 stock is not pure carbonate\n\nOwner: `data/ingredients/mapped/Na2co3.yaml`. Proposal: `kg-carbonate-buffer-alias`.\n\nThe CHEBI:29377 sodium carbonate owner accepts `Na2CO3-CO2 buffer`. MediaDive ingredient 1685 and JCM 209 recipe context describe a 4% preparation supplied as 100 mL, not dry solute identity. Reject that stock alias on the pure-substance owner while preserving the raw source and unresolved CO2/preparation requirements. Do not assign this stock to another formulation merely because it contains carbonate. Inherited malformed NaCO3/Na CO aliases remain withheld; the separate Sodium_Carbonate_Solution owner still needs its own primary-source correction.\n\nPrimary references: [kg-md1685](https://mediadive.dsmz.de/rest/ingredient/1685); [kg-mdJ209](https://mediadive.dsmz.de/rest/medium/J209); [kg-CHEBI_29377](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/api/ontologies/chebi/terms?iri=http%3A%2F%2Fpurl.obolibrary.org%2Fobo%2FCHEBI_29377).\n\n## Exact proposal artifacts\n\nAll paths below are relative to `reports/all_unmatched_20261107/worker-10/` on `review/all-unmatched-w10-source-review-20261107`. Source captures include URLs, retrieval times and SHA256 digests in `kg/evidence.json`. `kg/decisions.json` binds each claim to its frozen case. `reviews/root-review-of-kg-v1.json` independently assesses all 52 KG cases and nine bounded proposal deltas.\n\n| Proposal | Baseline owner SHA256 | Proposed payload and SHA256 |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `kg-glyoxylate-label` | `4f40039bb78bdf2657e2bbbbf6a1c6f8ca6d31a1fd56f3101f795cc9e3c4ebc8` | `kg/proposed/glyoxylate-label.yaml`; `33eff8fa0f121579f1c42bc3cef6ac49abae0ac5415b647dca299b10cd811349` |\n| `kg-vanadate-variable-hydrate` | `d4d3f05a57d260547b23249d5fb316600c7b5adb56887463394cbe3417dc57e4` | `kg/proposed/vanadate-variable-hydrate.yaml`; `74df07af5a618ea4f2bc0402fb6566b1a7777c1ff8befa8612ca4a1d9c881e7a` |\n| `kg-aqds-dianion-alias` | `0d70097612d9e5c0c4855bb8d86dc22fbbfc754f09a553819f5f1fcb5fa0eb29` | `kg/proposed/aqds-dianion-alias.yaml`; `58adfba88aa6819f3af7877287f27afc0fda277f0032d4d851aeb551cccc5007` |\n| `kg-carbonate-buffer-alias` | `e6526a84af20b9b9d0c0c52ef2d53fb354508adc13b648190dcd753be8ff7650` | `kg/proposed/carbonate-buffer-alias.yaml`; `b8e08c21022fcbd8b96d492f0365309e23cc52c241ea5ae47af5d37848efa9b8` |\n\n## Required follow-up and limits\n\nReview the exact current owner bytes before applying the proposed deltas. If they have changed since the frozen baseline, reconcile those changes explicitly. Preserve every existing WITHHOLD, REJECTED_LABEL, source-context and downstream hold. Complete owner/role/publication assessment remains separate from these supported corrections. Structural LinkML validation of the proposal files does not establish scientific completeness.\n\nApply any accepted correction through the native guarded workflow with curation history, synchronized maintained surfaces, appropriate SSSOM updates and required validation. No owner merge, downstream rebuild, blanket alias activation or release approval is requested by this issue.\n\n## Deduplication\n\nNo exact four-owner bundle was found in the live all-state issue snapshot. #251 concerns the historical hydrate-report blind spot and is closed; #459 is a wider hydrate cohort and #901 concerns asymmetric parent predicates. #1157 records five different source IDs. #718 comment 6717638594 already preserves a whole-owner carbonate hold and a different DSMZ 863 stock proposal; this JCM 209 alias finding is a bounded continuation, not proof that the whole carbonate owner is releasable. #1117 concerns carbonate monohydrate, and #1121 concerns carbonate used as a ceftazidime stabilizer. None establishes that these specific aliases are safe. Link this scoped follow-up to #718/#459 and do not close those umbrellas.\n\nThe checked GitHub issue snapshots and complete relevant comment payloads are retained under `context/kg-issue-*-live.json`; the all-state snapshot is `context/kg-issues-all-live.json`. The borax component dependency already belongs to #1168 and is excluded here.\n\n<!-- mim-review-round: 20261108T052536Z-worker10-kg-source-review -->\n<!-- mim-review-bundle: kg-form-stock-aliases -->\n<!-- mim-fingerprints: d8caf598fddc408d4016c18556da31458bd850bad08db8777b4438c16d0f07d0,f746083e9a713ab6de3670ae48bce81b27884a8bfe281a81bd7c4c003372c6eb,a41c45d77c6dc0d9258507d1966cc7a49e8689779e5900c1c6616f9a19f81867,f0a7618beced896742900671d55d686ab59796f1f822ec6d79d5caa2339f7149 -->\n","number":1189,"state":"open","title":"Preserve chemical form and stock boundaries in glyoxylate, vanadate, AQDS and carbonate aliases","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1189"},{"body":"The worker-10 successor source review found two maintained identities whose exact mappings lose a source-supported chemical distinction. The inspected baseline is `e000d66dd26ab053e9b17ce7bf8d3c873c3dde99`; these are isolated proposals, not changes to maintained YAML or a publication approval.\n\nA read-only GitHub `main` check at 2026-11-08T06:11:28.616136+00:00 found the relevant owner bytes identical to this frozen baseline. Exact decoded-body hashes and API captures are retained in `reports/all_unmatched_20261107/worker-10/successor-v1/issues/current-main-owner-check.json`.\n\n| Owner | Baseline assertion | Supported bounded correction |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `data/ingredients/mapped/Pipes.yaml` | `CHEBI:39033` exact PIPES grouping identity, although its CAS is 5625-37-6 | Evaluate the exact free-acid owner `CHEBI:44933`, preserving separately prepared PIPES buffers and salt owners. |\n| `data/ingredients/mapped/Dl-aspartic_Acid.yaml` | `CHEBI:22660` exact stereo-unspecified aspartic acid identity for a DL racemate | Preserve the verified racemate as `cas:617-45-8`, with the stereo-unspecified term only as an asymmetric broader mapping; retain racemate names and remove unsupported generic identity/structure claims. |\n\nMediaDive ingredient 867 identifies the PIPES reagent by CAS 5625-37-6, formula C8H18N2O6S2 and mass 302.37; its medium 964 context weighs the reagent. ChEBI44933 and PubChem79723 agree on the specific acid. This evidence is source-specific: it does not identify the supplied form in an unrelated final-10-mM PIPES occurrence or make every PIPES-buffer label an acid synonym. Primary references: [MediaDive867](https://mediadive.dsmz.de/rest/ingredient/867), [medium964](https://mediadive.dsmz.de/rest/medium/964), [ChEBI44933](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/CHEBI:44933), [PubChem79723](https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/79723).\n\nThe DL-aspartic-acid source is MediaDive1445. TCI A0544 and NIST identify the DL material with CAS617-45-8, while ChEBI22660's definition and structure do not specify the racemic mixture. A CAS cross-reference on a broader ontology term does not by itself make their identity scopes equal. Primary references: [MediaDive1445](https://mediadive.dsmz.de/rest/ingredient/1445), [TCI A0544](https://www.tcichemicals.com/US/en/p/A0544), [NIST617-45-8](https://webbook.nist.gov/cgi/cbook.cgi?ID=617-45-8), [ChEBI22660](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/CHEBI:22660).\n\nExact proposal/evidence references are `reports/all_unmatched_20261107/worker-10/successor-v1/kg/{decisions,evidence,proposals}.json`: cases184 and166; proposals `sv1-kg-pipes-free-acid` and `sv1-kg-dl-aspartic-racemate`. The proposal ledger binds actual before-owner and proposed-payload hashes.\n\nThis is a concrete child scope of [#718](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/718). Its [worker01 PIPES comment](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/718#issuecomment-6717638594) concerns an unresolved final-concentration occurrence; that hold remains. Closed [#114](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/114) describes historical buffer modelling, and closed [#416](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/416) describes registry-row generation; neither establishes the exact source reagent identities above.\n\nIntegrate only after reconciling current owner bytes and cross-worker proposals under MAPPING_SEMANTICS sections3 and6. Preserve every source/downstream hold and rejected label, withdraw stale content-bound support, synchronize maintained surfaces and validate exact identity, registry rows and exported aliases. These bounded corrections do not approve the entire inherited owner, roles or release.\n","number":1211,"state":"open","title":"Correct source-supported PIPES and DL-aspartic acid identities now mapped to broader terms","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1211"},{"body":"Independent review found a safety defect in the **uncommitted, standalone cleanup helper prepared for this thread**. It is outside PR #1106's tracked source. Neither its production verification nor cleanup mode was executed, and no real branch was affected. The user required stopping on any failure, so #1106 was removed from the merge queue and remains unmerged.\n\nThe helper constructs this transaction at `cleanup_owned_branch.py:268`:\n\n```text\noption no-deref\nstart\ndelete refs/heads/OWNED EXPECTED_HEAD\ndelete refs/remotes/origin/OWNED EXPECTED_HEAD\nprepare\ncommit\n```\n\nThe stdin `option no-deref` protects only the next command naming a ref. If the second tracking ref becomes a symbolic ref to an unrelated branch at the same expected OID after the earlier symbolic-ref check, its deletion follows that target. The intermediate inventory compares names and resolved OIDs, so it does not detect this substitution. The exact transaction returned zero in an isolated fixture and deleted the unrelated branch; the tracking symref remained dangling. The later inventory detects the damage only after deletion.\n\nThe reproduction deliberately injects a race outside the helper README's coordinator-exclusivity assumption. That limitation does not make the intended no-dereference guard effective. This is a confirmed unsafe property of the prepared helper, not evidence that any concurrent session changed a real ref.\n\nPreserved evidence under `output/approved-plan-20261101T031134Z/step7b-exception-20261102/cleanup-preparation/`:\n\n- Frozen helper SHA256: `5c013cd59968d4bac27e06442bb8affc8a2523e4c4cbc45f68d03f97758cc65f`.\n- Independent review `independent-review/review.json`: `dc6b80de8c2634c5d198a5ed7ba5132ee7c547297a92b6bd4860357438a48ca6`.\n- Reproduction `independent-review/symbolic-tracking-results.json`: `cb3a08abf4fc4dfd2bd1a9f9e531e1e820c6d5c0d44ef3d11e38de1872a2e388`.\n- Actual fixture commands `independent-review/symbolic-tracking-commands.json`: `8cf0ca5c171701659624f3384a213684a5a482bb0f6dfe1746d67e49d61582f3`.\n\nBefore any reuse, protect every deletion against dereferencing and independently reproduce the symbolic-substitution case with all unrelated refs preserved. No repair has been applied, and the frozen helper must not be executed. Any repair and resumed closure need a new reviewed execution decision; this issue does not authorize changing #1106, its tests, gates, approved snapshot pair, or scientific holdings.\n\nPR #1106's head remains `6372aca7eaa9d2863d01aef3a778334709a89aaf`. Its exact-pair review, local QC/full suite and all sixteen PR CI checks passed before this separate cleanup review. Queue completion, squash merge, branch deletion and Step 7c are not claimed.\n","number":1109,"state":"open","title":"Prepared PR #1106 cleanup helper can delete an unrelated branch through a symbolic tracking ref","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1109"},{"body":"The unapplied W04 BD_228950_GC_Agar_Base.yaml payload is opaque despite TOGO M2206 explicitly listing six dry-base components: NaCl 5 g, KH2PO4 1 g, K2HPO4 4 g, agar 10 g, corn starch 1 g and Proteose Peptone No. 3 15 g (nominal total 36 g). Preparation water, hemoglobin and IsoVitaleX are separate. The captured manufacturer SDS identifies BD Diagnostic Systems, not evidence for the proposal’s narrower Difco brand assertion.\n\nExact reviewed scope:\n- Finding `MEDIA-SCI-02`; stable key `w04-independent-MEDIA-SCI-02`; immutable peer review `20261108T070033Z-w04-successor-science-v1`.\n- `media-product-bd_228950_gc_agar_base`; owner `data/ingredients/mapped/BD_228950_GC_Agar_Base.yaml`; before `absent (new proposal; no maintained owner created)`; proposed SHA256 `0372144e888937706c0c71cbca4a4c2ee04a4c3729dab6281f520e1e35a4e6e2`.\n  [Exact published proposal](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/blob/548e7bdd0ce2a68b3ae0df6ad7a6150a4e5c9bb9/reports/all_unmatched_20261107/worker-04/proposals/media/BD_228950_GC_Agar_Base.yaml).\n\nRepresent the six literal source-supported dry-base components and a source-specific component assertion with honest completeness/grounding limits. Preserve unresolved component form instead of borrowing a convenient hydrated or branded owner. Keep water, hemoglobin and IsoVitaleX outside this dry base. Use evidenced BD manufacturer scope unless independent evidence establishes Difco. Preserve exact recipe source, quantities, historical payload and all approval/role/export holds.\n\nEvidence:\n\n- [Primary/ontology source](https://togomedium.org/sparqlist/api/gmdb_medium_by_gmid?gm_id=M2206); capture SHA256 `353f8020959754028cfc23c7bac0095f88114dd9194bf07f84aebbfc6c174044`; components paragraph_index11 GC agar base(BD228950), paragraph9 SolutionA, paragraph13 SolutionB; comments4-6\n- [Primary/ontology source](https://imgcdn.mckesson.com/CumulusWeb/Click_and_learn/SDS_BBL_MEDIA_AGAR_GC_MED_BASE_500G.pdf); capture SHA256 `8f1a0987df3db96433a0dbe971ed0dee591c3ca34d65e99d09ce1c6157648cea`; Sections1 ProductIdentifier/Manufacturer,2 selected hazard ingredients,9 powder physical form\n\nDeduplication: Fresh all-state 228950 search found no exact tracker. #1201 is related completeness context; this dedicated new-payload correction has a specific composition and unsupported brand scope.\n\nReview context: W04 published head `548e7bdd0ce2a68b3ae0df6ad7a6150a4e5c9bb9`, frozen campaign base `e000d66dd26ab053e9b17ce7bf8d3c873c3dde99`. Main as observed at `0dac278466699129a083343a921c2e0ca18a9ab4` has the same eighteen affected inherited-owner blobs as the frozen baseline; this is not a report of already-applied campaign curation. The full independent assembly covers 188 source cases and 115 proposals and remains `needs_curation`. Original peer review and evidence are immutable. Agent-authored new mapping evidence must satisfy #1226 without relabeling historical human evidence. The fix must use existing native guarded proposal validation and independent re-review; it grants no maintained-owner or export approval and requires no new gate, threshold, or synonym-selection rule.\n\n<!-- mim-review-round:20261108T071043Z-w04-final-scientific-issue-plan -->\n<!-- mim-review-bundle:gc228950-composition -->\n<!-- mim-fingerprints:w04-independent-MEDIA-SCI-02 -->\n","number":1235,"state":"open","title":"Complete source-stated BD 228950 GC agar base composition and correct brand scope","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1235"},{"body":"Independent review of Worker08’s published `b90a2fd4681d20e2586481b7b9b1114a78281b94` found bounded corrections in isolated proposals. Maintained owners/canonical mappings have not been changed by this review. The complete source assessment has188 cases and113 selected payloads;94 affected payloads are a union across findings, not94 chemical rejections.\n\nThe frozen external review is `.temp/all-unmatched-20261107/workers/w03/worktree/.temp/w08-published-head-independent-review-v1/`: findings SHA256 `11e3681d90be88d52a309fc6ed301bcb4c37c749ffb144640175cea6db501af5`, full manifest `9b34903748fba1da78b5649ad471a1804da04a78b7775004d71f6b8d36f8cf76`. Coordinator triage independently verified all5070 manifest members and all113 exact Git payloads; its action matrix SHA256 is `9a26d2adf42df8195f19b2090754978db063dfe515f8738a2a54f9d4c3f772f4`. Original findings, drafts, captures and reviews remain immutable.\n\nThis tracker covers the following seven remaining W08 groups. W08-IR-001 belongs to1226, IR-003/004 to1201, IR-008 to811 and IR-010 to1210; those scopes are not duplicated here.1173 tracks Worker01 lineage, and718 is broader context rather than this correction ledger.\n\n| Finding | Confirmed defect | Bounded correction |\n|---|---|---|\n| W08-IR-002 | JCM403 numbered pointers remain global resolving labels | Qualify preferred terms with JCM and preparation identity; preserve exact old pointer strings and all source provenance as REJECTED_LABEL; retain source occurrence routing and append history. Re-review exact successor and all backlinks; do not resolve generic pointers by label alone. |\n| W08-IR-005 | Retained supernatant water inference uses literal transcription method | Use CURATED_INTERPRETATION for the water-retention inference with explicit rationale and unmeasured retained-salt limits, or omit the derived has-part list until supported. Preserve all charge recipe facts in provenance and do not convert them into retained concentrations. Ground the water component if retained. |\n| W08-IR-006 | Component completeness does not consistently follow source-relative enum meanings | For each precisely identified assertion, distinguish explicit omitted constituents (PARTIAL), source-unestablished closure (UNKNOWN), and complete source enumeration (COMPLETE). Missing concentration, final volume, titrant quantity or molecular grounding alone does not determine membership completeness. Preserve the bounded stock and all unknowns. |\n| W08-IR-007 | Nalidixic-acid occurrence applicability lacks readable primary support | Bind a readable exact NBRC1314 primary capture or record that occurrence as source-held/unverified while retaining the independently supported acid candidate. Keep the successful1301 capture separate from failed1314/M2023 requests; do not claim an unread source was inspected. |\n| W08-IR-009 | Trypticase soy broth mapping grade overstates primary-label match | Change mapping_quality to SYNONYM_MATCH with a truthful history event and preserve correct identifier, canonical ontology label, mixture components and own-identifier exact SSSOM semantics. |\n| W08-IR-011 | Allen phosphate remaining requirement repeats a resolved dose distinction | Narrow case63 remaining requirement to unusual seven-water supplied-form confirmation or correction, and state the source-resolved5ml dose transcription separately. Preserve the chemical hold and frozen input. |\n| W08-IR-012 | L-asparagine monohydrate retains parent treatment despite exact NCIT evidence | Adjudicate NCIT:C87433 against its exact L-hydrate synonym and CAS5794-13-8 under section3 exact-term priority, updating primary identifier and mapping grade where supported. Preserve the CAS/source alias/history and distinguish DL hydrate. Separately clarify or verify stereochemical SMILES scope without inventing a structure or calling an unspecified SMILES a racemate. |\n\nThe completeness correction requires source-specific judgment. The native enum means COMPLETE only when the cited source establishes the retained constituent list; PARTIAL means the source identifies omitted constituents; UNKNOWN means closure is not established. Missing amount, final volume, hydration, stereochemistry or a catalog CURIE is not an omitted constituent.\n\nThe external final reviewer identified29 unsupported PARTIAL assertions among34, excluding five rather than rewriting them all. Independent coordinator source-table readback refines five of those29: the two PCR-S11 phosphate stock recipes, Allen phosphate stock, MediaDive552 “5.0g in100ml H2O” preparation, and JCM941 Solution C explicitly enumerate their two retained inputs. COMPLETE is recommended for that literal input-list scope, preserving all chemical-form and physical-composition uncertainty; the other24 uncertain lists warrant UNKNOWN. The five exclusions remain separately assessed (YPS omitted literal inputs; PCR buffer and JCM628/629 pH/CO2 treatment). Do not invent titrants, carriers or gas quantities. The exact34-row table and original source hashes are in `completeness-adjudication-refinement.json` and `complete-list-primary-proof.json`.\n\nFor JCM403, actual primary formulation separates the six-input vitamin solution from the5mg B12/100ml distilled-water stock. Six provider-free pointer aliases resolve through native policy despite citation provenance. Qualify the preferred preparation names and preserve pointers as non-resolving provenance; registry promotion of the existing B12 placeholder is separately tracked under1201.\n\nFor NBRC1356 mineral base1, the source instructs use of the supernatant after precipitation. Retained water is an inference, so record CURATED_INTERPRETATION if retained, with UNKNOWN retained composition. Charged salt quantities must not become retained concentrations.\n\nFor nalidixic acid, independently readable NBRC1301 supports the acid/source claim; exact NBRC1314/TOGOM2023 applicability remains unavailable. Obtain readable primary evidence or hold only that occurrence. Source retrieval failure neither rejects the supported acid identity nor justifies claiming the failed source was read.\n\nFor L-asparagine monohydrate, the archived official NCIT:C87433 response (`54c71948e42aaa1995f271abc3cf70e4ccb9c7b6ae304fa1eff8d754a7af4dae`) records CAS5794-13-8, formula C4H8N2O3.H2O and exact synonym “L-Asparagine Monohydrate”. Under MAPPING_SEMANTICS§3, evaluate that exact target before retaining CAS-primary/NARROW_MATCH. Preserve the distinct DL hydrate. The non-isomeric SMILES does not itself assert racemization; do not invent a replacement structure. Any primary-ID change requires dependent-reference accounting and fresh content-bound review.\n\nOther small corrections retain sound chemistry: Trypticase soy broth is an exact synonym of the ontology’s preferred “tryptic soy broth”, so its actual lookup grade is SYNONYM_MATCH; the Allen stock dose is already resolved as5ml of a6g/L source stock, while the unusual seven-water supplied formula remains held.\n\nExact non-bulk targets follow. The29 completeness targets are fully listed in the attached action matrix rather than authorizing edits to every PARTIAL record.\n\n**JCM403 numbered pointers remain global resolving labels**\n\n| Proposal / owner | Exact payload SHA256 |\n|---|---|\n| `vm-prop-jcm403-b12` / `data/ingredients/unmapped/Vitamin_B12_Solution_See_Medium_No_403.yaml` | `94956716b71347bd431921e64a06ddd2593d6e7bc50e748e1be3c3cc78bb73a8` |\n| `vm-prop-jcm403-vitamin` / `data/ingredients/mapped/Vitamin_Solution_See_Medium_No_403.yaml` | `a86933326bed8cdf623c0a8dc41622bd6e3bc54243addf1610be4f884dda1db5` |\n\n**Retained supernatant water inference uses literal transcription method**\n\n| Proposal / owner | Exact payload SHA256 |\n|---|---|\n| `vm1201-prop-nbrc1356-mineral-base1` / `data/ingredients/mapped/NBRC1356_Mineral_Base1_Supernatant.yaml` | `0aa699c355adee9161a6778c5ee892e07d5f85b2cf90494dea6b31f9072f0256` |\n\n**Nalidixic-acid occurrence applicability lacks readable primary support**\n\n| Proposal / owner | Exact payload SHA256 |\n|---|---|\n| `op-nalidixic-new` / `data/ingredients/mapped/Nalidixic_Acid.yaml` | `ca0acf4743b49628c9a2086a412abbc2c82840367a40251061e8b43608b50d72` |\n\n**Trypticase soy broth mapping grade overstates primary-label match**\n\n| Proposal / owner | Exact payload SHA256 |\n|---|---|\n| `op1201-trypticase-soy-broth` / `data/ingredients/unmapped/Trypticase_Soy_Broth.yaml` | `cbc788c1a9ce7cea22a92b4360bad7c113f0e99c2cf5a1ecbbd3a0e79f99aa6c` |\n\n**L-asparagine monohydrate retains parent treatment despite exact NCIT evidence**\n\n| Proposal / owner | Exact payload SHA256 |\n|---|---|\n| `op-l-asn-hydrate-alias` / `data/ingredients/mapped/L-Asparagine_Monohydrate.yaml` | `07d8b55d6773f4e16ad57e18e5ed72a4438bdeb207cdfb435da84cc2cb62e20a` |\n\n\nExact affected `/component_assertion/completeness` entries (all currently PARTIAL):\n\n| Proposal | Source-relative recommendation |\n|---|---|\n| `if1201-new-dsmz_trace_2n_naoh` | UNKNOWN |\n| `if1201-new-jcm_1130_10n_naoh` | UNKNOWN |\n| `if1201-new-atcc_nitrosolobus_6n_naoh` | UNKNOWN |\n| `if1201-new-pcr_s11_01n_naoh` | UNKNOWN |\n| `if1201-new-dsmz_104_8n_naoh` | UNKNOWN |\n| `if1201-new-jcm_221_02n_naoh` | UNKNOWN |\n| `if1201-new-wolin_mineral_05n_naoh` | UNKNOWN |\n| `if1201-new-atcc_1340_naoh_4g_100ml` | UNKNOWN |\n| `if1201-new-jcm_574_1m_kh2po4` | UNKNOWN |\n| `if1201-new-jcm_574_1m_k2hpo4` | UNKNOWN |\n| `if1201-new-pbbm_1m_na2hpo4` | UNKNOWN |\n| `if1201-new-pcr_s11_50mm_nah2po4` | COMPLETE for the explicit literal two-input list |\n| `if1201-new-pcr_s11_50mm_na2hpo4` | COMPLETE for the explicit literal two-input list |\n| `if1201-new-allen_m2231_phosphate_stock` | COMPLETE for the explicit literal two-input list |\n| `if1201-new-jcm_908_sulfide_10pct_wv` | UNKNOWN |\n| `if1201-new-jcm_713_sulfide_24pct` | UNKNOWN |\n| `if1201-new-atcc_1943_sulfide_25pct` | UNKNOWN |\n| `if1201-new-atcc_1942_sulfide_5pct` | UNKNOWN |\n| `if1201-new-mediadive_552_thiosulfate_5g_100ml` | COMPLETE for the explicit literal two-input list |\n| `if1201-new-jcm_941_solution_c` | COMPLETE for the explicit literal two-input list |\n| `op1201-r2a-medium` | UNKNOWN |\n| `op1201-amphotericin-stock` | UNKNOWN |\n| `vm1201-prop-nbrc1356-mineral-base1` | UNKNOWN |\n| `vm1201-prop-jcm515-b12-partial` | UNKNOWN |\n| `vm1201-prop-jcm647-b12-partial` | UNKNOWN |\n| `vm1201-prop-jcm1354-b12-partial` | UNKNOWN |\n| `vm1201-prop-atcc2883-b12-partial` | UNKNOWN |\n| `vm1201-prop-togo125-k1-partial` | UNKNOWN |\n| `vm1201-prop-dsm1245-k1-partial` | UNKNOWN |\n\nRequired follow-up: preserve beforeimages and historical/source evidence; make bounded successor proposals and exact case/evidence backlinks; bind a new complete manifest; obtain independent review of the final scientific payloads and native schema/partonomy/lineage checks. No maintained-data application, source-hold release, role/export approval, owner merge or cross-shard winner selection is authorized by this issue.\n\nDeduplication context: all-state title/body inventory and targeted current comments were read. Generic pedigree in718/1173 and the worker06-specific1217 findings do not already track these W08 payload corrections. The coordinator performs final live deduplication before filing.\n","number":1227,"state":"open","title":"Correct Worker08 source scope, completeness, identity and provenance findings","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1227"},{"body":"The frozen unmatched queue contains the raw label `Na` in six CultureMech recipe occurrences. Every occurrence is tagged `extracted_from_notes`; each complete recipe note names `pH buffer: Na-sesquicarbonate`. These are truncated source tokens, not evidence for elemental sodium or sodium-ion ingredient identities.\n\nThe current upstream parser is still reproducible at CultureMech `199cd9191881e8e929cd95ea0ed13aabe388e58c`: [extract_ph_buffers](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/CultureMech/blob/199cd9191881e8e929cd95ea0ed13aabe388e58c/scripts/cleanup_recipe_ingredients.py#L157) uses a character class that stops at the hyphen. Running that exact function from the live GitHub source capture against copies of all six frozen recipe notes emits only `Na`. Its Git blob is `6b19e990d36f920145f1fb802ee69425f9e570ba`. No source file was changed during this read-only reproduction.\n\nAffected frozen source files:\n\n- `data/normalized_yaml/bacterial/KOMODO_1063_MICROCELLA_ALKALIPHILA_medium.yaml`\n- `data/normalized_yaml/bacterial/medium_220_modified_for_dsm_13870.yaml`\n- `data/normalized_yaml/bacterial/medium_372_modified_for_dsm_18494.yaml`\n- `data/normalized_yaml/bacterial/medium_372_modified_for_dsm_18495.yaml`\n- `data/normalized_yaml/bacterial/medium_830_modified_for_dsm_21670.yaml`\n- `data/normalized_yaml/bacterial/medium_86_modified_for_dsm_19627.yaml`\n\nWorker08 case `CM_NAME:6987456ddf2caf9d7981c8970484d0b2873d088bd56eaf9c50dcebfaf50070ab` retains all six occurrence IDs and classifies the token as malformed source extraction, with no MIM owner proposal. Durable source hashes and the reproduction are under `reports/all_unmatched_20261107/worker-08/review-history/na-extractor-reproduction.json` on `review/all-unmatched-w08-source-review-20261107` (publication pending).\n\nFollow-up: repair the upstream note extractor so hyphenated buffer names retain their complete source scope, review the six affected occurrences against the original KOMODO/DSMZ formulation, and regenerate the normalized source rows in CultureMech's own authorized workflow. Preserve original labels and provenance; do not automatically turn the bare buffer name into a particular stock composition. MIM should keep this source token unresolved as an ingredient until the corrected source is admitted.\n\nAcceptance: a regression invokes the actual extractor on these source notes and cannot emit an elemental `Na` ingredient; all six occurrence corrections are accounted for, and no alias or identity for sodium metal/ion is introduced by this repair. Native source and downstream mapping gates must run in the eventual integration task.\n\nDeduplication checked live all-state searches for `sesquicarbonate` and `extract_ph_buffers` in MIM and `sesquicarbonate`/`pH buffer` in CultureMech. CultureMech #273 concerns a different preparation-sentence artifact, #299 malformed NBRC tables, and #408 volume-to-mass units. This issue tracks the exact downstream source-token hold and the parser cause, not those other repairs. This worker cannot edit the read-only CultureMech checkout or rebuild downstream data.\n","number":1172,"state":"open","title":"Preserve the source hold for Na truncated from Na-sesquicarbonate buffer notes","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1172"},{"body":"Four concentration-qualified labels resolve uniquely to pure-compound owners in the frozen worker08 label index. The same owner bytes remain on live `main`, checked through the GitHub contents API on 2026-11-08 UTC.\n\n| Owner | Resolving labels | Frozen owner SHA256 |\n|---|---|---|\n| `data/ingredients/mapped/Kh2po4.yaml` (`CHEBI:63036`) | `KH2PO4 (0.4 g/l)`, `KH2PO4 (7% w/v)` | `e721979cb20e8dcac144900643442d9d9e1f01e4d69b2073de8266120419f1c4` |\n| `data/ingredients/mapped/Na2s_X_9_H2o.yaml` (`CHEBI:76209`) | `Na2S x 9 H2O (3% w/v)`, `Na2S x 9 H2O(24% w/v)` | `e849eb5c8d5a8d977c572ee249927b7fb71b40506e23ed6fb4c30d089b5c8219` |\n\nThe phosphate labels are `RAW_TEXT`; the sulfide labels are `HYDRATE_FORM`. All four appear as `synonym`, `MAPPED`, `unique` rows targeting the pure compound in [the exact frozen label index](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/blob/7efd3f8b98f9e822431dcb510c2c242a2569cfc7/docs/data/label_index.csv). This is a direct index observation, not a claim that a new downstream rebuild was performed.\n\n[ChEBI:63036](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/api/ontologies/chebi/terms?obo_id=CHEBI:63036) denotes potassium dihydrogen phosphate and [ChEBI:76209](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/api/ontologies/chebi/terms?obo_id=CHEBI:76209) denotes sodium sulfide nonahydrate. A concentration-qualified preparation or receiving-dose instruction is not an exact global synonym for either pure substance. Missing stock carrier or percentage-basis evidence must remain explicit; neither quantity nor solvent should be invented.\n\nWorker08's authorship-separated issue1201 review identified this as IF1201-R2. Isolated proposals `if1201-edit-kh2po4-alias-scope` and `if1201-edit-nonahydrate-stock-aliases` retain the exact labels and provenance as `REJECTED_LABEL`, preserving identity, roles, counts and source holds. The same phosphate proposal also rejects the orphan `(dibasic)` owner token, but that bare token was not found as an exact row in the frozen label index, so no consumer-resolution claim is made for it. Maintained data are unchanged.\n\nIntegration follow-up:\n\n- Reconcile these exact preimages with other campaign proposals and apply the separately reviewed owner deltas through the guarded writer.\n- Synchronize maintained surfaces and regenerate/check the label index and SSSOM through the coordinator's authorized integration workflow.\n- Verify these four full labels no longer resolve globally to the pure owners; retain source-specific stock and receiving-dose context.\n- Preserve all existing source/role/export holds. This finding does not approve a stock formulation, source membership, owner-wide payload or release.\n\nDeduplication: read #811, #868, #758, #464, #899 and #901 context, and searched all issue states for `sulfide`, `Kh2po4`, `dibasic` and the exact concentration-qualified labels. The earlier calcium-sulfate/potassium-phosphate issue and the generic phosphate-label issue are different defects. Tris stock/nitrilotriacetate alias work is already tracked in #1157 and is intentionally excluded here. The source-review completion work remains under #1201.\n\nExact live-main hashes, frozen-index rows, search results and immutable proposal/review bindings are retained in `reports/all_unmatched_20261107/worker-08/successors/issue-1201-v1/`. No PR, merge, rebuild or publication was performed by this finding.\n","number":1215,"state":"open","title":"Concentration-qualified KH2PO4 and sulfide labels resolve to pure-compound owners","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1215"},{"body":"Worker-02 continuation of #1201. These are isolated review proposals against the campaign's unchanged maintained owner bytes, not applied data changes. All artifact paths are repository relative. The published predecessor at `58f885fef05f8ff6e8b9b56fcb2479fef23b55c8` remains unchanged.\n\nThe Type III porcine-stomach mucin owner is classified `SINGLE_INGREDIENT` because an automatic rule treated a CAS primary as a defined chemical. Its own curation history records that rule. The primary material evidence supports a partially purified biological preparation, so the isolated proposal changes `ingredient_type` to `UNDEFINED_MIXTURE` and appends the evidence/history.\n\nDSMZ 1580 specifies 2 g of Type III porcine-stomach mucin. Sigma's M1778 documentation independently describes this named type as partially purified powder with a range of bound sialic acid. This supports the material classification without establishing that the DSMZ recipe purchased Sigma M1778. The supplier, complete composition, Type II/Type III shared-CAS relationship and grade identity remain unasserted or held. The CAS, preferred name, original aliases and occurrence statistics are preserved.\n\n- Proposal `c2-proposal-mucin-type-iii-material`. Current campaign-baseline owner: `data/ingredients/mapped/Mucin_From_Porcine_Stomach_Type_III.yaml` (SHA256 `858d6f80087acfd16f225cb2f20bb60c6573594a53f79691bb5fc3e4b319a440`).\n- Exact proposed payload: `reports/all_unmatched_20261107/worker-02/continuation-1201-v1/c/proposed-owners/c2-proposal-mucin-type-iii-material.yaml` (SHA256 `0bdb3709c3791ab4e5031a1b63b8bfe599a8737833de718fa5378b579d1e965c`).\n\n- `KG_CASE:71286bc360cc71389bb0470c1f95ffb80dd5e44c71f6fbc6f5fb4ac7fda67aff`; source `mediadive.ingredient:1433`; raw source label `Mucin from porcine stomach Type III`.\n\n- `c2-dsmz-1580`: [https://www.dsmz.de/microorganisms/medium/pdf/DSMZ_Medium1580.pdf](https://www.dsmz.de/microorganisms/medium/pdf/DSMZ_Medium1580.pdf); accessed `2026-11-08T05:55:52.265085+00:00`; `reports/all_unmatched_20261107/worker-02/continuation-1201-v1/c/captures/dsmz-1580.pdf` (SHA256 `66b5b51686017f2cc5def1a87553ca6bddbcf9e2390f079c14c5a704ff01344a`).\n- `c-sigma-mucin`: [https://www.sigmaaldrich.com/DM/en/product/sigma/m1778](https://www.sigmaaldrich.com/DM/en/product/sigma/m1778); accessed `2026-11-08T05:09:34.465Z`; `reports/all_unmatched_20261107/worker-02/c/captures/sigma-mucin.json` (SHA256 `dd57bb25c9d8f7e2f1fcd3b6eb470525e6d5728e1d02c5a9966fc7b513094f00`).\n\nIndependent reviewer `/root/research_a` approved these exact bounded payloads in `reports/all_unmatched_20261107/worker-02/continuation-1201-v1/a/review-c/independent-review.json` (SHA256 `37577be2e1881e5e32fb430f68ffd15a7dedc86994b135c9bd5764f3b3116a71`). This is not a whole-owner, merge or publication approval.\n\nThe inherited full-row hold about the bound-sialic-acid source text in the synonym channel is retained. Classification repair alone does not approve that alias or the owner for export. #478 is the broader ingredient-type schema-axis discussion; this is a concrete material misclassification that can be corrected within the current schema, without claiming that the schema discussion is resolved.\n\nBefore applying any accepted delta, compare the then-current owner bytes and reconcile concurrent proposals. Preserve every raw label, original source reference, existing REJECTED_LABEL, WITHHOLD, source-quantity and downstream hold. Use guarded curation with history and the required maintained-surface/SSSOM synchronization and native validation. This draft requests no merge, downstream rebuild, automatic owner release or closure of broader issues.\n\n<!-- mim-review-round: worker02-continuation1201-v1 -->\n<!-- mim-review-bundle: new-mucin-type-iii -->\n<!-- mim-fingerprints: 61941af4e226873e412ddd6fe68a46057a30ff36329f14672e2b9e4e07706787 -->\n","number":1218,"state":"open","title":"Classify Type III porcine mucin as a biological mixture rather than a single compound","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1218"},{"body":"Observed during the full current-owner Step 7d review for #901 at main `467aa319a06ef992035f509f66fe251082b44ae1`. These are proposed corrections or explicit deferrals before source writes; structural validation is not scientific approval. Exact owner preimages, primary-source capture hashes and full rubrics are prepared under `reports/step7d_mapping_901_20261103/` for the implementation PR.\n\nThese **13 owners are dropped in full** from Step 7d. Each needs a source-material, registry-scope or ontology decision before even a relation-only update is safe. Source records, chemistry, memberships and mapping holds remain unchanged.\n\n| Owner | Observed conflict and primary evidence | Options to resolve | Recommendation |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| [`A-Ketoglutaric_Acid_Disodium_Salt_Hydrate`](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/blob/467aa319a06ef992035f509f66fe251082b44ae1/data/ingredients/mapped/A-Ketoglutaric_Acid_Disodium_Salt_Hydrate.yaml) | Sigma K3752 uses variable-water hydrate wording and CAS 305-72-6, also used for the dry disodium compound. [supplier-K3752](https://www.sigmaaldrich.com/US/en/product/sial/k3752); [sigma-sid-K3752](https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/rest/pug/substance/sid/329817140/JSON); [pubchem-cas-305-72-6](https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/rest/pug/compound/name/305-72-6/property/Title,MolecularFormula,MolecularWeight,InChI,InChIKey,CanonicalSMILES/JSON) | Decide whether the source CAS is a defensible identity for the complete commercial hydrate, or whether a distinct local supplied-form owner is required. | Obtain the source specification and record the CAS-versus-supplied-form ruling; retain this owner unchanged meanwhile. |\n| [`Adenine_Hydrochloride_Hydrate`](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/blob/467aa319a06ef992035f509f66fe251082b44ae1/data/ingredients/mapped/Adenine_Hydrochloride_Hydrate.yaml) | A8751 specifies C5H5N5·HCl·xH2O, while CAS 2922-28-3/PubChem 76219 denotes water-free hydrochloride. [supplier-A8751](https://www.sigmaaldrich.com/US/en/product/sigma/a8751); [sigma-deposit-A8751](https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/rest/pug/substance/sourceid/Sigma-Aldrich/A8751_SIGMA/JSON); [pubchem-cas-2922-28-3](https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/rest/pug/compound/name/2922-28-3/property/Title,MolecularFormula,MolecularWeight,InChI,InChIKey,CanonicalSMILES/JSON) | Establish whether the CAS concept includes the commercial hydrate, or retain a separately identified hydrate material. | Do not mint or promote by inference; obtain the original specification and make the owner-boundary decision. |\n| [`Bathophenanthrolinedisulfonic_Acid_Disodium_Salt_Hydrate`](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/blob/467aa319a06ef992035f509f66fe251082b44ae1/data/ingredients/mapped/Bathophenanthrolinedisulfonic_Acid_Disodium_Salt_Hydrate.yaml) | Historical catalogue 146617 is a trihydrate, but candidate CHEBI:234116 places sulfonates at phenanthroline 3,8, whereas current CHEBI:78157 represents phenyl 4′,4″ positions. [supplier-146617](https://www.sigmaaldrich.com/US/en/product/aldrich/146617); [chebi-78157](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/backend/api/public/compound/78157/); [chebi-232316](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/backend/api/public/compound/232316/); [CHEBI:234116 candidate](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/backend/api/public/compound/234116/) | Verify the actual supplied positional isomer, then select its form-specific term or distinct registry identity; alternatively document that the catalogue/label association is wrong. | Resolve positional structure from the original specification. Label and formula agreement alone cannot choose the candidate. |\n| [`Ceftazidime_Hydrate`](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/blob/467aa319a06ef992035f509f66fe251082b44ae1/data/ingredients/mapped/Ceftazidime_Hydrate.yaml) | Historical C3809 is a stabilized ceftazidime hydrate preparation with sodium carbonate. [sigma-deposit-C3809](https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/rest/pug/substance/sourceid/Sigma-Aldrich/C3809_SIGMA/JSON); [pubchem-cas-120618-65-7](https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/rest/pug/compound/name/120618-65-7/property/Title,MolecularFormula,MolecularWeight,InChI,InChIKey,CanonicalSMILES/JSON); [chebi-3508](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/backend/api/public/compound/3508/) | Decide whether this owner denotes the active hydrate or the complete stabilized product; the latter needs preparation/component representation based on source evidence. | Obtain the historical product composition and retain the whole owner unchanged; do not guess hydration or remove carbonate from the product concept. |\n| [`CrKSO42_X_12_H2O`](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/blob/467aa319a06ef992035f509f66fe251082b44ae1/data/ingredients/mapped/CrKSO42_X_12_H2O.yaml) | CAS 7788-99-0 supports potassium chromium sulfate dodecahydrate. Current CHEBI:53471 chromium(III) sulfate omits potassium and has different sulfate stoichiometry. [pubchem-cas-7788-99-0](https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/rest/pug/compound/name/7788-99-0/property/Title,MolecularFormula,MolecularWeight,InChI,InChIKey,CanonicalSMILES/JSON); [chebi-53471](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/backend/api/public/compound/53471/) | Identify an exact whole double-salt term or justify the appropriate whole-material parent relationship; a chromium sulfate-only hydrate relation is insufficient. | Keep the existing local owner and hold pending an explicit double-salt grounding decision; do not invent component assertions. |\n| [`DL-Isocitric_Acid_Trisodium_Salt_Hydrate`](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/blob/467aa319a06ef992035f509f66fe251082b44ae1/data/ingredients/mapped/DL-Isocitric_Acid_Trisodium_Salt_Hydrate.yaml) | Sigma I1252 is a variable-water DL-isocitrate trisodium hydrate. CHEBI:172950 and CAS 1637-73-6 describe a water-free trisodium representation despite the acid-like label. [web:I1252](https://www.sigmaaldrich.com/US/en/product/sigma/i1252); [v1:29-pubchem-cas](https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/rest/pug/compound/name/1637-73-6/property/MolecularFormula,MolecularWeight,InChI,InChIKey,IUPACName/JSON); [v1:29-chebi-parent](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/backend/api/public/compound/172950/) | Determine whether the registry/ontology concept genuinely includes the supplied hydrate or only its dry parent. | Preserve the local hydrate owner; do not promote merely because the supplier repeats the same CAS. |\n| [`Fosmidomycin_Sodium_Salt_Hydrate`](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/blob/467aa319a06ef992035f509f66fe251082b44ae1/data/ingredients/mapped/Fosmidomycin_Sodium_Salt_Hydrate.yaml) | Catalogue CAS 66508-37-0 comes with FW 183.1. NCIT:C223823 defines sodium fosmidomycin at 205.08; supplier F8682 instead uses variable sodium/water, CAS 66508-53-0 and free-acid-basis 183.10. [web:F8682](https://www.sigmaaldrich.com/US/en/product/sigma/f8682); [v1:33-pubchem-cas](https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/rest/pug/compound/name/66508-37-0/property/MolecularFormula,MolecularWeight,InChI,InChIKey,IUPACName/JSON); [prior:ncit-C223823-fosmidomycin-sodium](https://api-evsrest.nci.nih.gov/api/v1/concept/ncit/C223823?include=full) | Recover the actual supplier/material to distinguish a fixed monosodium compound from a variable sodium hydrate; correct a source transcription only with evidence. | Do not assign F8682 to a catalogue row with no supplier or promote to NCIt solely by CAS overlap. |\n| [`Gold_Iii_Chloride_Hydrate`](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/blob/467aa319a06ef992035f509f66fe251082b44ae1/data/ingredients/mapped/Gold_Iii_Chloride_Hydrate.yaml) | Source CAS 27988-77-8 is associated with HAuCl4·aq by supplier 254169; current CHEBI:30076 is AuCl3. [web:gold254169](https://www.sigmaaldrich.com/US/en/product/aldrich/254169); [v1:37-chebi-parent](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/backend/api/public/compound/30076/) | Resolve the source as chloroauric acid/tetrachloroaurate hydrate versus gold trichloride hydrate, then choose the whole-material identity and parent. | Keep the owner unchanged; do not substitute the tetrachloroaurate anion or infer a water count from a display structure. |\n| [`Neomycin_Trisulfate_Salt_Hydrate`](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/blob/467aa319a06ef992035f509f66fe251082b44ae1/data/ingredients/mapped/Neomycin_Trisulfate_Salt_Hydrate.yaml) | Catalogue N1142 currently identifies neomycin 10 mg/mL in 0.9% NaCl, conflicting with the archived trisulfate hydrate name. [supplier-neomycin](https://www.sigmaaldrich.com/US/en/product/sigma/n1142); [Neomycin_Trisulfate_Salt_Hydrate--pubchem-cas](https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/rest/pug/compound/name/1405-10-3/JSON); [Neomycin_Trisulfate_Salt_Hydrate--chebi-parent](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/backend/api/public/compound/31635/) | Recover the historical N1142 specification and decide active substance versus complete saline formulation. | Retain the owner unchanged; do not equate a solution, sulfate mixture and hydrate powder. |\n| [`Potassium_Tellurite_Hydrate`](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/blob/467aa319a06ef992035f509f66fe251082b44ae1/data/ingredients/mapped/Potassium_Tellurite_Hydrate.yaml) | Archive P0677/CAS 123333-66-4 says hydrate; current P0677 lot MKCQ8564 gives CAS 7790-58-1, K2O3Te and mass 253.79. [Potassium_Tellurite_Hydrate--pubchem-cas](https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/rest/pug/compound/name/123333-66-4/JSON); [supplier-tellurite-coa](https://cloud-qa.sial.com/certificates/COFA/P0/P0677/P0677-BULK________MKCQ8564__.pdf); [Potassium_Tellurite_Hydrate--chebi-parent](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/backend/api/public/compound/75248/) | Establish whether the historical catalogue denoted a different product/form or contains an incorrect CAS/label. | Use original source material evidence; do not silently adopt the current dry-salt CAS. |\n| [`Sodium_Hypophosphite_Monohydrate`](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/blob/467aa319a06ef992035f509f66fe251082b44ae1/data/ingredients/mapped/Sodium_Hypophosphite_Monohydrate.yaml) | Primary S5012 certificate supports NaH2PO2·H2O and 105.99 g/mol, while current CHEBI:234148 and derived structures omit the two phosphorus-bound hydrogens. [sigma-hypophosphite-coa](https://www.sigmaaldrich.cn/certificates/Graphics/COfAInfo/fluka/pdf/PDF690391.pdf); [Sodium_Hypophosphite_Monohydrate--pubchem-cas](https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/rest/pug/compound/name/10039-56-2/JSON); [Sodium_Hypophosphite_Monohydrate--chebi-parent](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/backend/api/public/compound/234148/) | Select a chemically defensible parent/exact term and resolve the ontology structure discrepancy; otherwise retain a registry identity with an explicitly justified broader parent. | Do not approve the present parent or import the erroneous PubChem hydrate formula. Request a source/ontology ruling before any predicate change. |\n| [`Terpene_Hydrate`](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/blob/467aa319a06ef992035f509f66fe251082b44ae1/data/ingredients/mapped/Terpene_Hydrate.yaml) | Source CAS 2451-01-6 is associated with terpin hydrate; CHEBI:751529/NCIT:C219387 names and unstereospecified structures need reconciliation with GSRS S3V868548T cis-relative scope. [prior-primary-gsrs-terpin-hydrate-full](https://gsrs.ncats.nih.gov/api/v1/substances/S3V868548T?view=full); [Terpene_Hydrate--pubchem-cas](https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/rest/pug/compound/name/2451-01-6/JSON); [prior-primary-ncit-C219387-terpin-hydrate](https://api-evsrest.nci.nih.gov/api/v1/concept/ncit/C219387?include=full) | Determine whether the exact concept denotes the supplied cis material, a broader stereochemical class, or a differently scoped substance. | Retain the current CAS owner and hold; do not select the exact ontology identity or rename the material until the cis scope is settled. |\n| [`Vancomycin_Hydrochloride_Hydrate`](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/blob/467aa319a06ef992035f509f66fe251082b44ae1/data/ingredients/mapped/Vancomycin_Hydrochloride_Hydrate.yaml) | Archive lists Sigma and MP:195545-250MG; inspected MP certificate is catalogue 195540, lot S4331, with dry hydrochloride formula and 1.3% water. [Vancomycin_Hydrochloride_Hydrate--pubchem-cas](https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/rest/pug/compound/name/123409-00-7/JSON); [supplier-vancomycin](https://www.mpbio.com/media/document/infor/S/4/3/3/1/S4331.pdf); [Vancomycin_Hydrochloride_Hydrate--chebi-parent](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/backend/api/public/compound/9932/) | Recover the exact historical SKU/specification and decide the commercial material/hydrate scope. | Do not silently correct the SKU, assign the inspected lot or import the implausible CAS-resolved PubChem structure. |\n\nScope and deduplication: the 13 unresolved raw formula-labelled hydrates are tracked on #459, not duplicated here. G6501 remains on #1118; the coupled sodium phosphate correction stays on #758/#740; the potassium trihydrate owner collision belongs with existing #656. Closed #326 recorded a limited inherited-formula repair with intentional exclusions; it does not settle the new source/SKU and ontology boundaries above. Closed #455 concerned a different `potassium tellurate` owner and Te(VI)/Te(IV) conflict, not this P0677 hydrate history. #718 and #901 remain the broader review backlogs.\n\nAcceptance: settle each row explicitly from primary source and exact existing-owner context, document the chosen material boundary and rejected alternatives, then propose a separately reviewed guarded correction. Keep unresolved rows open. No guessed water count, invented catalogue assignment, role approval, automatic promotion or owner merge. This issue does not authorize any edit to these 13 owners in Step 7d.\n","number":1121,"state":"open","title":"Resolve thirteen catalogue and registry identity decisions deferred from Step 7d","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1121"},{"body":"The final campaign intake contains two individually source-bound proposals for the same authentic `data/ingredients/unmapped/Reinforced_Clostridial_Medium.yaml` beforeimage (`7f94183c0d9b070b57c1ffa8beab53817b48791a75278957163619ece7a24c2e`). They cannot be combined as a compatible delta union.\n\nThe baseline is UNMAPPED_0507, a named medium originally imported from MediaDive ingredient1769. Later history explicitly added BD-Difco and two Oxoid aliases to this one owner. The record currently contains no component formula. Its history and both origin scopes must survive reconciliation.\n\n- W03 `b-proposal-cm0149-form`, head `1196d5bc3aaba7563b829d5cbe7e54722fccc35d`, payload `137dd17759a3ed4d80255d3869611c81233c57c5ca61b58b0ff8fad82d71c328`, adds the Oxoid CM0149 supplied form while retaining UNMAPPED and an explicit mixed-owner partition requirement. Case `CM_NAME:2be5d4f231ea346e62be345a8e8b35adbc735ccdced2de73963ce5265c48fd66`, occurrence `CM_OCC:ffcf2b54ee7027b6166807e15a6a72a9bae955a7c8c4576805c5bb8e99196307`, CultureMech:008256, TOGO M1696/NBRC902.\n- W05 `M1193-P-RCM-182`, head `43f7cf6e8681fa617b5d6d3529d94d537ca01f0d`, payload `21ae9f818beef065d14b1562cf7d968dc8441a7edc6dd3c7ea7e7e87d2d26b30`, proposes the BD-Difco product identity `kgmicrobe.ingredient:reinforced_clostridial_medium_difco`, its nine-part formula, and rejection of the two Oxoid aliases for that selected product. Case `KG_CASE:deb11ac97bd19cd33a5b09abfc21a6f25108c238ab3ae43b7037b520466325bd`, MediaDive ingredient1769.\n\nThe primary evidence establishes an actual product/formulation distinction:\n\n- [NBRC902](https://www.nite.go.jp/nbrc/catalogue/NBRCMediumDetailServlet?NO=902) specifies CM0149 Broth (OXOID), 38g, with water and optional additional agar. Its exact brand is not BD-Difco.\n- [Current Oxoid IFU0539](https://assets.thermofisher.com/TFS-Assets/MBD/Instructions/MBD_BT_IFU-0539%20CM0149B%20REINFORCED%20CLOSTRIDIAL%20MEDIUM.pdf), page1 CM0149B/T table, lists yeast extract13g/L and no beef extract, with agar0.5g/L. The separate CM0151 agar table must not be substituted.\n- [Manufacturer Difco/BBL manual, RCM section](https://cdn.media.interlabdist.com.br/uploads/2021/01/Reinforced-Clostridial-Medium.pdf), page1, lists peptone10g/L, beef extract10g/L, yeast extract3g/L and agar0.5g/L. The printed peptone is not a proven named historical Tryptose subproduct.\n- [MediaDive1769](https://mediadive.dsmz.de/ingredients/1769) records three BD-Difco uses, supporting the original owner’s source continuity but not erasing later Oxoid source claims. [DSMZ634](https://www.dsmz.de/microorganisms/medium/pdf/DSMZ_Medium634.pdf) permits multiple suppliers; permission to use alternatives is not whole-product identity evidence.\n\nDo not append the W03 Oxoid supplied form under a Difco-specific identity or assign the Difco formula to the Oxoid occurrence. Conversely, do not discard the supported Difco source case merely to keep one generic owner. Manufacturer-version differences cannot be backdated without evidence.\n\nBounded resolution requires an explicit whole-owner/source partition decision under MAPPING_SEMANTICS §3/§6, including: retain a generic unresolved named-medium owner plus separately deduplicated product identities, or preserve the original MediaDive1769 continuity on the Difco owner and separately represent the Oxoid scope, with explicit handling of every prior alias/source occurrence. These are options, not an implemented owner split or an identifier selection. Check existing records before creating anything. Keep authentic beforeimage, every origin history and source-case backlink; reject incorrect aliases only for the scope that makes them false, not as rejection of genuine source observations.\n\nAcceptance: source/brand/version-bounded records, explicit identity lineage, reviewed memberships and any necessary dependent references, native writer/schema/partonomy/SSSOM gates, and independent review of the final full owner payloads. No automatic worker/hash-order winner, inferred product equivalence, role approval or lost case. TARGET-032 remains blocked; other groups can proceed independently.\n\nLocal exact evidence and copied primary captures: `.temp/all-unmatched-20261107/integration/final-same-target-reconciliation-v1/finding-rcm-v1/`. Live deduplication checked all 1,223 visible issue/PR bodies across 13 complete REST pages, three indexed searches and complete comments on #1193/#1196/#1212. No existing RCM/CM0149 formulation-partition issue was found in those checks; arbitrary comments outside indexed/direct reads were not exhaustively downloaded. No RCM successor has been written or applied.\n","number":1224,"state":"open","title":"Resolve RCM owner scope before combining Difco identity with Oxoid CM0149 provenance","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1224"},{"body":"Four source owners assert a broader descriptor, another molecule's chemistry, or a component class as though it described the supplied material. The worker-10 source review proposes bounded corrections while preserving existing release holds. These findings concern the frozen campaign baseline `e000d66dd26ab053e9b17ce7bf8d3c873c3dde99`; no maintained ingredient was edited.\n\n## Findings and primary evidence\n\n### Levorin descriptor collapse\n\nOwner: `data/ingredients/mapped/Levorin.yaml`. Proposal: `kg-levorin-concept-scope`.\n\nThe owner uses MeSH D002174 as exact Candicidin identity. NLM's preferred Candicidin concept M0003252 explicitly has narrower Levorin concept M0352008; sharing the descriptor does not make those concepts chemically identical. Restore the historical local `kgmicrobe.compound:levorin` identity and retain Candicidin only with `NARROW_MATCH`, published as `skos:broadMatch`. The inherited `SINGLE_INGREDIENT` classification remains unadjudicated against antibiotic-complex scope. This proposal does not establish a specific candicidin molecular component.\n\nPrimary references: [kg-mesh_M0003252](https://id.nlm.nih.gov/mesh/M0003252.json); [kg-mesh_M0352008](https://id.nlm.nih.gov/mesh/M0352008.json).\n\n### Ristocetin B inherited another molecule's structure\n\nOwner: `data/ingredients/mapped/Ristocetin_B.yaml`. Proposal: `kg-ristocetin-b-parent-chemistry`.\n\nThe local B owner carries CHEBI:85129 parent-derived C95H110N8O44, InChI and SMILES. PubChem CID 90478637 identifies ristocetin B as C84H92N8O35, a different molecular composition. Remove the unsupported parent and borrowed structural fields; retain the local B identity with fallback registry mapping until a justified exact ontology/registry selection is available. Do not infer a CAS identity from aggregated synonyms. The already rejected `produces:ristocetin B` trait phrase stays rejected.\n\nPrimary references: [kg-pc_ristocetin_b](https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/rest/pug/compound/cid/90478637/property/InChI,InChIKey,IUPACName,MolecularFormula/JSON); [kg-pc_ristocetin_b_names](https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/rest/pug/compound/cid/90478637/synonyms/JSON); [kg-CHEBI_85129](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/api/ontologies/chebi/terms?iri=http%3A%2F%2Fpurl.obolibrary.org%2Fobo%2FCHEBI_85129).\n\n### Unsuffixed halomicin is an antibiotic mixture\n\nOwner: `data/ingredients/mapped/Halomicin.yaml`. Proposal: `kg-halomicin-complex`.\n\nThe current local compound is classified as a single ingredient. Original patent US3511909A describes halomicin as at least four chromatographic components A, B, C and D. The unsuffixed source supports an undefined mixture with a `kgmicrobe.ingredient` identity, not a chosen component molecule. Preserve the original source provenance and do not invent component proportions or complete structures.\n\nPrimary references: [kg-halomicin_patent](https://patents.google.com/patent/US3511909A/en).\n\n### ISP4 medium is not an inorganic salt\n\nOwner: `data/ingredients/unmapped/Inorganic_Salts-starch_Agar.yaml`. Proposal: `kg-isp4-remove-salt-parent`.\n\nThe named inorganic salts-starch agar owner is grounded to the inorganic-salt class CHEBI:24839. Official JCM medium 58 lists starch, inorganic salts, trace salts, agar and water. Remove that invalid whole-medium parent while retaining the existing UNMAPPED named-medium owner. The bounded correction does not mint a new formulation or assign a component identity to the preparation.\n\nPrimary references: [kg-md1692](https://mediadive.dsmz.de/rest/ingredient/1692); [kg-jcm58](https://www.jcm.riken.jp/cgi-bin/jcm/jcm_grmd?GRMD=58); [kg-CHEBI_24839](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/api/ontologies/chebi/terms?iri=http%3A%2F%2Fpurl.obolibrary.org%2Fobo%2FCHEBI_24839).\n\n## Exact proposal artifacts\n\nAll paths below are relative to `reports/all_unmatched_20261107/worker-10/` on `review/all-unmatched-w10-source-review-20261107`. Source captures include URLs, retrieval times and SHA256 digests in `kg/evidence.json`. `kg/decisions.json` binds each claim to its frozen case. `reviews/root-review-of-kg-v1.json` independently assesses all 52 KG cases and nine bounded proposal deltas.\n\n| Proposal | Baseline owner SHA256 | Proposed payload and SHA256 |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `kg-levorin-concept-scope` | `77cccb8ad35797ec34744abe85b0125c5783466a832ec52b7a3442e51cd441a4` | `kg/proposed/levorin-concept-scope.yaml`; `7490a66ecda86d31016fc8096b10a5b20f5da32803e3bdc19af55bc31da27079` |\n| `kg-ristocetin-b-parent-chemistry` | `9630cba6e27f198278c5aa247dfc10bef5de0a07ea34991777913de887b1e1e6` | `kg/proposed/ristocetin-b-parent-chemistry.yaml`; `31f6897af214e673e8a859c1c2c14bd60e119a3a8c4373ae2266a3197ab4c3de` |\n| `kg-halomicin-complex` | `8c0bad7398d8e43ee0812117f6d368303dc8c6a0ff5a2f098b976b565bf5ac5e` | `kg/proposed/halomicin-complex.yaml`; `61cc2355010ce9a7bea6d2e5534cbaad49ab443147a217346d9f37c3df4db09c` |\n| `kg-isp4-remove-salt-parent` | `76a430265f543685167823888f68629d2cbfc652f6faced54387692517118178` | `kg/proposed/isp4-remove-salt-parent.yaml`; `70c4b138c58230159aa991533c6048a4503761e01853a0a54b7759b96eb76435` |\n\n## Required follow-up and limits\n\nReview the exact current owner bytes before applying the proposed deltas. If they have changed since the frozen baseline, reconcile those changes explicitly. Preserve every existing WITHHOLD, REJECTED_LABEL, source-context and downstream hold. Complete owner/role/publication assessment remains separate from these supported corrections. Structural LinkML validation of the proposal files does not establish scientific completeness.\n\nApply any accepted correction through the native guarded workflow with curation history, synchronized maintained surfaces, appropriate SSSOM updates and required validation. No owner merge, downstream rebuild, blanket alias activation or release approval is requested by this issue.\n\n## Deduplication\n\nThe live all-state issue snapshot and relevant comments did not contain these four owner-specific defects. #312 is a broader historical research-versus-mapping conflict ledger, and #718 is the continuing withheld-claim umbrella. #703 already resolved process/trait synonyms; the ristocetin B finding here concerns borrowed chemistry and does not reopen that completed cleanup.\n\nThe checked GitHub issue snapshots and complete relevant comment payloads are retained under `context/kg-issue-*-live.json`; the all-state snapshot is `context/kg-issues-all-live.json`. The borax component dependency already belongs to #1168 and is excluded here.\n\n<!-- mim-review-round: 20261108T052536Z-worker10-kg-source-review -->\n<!-- mim-review-bundle: kg-owner-identity-defects -->\n<!-- mim-fingerprints: fe1fee7bcc07e14a4f5b8844f107639a850c493503a496958427b78593e3709a,910bcb507fb663855394f924fd161272e30f1b4873d568fcce9e1d13baecfa68,0c2873cbb6dfb24d179ae919e2835e1ec3827a58bc00ee16780d81d885f47e91,6d896cbc3a4a0a45e474ffb328fe94c201c6e9922d7e99ae5c2f69ef077c1c1f -->\n","number":1188,"state":"open","title":"Correct four source-owner identity and parent-chemistry defects: Levorin, ristocetin B, halomicin and ISP4","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1188"},{"body":"Found while reviewing PR #881. #897 is one instance; this is the backlog.\n\n`MAPPING_SEMANTICS.md` §3 step 2 requires the following for a hydrate with no exact ontology term:\n- its own identity (`cas:` or `kgmicrobe.compound:`);\n- a `skos:broadMatch` to the nearest parent;\n- the Rule B1 registry row.\n\nThe #245 ruling rejected `closeMatch` here, and #664 moved four sulfate hydrates off it. Among MAPPED records whose preferred term states a hydrate, counted on #881's head:\n\n| identity | mapping_quality → parent predicate | records |\n|---|---|---:|\n| `cas:` | CLOSE_MATCH → `skos:closeMatch` | 45 (one is `Mgso4h2o`, fixed by #881) |\n| `kgmicrobe.compound:` | CLOSE_MATCH → `skos:closeMatch` | 28 |\n| `cas:` | NARROW_MATCH → `skos:broadMatch` | 12 |\n| `kgmicrobe.compound:` | NARROW_MATCH → `skos:broadMatch` | 7 |\n\nExamples:\n- `cas:` with closeMatch: `Sodium_Deoxycholate_Monohydrate`, `L-Glutamic_Acid_Monopotassium_Salt_Monohydrate`, `Fosmidomycin_Sodium_Salt_Hydrate`;\n- `kgmicrobe.compound:` with closeMatch: `Cocl2_X_4_H2o`, `Na2hpo4_X_3_H2o`, `Fecl2_X_6_H2o`, `Fecl2_X_7_H2o`, `Cu_No32_X_3_H2o`.\n\n**Why it matters:**\n- kg-microbe's consolidator treats `closeMatch` as symmetric (`scripts/consolidate_chemical_mappings.py:1776`, 1829-1873 at 1acb8b61f). It merges the hydrate's label and `other` tokens into the anhydrous parent's record and force-sets the parent's `canonical_name` to the hydrate label; the last row written wins.\n- This comes from reading the code. I did not run kg-microbe or check which of these parents it actually renames.\n\n**Also:**\n- `MAPPING_SEMANTICS.md` (§3 step 2) says 34 hydrate subjects carry the broadMatch pattern. The count above is lower, so the doc or the data has drifted.\n- `Cu_No32_X_3_H2o` keeps a local identity although its own CAS, 10031-43-3 (copper(II) nitrate trihydrate), is on the record. §3 step 2 would make it `cas:10031-43-3`.\n\n**Related:** #696 (BROAD_MATCH publishes closeMatch) and #884 (schema direction of NARROW/BROAD).\n\n**Fix:** a reviewed batch moving these records to NARROW_MATCH, with broadMatch and registry rows through the receipt chain. Check first which of them are SUPPORTED, because changing the bytes cancels approvals (#898).\n\n## History\n\n**How the cohort got here:**\n- #342 (`scripts/regrade_hydrate_to_close_match.py`, in #500 on 2026-08-28) regraded 72 hydrate→anhydrous rows from narrowMatch to closeMatch on purpose. Its reason: narrowMatch claims a subsumption that ChEBI contradicts.\n- That script also took those records out of Rule B1's scope.\n- 72 SSSOM rows still carry `MIM:curator=regrade_hydrate_to_close_match`.\n\n**The later ruling:**\n- The #245 ruling (#735, 2026-09-22) settled the subsumption concern differently. It keeps `skos:broadMatch`, reads it as \"form of\", and forbids projecting it as `subclass_of`.\n- It also records that `closeMatch` was rejected because it carries no direction and drops Rule B1.\n- §3 step 2 has required broadMatch plus the registry row since then.\n- #664 moved four sulfate hydrates back to that shape.\n\n**Decision for maintainers:** these 73 are the #342 cohort the ruling did not migrate, so whether to migrate them is a maintainer decision, not a data error introduced by one PR.\n","number":901,"state":"open","title":"73 hydrate records anchor their parent with closeMatch, which §3 step 2 rejects; kg-microbe folds them into the anhydrous parent","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/901"},{"body":"Independent adversarial review of the approved #914 replacement found raw CultureMech term IDs promoting labels with no MIM name owner into unrelated or insufficiently scoped MIM identities.\n\nConfirmed cases:\n- `MnSO4·xH2O` becomes anhydrous CHEBI:86360/CAS7785-87-7 (17 occurrences), despite existing MIM rejection of variable-hydrate aliases. Source: CultureMech `bacterial/JCM_J1503_AUTOTROPHIC_NITROBACTER_MEDIUM.yaml`; [ChEBI86360](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/CHEBI:86360) denotes the anhydrous salt.\n- `Cyanocobalamin (Vitamin B12)` becomes generic class CHEBI:176843 through `algae/yt75s_v.yaml`, despite the MIM class record explicitly rejecting the cyanocobalamin member name. [ChEBI176843](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/CHEBI:176843) is the class.\n\nTen additional preparation-qualified labels have no MIM name anchor and acquire pure-compound identity through the same fallback:\n- `1 M K2HPO4` → `CHEBI:131527`\n- `1 M KH2PO4` → `CHEBI:63036`\n- `1 M Sodium acetate solution` → `CHEBI:32954`\n- `1 M Tris base (pH 8)` → `CHEBI:9754`\n- `1 N NaOH` → `CHEBI:32145`\n- `10% Glucose solution` → `CHEBI:17234`\n- `25% HCl (7.7 M)` → `CHEBI:17883`\n- `5% L-Cysteine·HCl·H2O solution` → `CHEBI:91248`\n- `5% Na2S·9H2O solution` → `CHEBI:76209`\n- `8% NaHCO3 solution` → `CHEBI:32139`\n\nUnder MAPPING_SEMANTICS §0 these need explicit preparation-scope review, not inferred exact identity. The safe export repair rejects the named raw source ID for that exact label without inventing a replacement or minting a record. The existing four-column rejection ledger currently cannot express a rejection without an asserted replacement MIM ID; its supported contract must be extended narrowly in the producer and local gate. Keep source names/counts, scope rejections by label+ID, preserve a valid explicit MIM name owner, and do not globally blacklist an ontology term. Regenerate with zero unsafe identities on these held pairs, add regressions, rerun native QC and independent review.\n\nThis is a dependency of the approved #914 redo, separate from step5 SSSOM owned-name policy. Existing related source/label issues #811 and #868 remain distinct. Evidence: `output/approved-plan-20261101T031134Z/step1-independent/unified-fallback-findings.json` and `unified-54-identity-owner-check.json`; diagnostic snapshot SHA256 `e5c4bd6b5512606857feded03a45591476ce64626c261315f7ed2bed1f742f8a`. No scientific approval is granted by withholding.\n","number":1025,"state":"closed","title":"Hold unsupported raw-source identity promotions in the refreshed unified snapshot","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1025"},{"body":"The user identified a substance distinction requiring a separate owner-assignment review: **Tryptone is not Casamino acids**, yet the unified row `Tryptone Peptone (Difco 0123-17)` currently inherits the Casamino-acids owner through `FOODON:03315719`.\n\nThis issue records that unresolved assignment. **Do not fix it in #1106.** The one-time two-file unified-snapshot exception for #1106 preserves this inherited owner/identity assignment; it does not approve the assignment scientifically or authorize a record merge or replacement identity.\n\n### Verified current behavior\n\nRead-only inspection and native owner-resolution replay used MIM `2ed2804b9f787e354188b69284097c6af03b3409`, isolated Claw `33be66ab1b993e2a47792c13daa3e4f39635f2cb`, and CultureMech `a74dd489720c9df96cc073ffd11568d407d3c06c`.\n\n- `UNIFIED_INGREDIENT_MAPPING.tsv`, line 1696: ingredient label `Tryptone Peptone (Difco 0123-17)`, 3 occurrences, `mim_id=FOODON:03315719`, `culturemech_term_id=FOODON:03315719`, `cas_rn=65072-00-6`, and a Casamino-acids synonym payload. The CAS is reported as inherited output here, not independently validated for this catalog product.\n- The actual C4 regenerated candidate (SHA256 `1edaba0f0f2d1afb84129a2ba185963a2cdf3e9b982690fdfd610df1fd78cc88`) retains that assignment. Its only change to this row is clearing the stale `kg_microbe_node_id=CHEBI:78020`.\n- The native resolver selects **`data/ingredients/mapped/Casamino_Acids.yaml`**, preferred term `Casamino acids`. The exact catalog label has no entry in MIM's published label index and no name-index answer; the resolver falls back to the `FOODON:03315719` ontology index. That index uses the first loaded owner for a shared identifier.\n- Both `Casamino_Acids.yaml` and `Casein_Peptone.yaml` hold `FOODON:03315719` with `CLOSE_MATCH` mappings to `mammalian milk protein (hydrolyzed)`. The former's existing curation evidence describes acid-hydrolyzed casein; the latter's evidence describes enzymatic/tryptic/pancreatic casein hydrolysate. A shared parent term is not evidence that these source owners or products are interchangeable.\n\nThe three CultureMech source records retain the exact catalog label and local `term.id=FOODON:03315719`:\n\n| CultureMech ID | Normalized source path | Recorded source |\n|---|---|---|\n| `CultureMech:006494` | `data/normalized_yaml/bacterial/for_dsm_16260.yaml` | KOMODO Medium 78b.1; 0.15 g/L |\n| `CultureMech:006495` | `data/normalized_yaml/bacterial/for_dsm_16369.yaml` | KOMODO Medium 78b.2; 0.15 g/L |\n| `CultureMech:006496` | `data/normalized_yaml/bacterial/KOMODO_78b_medium_FOR_TREPONEMA_PARVUM.yaml` | KOMODO Medium 78b; 0.16 g/L |\n\nCultureMech's current `output/ingredient_occurrences.tsv` records all three as `local_fallback`, with the explanation `label not found in pinned MIM index; retained local identity`. Its aggregate groups this label under `Casein peptone`, while the unified MIM fallback selects `Casamino acids`. This is a source-owner question, not a reason to silently choose either record.\n\n### Required evidence-based follow-up\n\n1. Verify the exact Difco `0123-17` product and source-recipe wording against primary manufacturer/catalog and recipe evidence; establish the supplied material and preparation distinction under `MAPPING_SEMANTICS.md` section 3.\n2. Review the CultureMech local annotation, the two distinct MIM source owners sharing the broad FOODON term, and the identifier fallback behavior together. Determine whether this label has an adequately supported existing owner, needs a distinct record/identity, or should remain explicitly unresolved.\n3. Recommend and independently review a bounded correction before applying it. Do not infer an exact identity from the shared FOODON parent, CAS, a similar commercial name, or loader order; do not merge records without the required evidence.\n4. Any future change must preserve the raw catalog label and provenance, use guarded writers, synchronize maintained surfaces, regenerate affected products, and run the required tests/QC. It belongs in a separate change after the present #1106 scope.\n\n### Prior decisions and duplicate check\n\nClosed #414 explicitly identified `Casein peptone` versus `Casamino acids` as distinct substances that must not be merged merely because they share `FOODON:03315719`; its comments point to the existing duplicate-identifier baseline. Closed #504 discusses the broader owner/identifier resolver problem. Closed #263 explains the earlier Casamino correction away from the unrelated heptacosanoate term. These decisions remain intact.\n\nLive searches covered the exact `0123-17` catalog code in issue bodies/comments, `Tryptone`, `Casamino`, and `FOODON:03315719`, followed by the relevant issue comments. No exact catalog-label follow-up was found. This is one dedicated review issue, not a reopening of those broad closed tasks.\n\nEvidence is saved under `output/approved-plan-20261101T031134Z/step7b-exception-20261102/tryptone-issue/`; `source-trace.json` SHA256 is `192dc6d4904cb4f10803ebe9300f0c15e0380bb05107e1675848239c975860a6`. The trace records exact source paths/hashes, both observed rows, the native resolver result, and all three occurrence records. No new external scientific research, provider credits, record edits, or identity decision were used to file this issue.\n","number":1108,"state":"open","title":"Review owner assignment for Tryptone Peptone (Difco 0123-17), currently inherited from Casamino acids","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1108"},{"body":"Adversarial scientific review during #710 found a proposed TYGVS + Glucose correction substituted tryptone for Trypticase and promoted abbreviation-derived components to RECIPE_TRANSCRIPTION using a later Treponema denticola formulation. The original MicrobeDecoder occurrence is Treponema medium, linked to the 1997 species description (https://doi.org/10.1099/00207713-47-1-67).\n\nThe later oral TYGVS formula (https://academic.oup.com/femspd/article/60/3/251/530699) does not establish the exact preparation used for this source organism. Another primary paper explicitly cultures T. medium in Trypticase-TYGVS (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC145376/). The shared medium abbreviation is insufficient to choose the branded peptone or certify the source preparation.\n\nRevert the unsupported peptone substitution and evidence-method promotion. Retain the recovered source organism and conflicting preparation evidence in the audit, and keep all affected component claims open pending the original source recipe. Verify both YAML and per-assertion review dispositions; do not mark the scientific ambiguity resolved merely because this regression is corrected.\n","number":715,"state":"closed","title":"Do not certify TYGVS components from a different organism preparation","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/715"},{"body":"Found in the second review of #863.\n\n`data/ingredients/mapped/Na2hpo4_X_7_H2o.yaml` has the local identity `kgmicrobe.compound:na2hpo4_x_7_h2o`. It was minted on 2026-08-13 under MAPPING_SEMANTICS §3 step 3, which covers \"no exact term and no CAS\". The record now carries `cas_rn: 7782-85-6`, the heptahydrate's own CAS. The x3 and x6 sibling records that shared it in the #334 scramble no longer carry it, and #863's unified snapshot publishes it on the `Na2HPO4·7H2O` row.\n\n§3 step 2 says that when there is no exact term but the substance has its own CAS, `identifier` should be `cas:<its own CAS>`, with a broadMatch to the nearest parent and the Rule B1 registry row. Open semantic-review finding SEM:cb9ec35166086d936f70 already asks for \"exact CAS promotion\" on this record.\n\n**Coupling with #863.** #863 adds a rejection-ledger row keyed (`kgmicrobe.compound:na2hpo4_x_7_h2o`, CHEBI:34683). A promotion to `cas:7782-85-6` must update that row's `mim_id` in the same change. Otherwise claw's `load_mapping_rejections` fails closed with \"rejection disagrees with MIM identity\".\n\n**To do:**\n1. Verify 7782-85-6 against an authoritative source for disodium hydrogen phosphate heptahydrate.\n2. If it is confirmed, promote the record under §3 step 2 through the SSSOM receipt chain, update the ledger row in the same commit, and rebuild the unified snapshot.\n","number":875,"state":"open","title":"Na2HPO4 x 7 H2O carries its own CAS 7782-85-6: decide §3 step 2 promotion (the #863 ledger row must move with it)","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/875"},{"body":"Independent adversarial review reproduced an incomplete completion check in the unpublished deferred-23 #669 writer, before any real mapping publication.\n\nThe frozen operator `promote_deferred_aliases_v1.py` (SHA256 `4c31a61fabda38cbb848bc636d9e42349283f2302ca2694419b546c707ef1a3e`) checks captured file bytes and the completed receipt chain, but its immediate `verify_completed` does not re-enumerate source membership after publication. A private fixture inserts `.late-independent-owner.yaml` after native publication returns and before that check. The source inventory grows from 2,956 to 2,957 files, yet the writer returns `verified-complete` with receipt 26. The real maintained mapping and ingredient files remain untouched.\n\nThe separate CLI verification reruns preparation and would catch this drift. That later check does not justify the immediate completion claim. This is a bounded completion-proof defect, not evidence of a wrong canonical row or a new scientific approval.\n\nRepair the run-owned writer to recheck complete source and relevant generation/absent-input inventories during immediate completion verification, preserving all captured-byte, alias, exact journal and receipt-prefix checks. Add deterministic late-addition/removal/alias controls and verify successful ordinary publication. Re-review before real application. Do not change thresholds, owner selection, the unified snapshot, or existing scientific decisions.\n\nPreserved evidence: `reports/owned_name_deferred_669_20261103/review-findings/completion-inventory-v1/{operator-v1.py,probe-v1.py,result.json,run.json,run.log,archive.json}`. Result SHA256 `e78b5be5d3a731f482ebcbd9218ca63c533598097b979f4c4705e8fa6fa2c581`; independent log SHA256 `b1e2618f4ce8accb2c6ff14032e2a6680b50c2bff3512e57cef97ec69517fb58`.\n\nAll current issue/PR pages were inspected for duplicates. Historical #1037 and #1104 concern different publishers and earlier completed fixes; this new unpublished writer is the repair owner.\n","number":1131,"state":"closed","title":"Recheck source membership in deferred-alias publication completion proof","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1131"},{"body":"Observed during the full current-owner Step 7d review for #901 at main `467aa319a06ef992035f509f66fe251082b44ae1`. These are proposed corrections or explicit deferrals before source writes; structural validation is not scientific approval. Exact owner preimages, primary-source capture hashes and full rubrics are prepared under `reports/step7d_mapping_901_20261103/` for the implementation PR.\n\nEleven active YAML synonym assertions do not name the complete owner substance. Ten are names of a water-free parent; the carbonate token is a preparation instruction. Six were identified in the author proposals and five by independent finding `step7d-independent-001`. All eleven exact `(subject, token)` pairs were compared with the 23 deferred #669 pairs and none overlaps. The 23 deferred pairs remain untouched.\n\n| Owner | Exact token | Type change | Source preserved | Source basis |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| [`D-Fructose_6-phosphate_Disodium_Salt_Hydrate`](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/blob/467aa319a06ef992035f509f66fe251082b44ae1/data/ingredients/mapped/D-Fructose_6-phosphate_Disodium_Salt_Hydrate.yaml) | `disodium;[(2R,3R,4S)-2,3,4,6-tetrahydroxy-5-oxohexyl] phosphate` | `EXACT_SYNONYM` → `REJECTED_LABEL` | `chebi_synonym_review` | Complete owner is hydrated; this systematic token denotes the dry parent. [web:F3627](https://www.sigmaaldrich.com/US/en/product/sigma/f3627); [v1:23-pubchem-cas](https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/rest/pug/compound/name/26177-86-6/property/MolecularFormula,MolecularWeight,InChI,InChIKey,IUPACName/JSON) |\n| [`DL-Glycerol_1-phosphate_Sodium_Salt_Hydrate`](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/blob/467aa319a06ef992035f509f66fe251082b44ae1/data/ingredients/mapped/DL-Glycerol_1-phosphate_Sodium_Salt_Hydrate.yaml) | `2,3-dihydroxypropyl dihydrogen phosphate` | `EXACT_SYNONYM` → `REJECTED_LABEL` | `chebi_synonym_review` | Complete owner is hydrated; this systematic token denotes the dry parent. [web:glycerol61668](https://www.sigmaaldrich.com/US/en/product/sigma/61668); [v1:28-pubchem-cas](https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/rest/pug/compound/name/17603-42-8/property/MolecularFormula,MolecularWeight,InChI,InChIKey,IUPACName/JSON) |\n| [`DL-Glycerol_1-phosphate_Sodium_Salt_Hydrate`](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/blob/467aa319a06ef992035f509f66fe251082b44ae1/data/ingredients/mapped/DL-Glycerol_1-phosphate_Sodium_Salt_Hydrate.yaml) | `glycerol 1-(dihydrogen phosphate)` | `EXACT_SYNONYM` → `REJECTED_LABEL` | `chebi_synonym_review` | Complete owner is hydrated; this systematic token denotes the dry parent. [web:glycerol61668](https://www.sigmaaldrich.com/US/en/product/sigma/61668); [v1:28-pubchem-cas](https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/rest/pug/compound/name/17603-42-8/property/MolecularFormula,MolecularWeight,InChI,InChIKey,IUPACName/JSON) |\n| [`Gly-Gln_Monohydrate`](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/blob/467aa319a06ef992035f509f66fe251082b44ae1/data/ingredients/mapped/Gly-Gln_Monohydrate.yaml) | `glycyl-L-glutamine` | `EXACT_SYNONYM` → `REJECTED_LABEL` | `chebi_synonym_review` | Complete owner is hydrated; this systematic token denotes the dry parent. [v1:35-pubchem-cas](https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/rest/pug/compound/name/172669-64-6/property/MolecularFormula,MolecularWeight,InChI,InChIKey,IUPACName/JSON); [v1:35-chebi-parent](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/backend/api/public/compound/73898/) |\n| [`Sodium_Carbonate_Monohydrate`](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/blob/467aa319a06ef992035f509f66fe251082b44ae1/data/ingredients/mapped/Sodium_Carbonate_Monohydrate.yaml) | `(adjust if required)` | `RAW_TEXT` → `REJECTED_LABEL` | `sssom_other_backfill` | Preparation instruction, not a substance label; exact NCIt monohydrate source identity. [supplier-carbonate](https://www.sigmaaldrich.com/US/en/product/sigald/230952); [Sodium_Carbonate_Monohydrate--pubchem-cas](https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/rest/pug/compound/name/5968-11-6/JSON) |\n| [`Sodium_Cholate_Hydrate`](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/blob/467aa319a06ef992035f509f66fe251082b44ae1/data/ingredients/mapped/Sodium_Cholate_Hydrate.yaml) | `sodium 3alpha,7alpha,12alpha-trihydroxy-5beta-cholan-24-oate` | `EXACT_SYNONYM` → `REJECTED_LABEL` | `chebi_synonym_review` | Complete owner is hydrated; this systematic token denotes the dry parent. [supplier-cholate](https://www.sigmaaldrich.com/US/en/product/sigma/c9282); [Sodium_Cholate_Hydrate--pubchem-cas](https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/rest/pug/compound/name/206986-87-0/JSON) |\n| [`4-Hydroxymandelic_Acid_Monohydrate`](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/blob/467aa319a06ef992035f509f66fe251082b44ae1/data/ingredients/mapped/4-Hydroxymandelic_Acid_Monohydrate.yaml) | `hydroxy(4-hydroxyphenyl)acetic acid` | `EXACT_SYNONYM` → `REJECTED_LABEL` | `chebi_synonym_review` | Complete owner is hydrated; this systematic token denotes the dry parent. [supplier-168327](https://www.sigmaaldrich.com/US/en/product/aldrich/168327); [sigma-sid-168327](https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/rest/pug/substance/sid/24850239/JSON) |\n| [`5-Fluoroorotic_Acid_Hydrate`](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/blob/467aa319a06ef992035f509f66fe251082b44ae1/data/ingredients/mapped/5-Fluoroorotic_Acid_Hydrate.yaml) | `5-fluoro-2,6-dioxo-1,2,3,6-tetrahydropyrimidine-4-carboxylic acid` | `EXACT_SYNONYM` → `REJECTED_LABEL` | `chebi_synonym_review` | Complete owner is hydrated; this systematic token denotes the dry parent. [supplier-F5013](https://www.sigmaaldrich.com/US/en/product/sigma/f5013); [sigma-deposit-F5013](https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/rest/pug/substance/sourceid/Sigma-Aldrich/F5013_SIGMA/JSON) |\n| [`Cadmium_Acetate_Dihydrate`](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/blob/467aa319a06ef992035f509f66fe251082b44ae1/data/ingredients/mapped/Cadmium_Acetate_Dihydrate.yaml) | `cadmium(2+);diacetate` | `EXACT_SYNONYM` → `REJECTED_LABEL` | `chebi_synonym_review` | Complete owner is hydrated; this systematic token denotes the dry parent. [supplier-102003](https://www.sigmaaldrich.com/US/en/product/mm/102003); [pubchem-cas-5743-04-4](https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/rest/pug/compound/name/5743-04-4/property/Title,MolecularFormula,MolecularWeight,InChI,InChIKey,CanonicalSMILES/JSON) |\n| [`Chlorhexidine_Diacetate_Salt_Hydrate`](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/blob/467aa319a06ef992035f509f66fe251082b44ae1/data/ingredients/mapped/Chlorhexidine_Diacetate_Salt_Hydrate.yaml) | `N,N''''-hexane-1,6-diylbis[N'-(4-chlorophenyl)triimidodicarbonic diamide] acetate (1:2)` | `EXACT_SYNONYM` → `REJECTED_LABEL` | `chebi_synonym_review` | Complete owner is hydrated; this systematic token denotes the dry parent. [supplier-C6143](https://www.sigmaaldrich.com/US/en/product/sigma/c6143); [sigma-deposit-C6143](https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/rest/pug/substance/sourceid/Sigma-Aldrich/C6143_SIGMA/JSON) |\n| [`Cu_No32_X_3_H2o`](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/blob/467aa319a06ef992035f509f66fe251082b44ae1/data/ingredients/mapped/Cu_No32_X_3_H2o.yaml) | `copper(2+) dinitrate` | `EXACT_SYNONYM` → `REJECTED_LABEL` | `chebi_synonym_review` | Complete owner is hydrated; this systematic token denotes the dry parent. [cu-gsrs-current](https://gsrs.ncats.nih.gov/api/v1/substances/066PG1506T?view=full); [pubchem-cas-10031-43-3](https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/rest/pug/compound/name/10031-43-3/property/Title,MolecularFormula,MolecularWeight,InChI,InChIKey,CanonicalSMILES/JSON) |\n\nRetain the synonym text and its source as provenance; change only its type and append the curation history/evidence. Regenerate through the native builder and verify none of these tokens is exported in the affected `other` payloads while unrelated synonyms and all 23 deferred pairs remain unchanged. Tokens already filtered upstream do not justify an invented TSV change. Do not infer a fixed hydrate from a display structure.\n\nDeduplication: #669 owns the broader cross-record preferred-name policy; this exact false-form/source-instruction batch is outside its deferred pairs. Closed #606 addressed the general parenthetical filter; the carbonate YAML source assertion remains to be retagged. #718 remains the umbrella for withheld claims. This issue records the bounded eleven source corrections, not a new alias-selection rule.\n\nUse exact-preimage guarded writers, preserve every historical event and raw source string, synchronize individual/aggregate records and native SSSOM, and append a real timestamped LLM-assisted curation event. Regeneration must be reviewed, pass `just qc` and the full suite, and receive independent final-head review. No role approval, owner merge or unrelated source change is authorized.\n","number":1119,"state":"closed","title":"Retire eleven false owner aliases found in the Step 7d hydrate review","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1119"},{"body":"The approved #901 continuation needs three separately assessed mapping claims for a CAS-primary supplied form: its own CAS identity, a broad form-of parent, and the native B1 registry handle required by MAPPING_SEMANTICS.md section 3. The strict approval-free supersession path landed in #1068 (`e568dbf0e0f759431ed317b973675680f031dd1d`). The existing follow-up intentionally accepts only exact relations, and its added-row path accepts a local primary with an empty alias payload. Those safeguards must remain intact.\n\nAdd a bounded form-review input for the melezitose correction first, followed by a separately reviewed sodium-perchlorate correction from merged main. This is a missing review capability, not a claim that the existing refusal is unsafe. #901 owns the source corrections; #718 retains the broader scientific-review backlog.\n\nAcceptance:\n- Bind each supported claim to its complete row, exact owner bytes, every emitted token, primary-source captures and a validated native scientific assessment of that specific claim.\n- Preserve immutable prior decisions and negative findings. Discharge only the separately settled form-relation hold; retain preparation, grade, foreign-owner and unrelated scientific safeguards in both retained-row and added-row paths.\n- Bind the actual verified receipt endpoint and B1 addition. Allow unrelated later position movement while refusing subsequent changes to the reviewed owner or row, including change-and-restoration cycles.\n- Capture regular repository-confined evidence once; reject conflicting hashes, duplicate or unused claims, stale evidence and mismatched native review pairs. Keep roles and whole-record status outside mapping approval.\n- Prove old CAS support withdraws before the fresh review. Test real assembly/export behavior, legacy refusal, stale and wrong-form cases, and continuity after an unrelated receipt.\n- Regenerate through the native chain, run QC and regression tests, and obtain independent final-head review before queue publication.\n\nNo mapping approval is supplied by this issue, a receipt, a passing validator or the triplet structure itself. No paid provider calls are required.\n","number":1070,"state":"closed","title":"Support explicit CAS-primary form-triplet reviews in the mapping completion assembler","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1070"},{"body":"Generic ingredient mapping must not erase the product/preparation reported by a source. Existing supplied-form and recipe evidence preserves this distinction, but it needs a structured, versioned export for KG-Microbe.\n\nTracking: https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/767. Depends on https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/768; publish alongside the manifest/annotation contract in https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/769.\n\n## Deliverables and acceptance\n\n- [ ] Define stable source occurrence and supplied-form identifiers, with links to the generic ingredient, source recipe/observation, original label, evidence, supplier, catalog number and known preparation/grade. Preserve source detail and uncertainty without inventing lot identities.\n- [ ] Model product alternatives explicitly. Preserve `CultureMech:015191`'s Sigma A9647 **or** A7409 as alternatives, not simultaneous confirmed ingredients, interchangeable identities or a pipe list with unspecified semantics.\n- [ ] Retain all seven reviewed BSA recipe memberships and their own amounts/preparation notes. Sigma A7030 belongs only to its reporting source; do not propagate its heat-shock/low-fatty-acid/protease qualifiers to every BSA occurrence.\n- [ ] Document when occurrence qualifiers suffice and when a separately identified product/material is required because its identity or associated facts differ. A catalog product, a generic ingredient, a stock solution and an individual lot must not be conflated.\n- [ ] Keep supplier/catalog fields out of generic ingredient exact synonyms and identity xrefs. A CAS shared by products does not equate those products; a catalog number alone does not establish molecular identity.\n- [ ] Export deterministic source-linked records with an explicit alternatives/unknown-value contract and schema version. Bind them into the common release manifest and existing evidence/review process, with owner and reference validation.\n- [ ] Add fixtures for the seven BSA occurrences, A9647-or-A7409, distinct products sharing a CAS, missing product information, and unresolved Sorbitan material. Preserve original source payloads and stable recipe references.\n\nImplementation starts from `supplied_form` in `src/mediaingredientmech/schema/mediaingredientmech.yaml` and the existing occurrence/source evidence; do not overwrite source-specific claims with the shared ingredient's attributes.\n\nEvidence and boundaries: [BSA source review](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/tree/ea697252223774072a188ae3ec23a0e2adea0778/reports/sssom_completion_20260921/mapping_review/identity-review-20260924), [#762](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/762). [#449](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/449) owns the broader recipe-membership import defect; preserve/reuse that work. Sorbitan identity evidence remains under [#761](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/761).\n","number":770,"state":"closed","title":"Export occurrence-specific supplied forms and preserve product alternatives","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/770"},{"body":"Independent review of the strict #764 port found a per-plan withdrawal defect in frozen `reports/semantic_review_20260921/resolution/build_review.py` (SHA256 `ed66627f1ffd10decf33ecd32384d37ac99ee49fbdf8d9728b67b79f312c2e6c`).\n\n`identity_support` first indexes original correction plans by owner, then `.update()` replaces them with recovered plans for the same owner. Validation sees only the surviving pin. The historical finding branch then uses that surviving pin's freshness but can cite the original correction plan as its resolving evidence.\n\nThe independent reproduction executes the real frozen `builder.main`, native receipt replay and graph exporter in the existing isolated builder fixture:\n\n- A changed owner with only its original archived identity pin correctly leaves `SEM:identity` OPEN.\n- Add a recovered identity plan for the same owner that binds its current exact bytes, while keeping the original stale plan and finding.\n- The finding becomes RESOLVED and cites `corrections/identity-plan.json`. No identity supersession is recorded, because the old pin was overwritten before validation.\n\nThe unrelated fixed Aromatic hold is omitted as in the maintained fixture; this is an actual builder/provenance counterexample, not a claimed full semantic-gate bypass. The durable corpus has 13 original and three recovered mapped destinations with no overlap, so no current production misclassification is alleged.\n\nRequired repair:\n\n- Retain original and recovered plan identities and archived hashes separately, even for the same owner. Prove withdrawal from each stale plan's own pin.\n- Associate a finding with its actual supporting plan. A current recovered pin must not silently close a finding using stale original-plan evidence.\n- Preserve legitimate current, explicitly scoped approvals and final mapping-only review behavior; add no approval to unrelated roles/components.\n- Add actual-main regressions for overlapping pins, mixed current/stale plans, and incomplete chains, then independently re-review. Keep existing current-corpus dispositions and protected source bytes unchanged.\n\nEvidence: `output/approved-plan-20261101T031134Z/step6-independent/consumers-9d8948/overlapping-identity-pins.json`, SHA256 `bf1e30430c298ddda7d32219638c984e0ff824115bd8a471c38995816ee85d4a`, with executable probe, exact source capture and logs. Actual non-overlap is separately recorded under `step6-independent/preparation/identity-plan-overlap.json`.\n\nDeduplication: three live targeted searches found no duplicate. #740 is the existing stale-plan blocker; closed #850 requires exact archived hashes, and #1063 validates receipt operations. This finding concerns retaining and selecting distinct plan pins before those checks. Search responses and the original issue comments are preserved under `github/`. No production records or scientific decisions were edited by the reproduction.\n","number":1066,"state":"closed","title":"Keep each archived identity pin when initial and recovered plans share an owner","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/1066"},{"body":"The remaining KG-Microbe candidate disagreements include seven NCIT cases, three FoodOn materials and Acriflavine registry history. Review original CultureBotHT names, CAS, supplier/catalog details and current independent authority evidence before replacing the legacy CAS/local identities.\n\nScope: Anabasine Hydrochloride, Cotarnine Chloride, Pretomanid, Sutezolid, Bovine Serum Albumin, Sunflower Oil, Zymosan, Locust Bean Gum, Tara Gum, Sodium Adipate and Acriflavine. Lysozyme invalid-CAS containment remains under #753.\n\nThe original source identifies Sigma A7030 (BSA), Sigma G0753 (locust bean gum) and Biosynth YT58656 (tara gum). Preserve those product details in `supplied_form`, distinct from the generic ingredient identity. Preserve all seven BSA recipe memberships, including their original preparation qualifiers. The current PubChem structures match MIM's stereochemical InChIs for Anabasine Hydrochloride, Pretomanid and Sutezolid; align the less-specific SMILES with that reviewed identity.\n\nFDA GSRS [Acriflavine 1T3A50395T](https://precision.fda.gov/ginas/app/api/v1/substances/1T3A50395T?view=full) records `65589-70-0` as PRIMARY and `8048-52-0` as SUPERSEDED and links NCIT:C76253. Preserve that source-qualified history separately from active CAS annotations; do not claim global withdrawal by CAS.\n\nAcceptance:\n- Archive original source rows, native ontology assertions, authority records and individual material/salt/structure decisions.\n- Promote only reviewed identities; preserve CAS as verified annotations, supplier/preparation details, stable MIM subjects and recipes.\n- Record the source changes through the mapping-change receipt chain and fresh complete-row/owner/evidence-bound reviews. No inherited approvals or changes to provisional roles.\n- Verify actual KG-Microbe lookups, CAS node xrefs, absence of false equivalence, the full candidate and current graph coverage. Production promotion remains gated by KG-Microbe #1123.\n\nImplementation continues in #754 and [KG-Microbe #1130](https://github.com/Knowledge-Graph-Hub/kg-microbe/pull/1130).\n","number":762,"state":"closed","title":"Review remaining NCIT and FoodOn ingredient identities and Acriflavine CAS history","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/762"},{"body":"## Problem\n\n`data/ingredients/mapped/Distilled_Water.yaml` is grounded to generic\n`CHEBI:15377 water`, but its live synonym list grades materially distinct water\npreparations as `EXACT_SYNONYM`, including:\n\n- `Tap water`\n- `Double distilled water`\n- `Demineralized water`\n- `BOUND WATER`\n\nThis makes `docs/data/label_index.csv` resolve those recipe labels to the\nDistilled water record as though the supplied materials were interchangeable.\nThey are not: tap-water composition is source-dependent, demineralization and\ndistillation are different processes, double-distilled water is a distinct\npurity specification, and bound water is not a purchasable synonym for\ndistilled water.\n\nThe labels arrived through kg-microbe synonym enrichment, but ontology synonym\nscope cannot override MIM's supplied-form identity rule. The same distinction\nis now explicit in `MAPPING_SEMANTICS.md` section 3 and\n`docs/HIERARCHY_GUIDE.md` after #448.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Review every synonym on the Distilled water record against recipe-level\n  supplied-form identity.\n- Remove or retype false `EXACT_SYNONYM` assertions without losing source\n  provenance.\n- Decide whether each recipe-relevant preparation needs its own record and\n  ontology/registry/local grounding; do not collapse them merely because ChEBI\n  supplies only generic water.\n- Regenerate the label index and published exports after curation.\n- Harden synonym enrichment so broad ontology synonym imports cannot silently\n  turn distinct material grades/preparations into exact recipe-label identity.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] Tap, double-distilled, demineralized, and bound water are not published as\n      exact synonyms of Distilled water unless evidence establishes literal\n      supplied-form identity.\n- [ ] All retained raw labels preserve their source/provenance.\n- [ ] Label-index resolution reflects the reviewed dispositions.\n- [ ] A regression prevents the enrichment path from recreating these false\n      exact synonyms.\n- [ ] No water/purity hierarchy is invented in MIM; any future relationship\n      model follows the stable-record contract tracked in #236/#447.\n\nRelated: #448, #232, #236, #447.\n","number":470,"state":"closed","title":"Review false exact synonyms that collapse distinct water preparations into Distilled water","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/470"},{"body":"Ingredient scope, canonical identifier choice and source-specific supplied products need separate, machine-readable representations. A generic ingredient must remain queryable without making every catalog preparation equivalent to it, and verified CAS annotations must remain available without becoming automatic identity substitutions.\n\nThis tracks the general producer/consumer contract following the case-specific work in [MIM PR #754](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/pull/754), [KG-Microbe PR #1130](https://github.com/Knowledge-Graph-Hub/kg-microbe/pull/1130) and [NCIT CAS export PR #1131](https://github.com/Knowledge-Graph-Hub/kg-microbe/pull/1131). MIM #754 merged on 2026-09-25; KG-Microbe #1130 and #1131 are ready with passing CI and await independent approval. Production promotion remains under KG-Microbe #1123.\n\n## Required behavior\n\n| Source meaning | Canonical identity | Annotation/context |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Unqualified rifamycin observation | CHEBI:26580 | Family scope; no single CAS; no inference of activity against every member. |\n| Explicit rifamycin SV | CHEBI:29673 | Verified xref cas:6998-60-3. |\n| Generic bovine serum albumin | NCIT:C85253 | Verified xref cas:9048-46-8; supplier/preparation remains source-specific. |\n| Reviewed MIM xanthine ingredient | CHEBI:17712 | Verified xref cas:69-89-6; retain the reviewed ingredient scope. |\n| Generic xanthine trait observation | CHEBI:15318 | Do not inherit 9H specificity or the ingredient's CAS annotation. |\n\nA CAS RN denotes a registered substance/material; a supplier catalog number identifies a commercial product specification. Neither namespace alone establishes scope. SSSOM's logical type slots do not distinguish chemical families from specific chemical concepts. Our canonicalization policy must require reviewed matching scope; SKOS exactMatch is not OWL sameAs and broadMatch does not by itself entail subclass_of.\n\n## Work packages\n\nMIM implementation is merged in #772, #773 and #775, following case-specific #754. The KG-Microbe stack is implemented, fully tested and ready for independent GitHub approval; its default-branch protection remains the merge gate.\n\n- [x] [MIM: scope profile and canonicalization contract](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/768)\n- [x] [MIM: identifier annotations and versioned release manifest](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/769)\n- [x] [MIM: occurrence-specific supplied forms and product alternatives](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/770)\n- [ ] [KG-Microbe: identity predicate and scope guard](https://github.com/Knowledge-Graph-Hub/kg-microbe/issues/1133)\n- [ ] [KG-Microbe: scope/registry bundle consumer](https://github.com/Knowledge-Graph-Hub/kg-microbe/issues/1134)\n- [ ] [KG-Microbe: product/occurrence preservation through KGX merge](https://github.com/Knowledge-Graph-Hub/kg-microbe/issues/1135)\n- [ ] [KG-Microbe: end-to-end acceptance and profile activation gate](https://github.com/Knowledge-Graph-Hub/kg-microbe/issues/1136)\n- [ ] Existing classification work: [MIM #734](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/734) and [KG-Microbe PR #1120](https://github.com/Knowledge-Graph-Hub/kg-microbe/pull/1120). Reuse this work; broad parent anchors must remain broad mappings unless independently supported classification is asserted.\n\n## Sequencing and closure\n\n1. Start the identity-predicate guard and shared scope contract first. The guard can begin independently; its final scope policy follows the agreed profile.\n2. Implement the MIM annotation and occurrence exports against that contract, then their KG-Microbe consumers.\n3. Complete the existing broad-match projection work and run a producer-to-transform-to-merge fixture using immutable inputs.\n4. Enable the new profile only after all required capabilities, bundle hashes, owner-bound reviews and round-trip checks agree. Keep legacy compatibility explicit and auditable.\n\nClosure requires a published contract, reproducible linked artifacts, reviewed handling of conflicts/unknown scope, preserved source alternatives/history, and verified KGX output. Shared CAS must not merge distinct product IDs; parent mappings must not manufacture identity or classification; uncertain materials retain local identities.\n\nExisting case curation remains in [MIM #760](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/760), [MIM #762](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/762) and [KG-Microbe #1132](https://github.com/Knowledge-Graph-Hub/kg-microbe/issues/1132). Lysozyme and Sorbitan evidence holds remain [#753](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/753) and [#761](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/761). Existing-release production promotion remains governed by [KG-Microbe #1123](https://github.com/Knowledge-Graph-Hub/kg-microbe/issues/1123); this roadmap does not silently add every future product feature to that existing gate.\n\nDesign basis: the 2026-09-24 representation review at `reports/kgmicrobe_sssom_feedback_20260922/ingredient-identities/REPRESENTATION.md` in the local MIM workspace. The requirements above and the linked issues are self-contained; that local report is not required to access the plan.\n\nReferences: [SSSOM declared extensions](https://github.com/mapping-commons/sssom/blob/master/examples/schema/extension-slots.sssom.tsv), [W3C SKOS](https://www.w3.org/TR/skos-reference/#mapping), [KGX format](https://biolink.github.io/kgx/kgx_format.html), [CAS REGISTRY](https://www.cas.org/cas-data/cas-registry).\n","number":767,"state":"closed","title":"Track scoped ingredient mappings, registry annotations and KGX integration","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/767"},{"body":"Found reviewing #863.\n\n12 of the 35 local-hydrate records (a `kgmicrobe.compound` identity and a hydrate label) carry an InChI with no water, which is the anhydrous parent's structure:\n- `Cocl2_X_2_H2o`, `Cocl2_X_4_H2o`, `K2hpo4_X_3_H2o`, `Nah2po4_X_2_H2o`, `Na2hpo4_X_7_H2o`, `Nicl2_X_2_H2o` (the six records #863 edits)\n- `Cecl3_X_7_H2o`, `Cu_No32_X_3_H2o`\n- four \"salt hydrate\" records with unspecified hydration (bathophenanthrolinedisulfonate, cefsulodin, chlorhexidine diacetate, DL-isocitrate)\n\nFor example, the CoCl2 hydrates' `inchi` and `smiles` equal CHEBI:35696's exactly, under `data_source: PubChem (via CHEBI:35696)`, while their `molecular_formula` states the hydrate. These values ship in the full KGX `record_json`. Open semantic-review findings SEM:058def08fac3452ea333 and SEM:a7481e9dc9fae5bece11 already name this for the CoCl2 pair.\n\nPrecedent: #646 (`Mgcl2_X_7_H2o`) and #321 (`Fe2_so43_X_N_H2o`) cleared the inherited `chemical_properties` entirely. Clearing everything would also discard values that are right for the hydrate. For example, `Na2hpo4_X_7_H2o` carries the heptahydrate CAS 7782-85-6.\n\n**Proposal:** one reviewed batch that removes the water-free InChI and SMILES (and any parent-derived `data_source`), keeps verified hydrate-specific values, and records a curation event on each record. Decide separately whether the unspecified \"salt hydrate\" records should carry an anhydrous structure at all.\n\nDeferred from #863 so that its record changes stay limited to node ids.\n\n**Reconciliation note (from #876).** #863 removes the stale node ids from the six records named above. Some saved review text still describes those node ids as stale:\n- `reports/sssom_completion_20260921/mapping-evidence.json`, rows 1526 and 1527 (\"kg_microbe_node_id still point at anhydrous CHEBI:131527\") and row 2034;\n- semantic-review findings SEM:3035de2f293076dd9772 and SEM:6973c07a619134de4877 in `finding-dispositions.tsv`.\n\nTheir rows and findings correctly stay WITHHOLD/OPEN, because the anhydrous structure strings tracked here remain. The next reconciliation round should mark the node-id half as resolved.\n\n\n\n## Update 2026-10-14 (second review of #881)\n\nThe same defect exists outside the local-hydrate cohort:\n\n| record | identity | formula | InChI / SMILES |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `Na2hpo4_X_2_H2o` | CHEBI:91258, disodium hydrogenphosphate dihydrate | `2H2O.HO4P.2Na` | anhydrous; ChEBI's own SMILES for CHEBI:91258 has the two waters |\n| `Disodium_Phosphate_Heptahydrate_002_M_Stock` | a heptahydrate stock solution | none | anhydrous InChI, and `molecular_weight: 141.958`, which is anhydrous Na2HPO4 |\n\n`Cu_No32_X_3_H2o`, already listed above, also carries its own CAS, 10031-43-3 (the trihydrate). Its identity question is part of #901.\n","number":871,"state":"closed","title":"12 local-hydrate records carry the anhydrous parent's InChI/SMILES in chemical_properties","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/871"},{"body":"## Problem\n\nThe evidence-aware #317 regrade audit found five records whose explicit source label conflicts with the CAS-selected ChEBI form. These are identity defects, not merely overstated mapping-quality grades, so the automatic regrader deliberately leaves them unchanged:\n\n| source label | current target | CAS | conflict |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Glycyrrhizic Acid, Ammonium Salt | CHEBI:15939 glycyrrhizinic acid | 1405-86-3 | target/formula is the free acid; no ammonium |\n| Colistin sulfate salt | CHEBI:37943 colistin | 1264-72-8 | target formula lacks sulfate; commercial colistin is a mixture |\n| Dimethylsulfoniopropionate hydrochloride | CHEBI:16457 S,S-dimethyl-beta-propiothetin | 7314-30-9 | target is zwitterionic DMSP and contains no HCl/chloride |\n| potassium tellurate | CHEBI:75248 potassium tellurite | 7790-58-1 | source says Te(VI) tellurate while the target/formula is Te(IV) tellurite; ChEBI only carries the former wording as historical related nomenclature |\n| N-lauroylsarcosine sodium salt | CHEBI:183704 [dodecanoyl(methyl)amino]acetate | 137-16-6 | target is the organic anion and contains no sodium |\n\nThe same audit also withheld Stachyose - 70% (a preparation), Ciprofloxacin Hydrochloride (target is a hydrate), and Esculin Monohydrate (target is anhydrous). Existing hydrate/preparation issues may already own those three.\n\n## Why this matters\n\nAll five currently publish skos:exactMatch to their primary identifier. Reclassifying their provenance as CAS_RN_LOOKUP would make the curation metadata more precise while leaving a chemically questionable identity untouched and less visible.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- Trace each label and CAS to its source dataset or recipe.\n- Decide the exact substance/form intended; do not infer a counterion solely from a ChEBI xref.\n- Reground to an exact ontology term when one exists; otherwise mint or retain a registry identity with an appropriately non-identity parent mapping.\n- Correct target-derived formula/structure/CAS fields after any regrounding.\n- Republish SSSOM and verify Rule D only asserts exact identity to the corrected primary identifier.\n- Remove a triple from REVIEWED_IDENTITY_CONFLICTS in scripts/regrade_synonym_matches.py only after that record is corrected.\n\n## Relation\n\n- Found during #317 review.\n- #438 remains orthogonal: own-identifier rows are exact identity rows, so the identifier itself must first be chemically defensible.\n","number":455,"state":"closed","title":"Reground five CAS-selected records with explicit form/counterion conflicts","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/455"},{"body":"Found reviewing PR #266 (#263).\n\nAll four records re-grounded in #263 had `molecular_formula` / `inchi` / `smiles` (and one `pubchem_cid`) written onto them by `AUTO_BACKFILL_CHEBI_CHEMISTRY`, copied from the CHEBI term the record pointed at. When that term is wrong, the backfill silently manufactures wrong chemistry that looks curated.\n\n`Casamino acids` shows the worst of it. It is a casein hydrolysate — an undefined mixture — and was correctly typed `UNDEFINED_MIXTURE` by a rule matching the name. Then:\n\n1. The record was grounded to CHEBI:78020 *heptacosanoate* (wrong).\n2. `AUTO_BACKFILL_CHEBI_CHEMISTRY` wrote heptacosanoate's `C27H53O2` / InChI / SMILES onto it.\n3. `auto_classify_ingredient_type` saw populated chemical structure and **re-typed the record `SINGLE_INGREDIENT`**, overwriting the correct classification — its own history says *\"chemical structure populated (molecular_formula)\"*.\n\nSo one wrong id cascaded into wrong structure and a wrong record type, each step looking like a legitimate automated curation entry in `curation_history`. Nothing flagged the contradiction between `ingredient_type=UNDEFINED_MIXTURE` and \"has a single exact molecular formula\".\n\nWorth considering:\n- **Record provenance on backfilled fields.** If `molecular_formula` carried \"derived from `<curie>`\", re-grounding could invalidate it automatically instead of relying on a curator to notice. (#138 is the same shape: stale derived columns outranking corrections.)\n- **Do not let a derived field drive a classification.** `auto_classify_ingredient_type` promoting `UNDEFINED_MIXTURE` → `SINGLE_INGREDIENT` on the strength of a backfilled formula lets a grounding error rewrite the record's identity.\n- **Flag the contradiction.** A name matching a known complex/mixture pattern *and* a single exact formula is a signal worth surfacing.\n\nPR #266 drops the poisoned fields on the four affected records rather than recomputing them, but does not address the mechanism — any future wrong grounding will do this again.\n\nRelated: #263, #266, #138, #237.","number":268,"state":"open","title":"AUTO_BACKFILL_CHEBI_CHEMISTRY propagates a wrong grounding into structure fields and can flip ingredient_type","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/268"},{"body":"## Problem\n\nThe Garden Soil cleanup in PR #544 tombstoned `MIM:Air-dried_Garden_Soil`, dropped its SSSOM subject, and republished `air-dried garden soil` as an `other` synonym on the exact `MIM:Garden_Soil -> ENVO:00002263` row.\n\nThat made a process-qualified dried soil preparation resolve as exact identity with generic garden soil. Per `MAPPING_SEMANTICS.md` Section 3, a MIM ingredient that is more specific than an available ENVO parent must keep its own registry node and assert a `skos:narrowMatch` to the parent, plus the Rule B1 exact registry row.\n\n## Fix\n\n- Keep Air-dried Garden Soil live.\n- Mint `kgmicrobe.ingredient:air-dried_garden_soil` as the record identifier.\n- Keep `ENVO:00002263` only as the broader ENVO parent through a `skos:narrowMatch` SSSOM row.\n- Emit the sibling Rule B1 exactMatch registry row.\n- Do not add `air-dried garden soil` to Garden Soil's exactMatch `other` cell.\n\nFound by the second adversarial review of PR #544.\n","number":547,"state":"closed","title":"Air-dried garden soil must stay narrower than Garden soil","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/547"},{"body":"The reviewed mappings distinguish generic rifamycin from SV and generic trait xanthine from MIM's ingredient, but the general contract still relies on comments and case-specific consumer policy. Define a machine-readable scope profile so consumers can determine whether a mapping authorizes ID replacement, a broader alignment, or neither.\n\nTracking: https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/767. Priority: foundation; coordinate with KG-Microbe before enabling the profile.\n\n## Deliverables and acceptance\n\n- [ ] Define controlled scope vocabulary and explicit unknown/missing behavior for chemical families, defined substances, mixtures, preparations and catalog products. Document overlapping axes; do not infer scope from identifier namespace, CAS presence or `subject_type=owl:Class`.\n- [ ] Declare application extension slots such as `ext_subject_scope` and `ext_object_scope` through SSSOM `extension_definitions`, with property IRIs, datatypes, profile version and examples. These are application fields, not existing SSSOM standard slots.\n- [ ] Specify which reviewed predicate/scope combinations may support canonical ID replacement. An exactMatch is not automatically sameAs; closeMatch, broadMatch, narrowMatch and annotation claims do not automatically authorize identity substitution. A CAS exact mapping is permitted only after independent same-scope review.\n- [ ] Keep source occurrence resolution separate from concept alignment. Preserve distinct scoped source concepts for generic Xanthine/CHEBI:15318 and MIM:Xanthine/CHEBI:17712; comments or context columns must not make two conflicting exact mappings from the same subject conditionally true.\n- [ ] Define stable references to owner-bound identifier annotations and occurrence records. Keep repeated registry history and product detail out of generic mapping rows unless explicitly associated with the proper subject.\n- [ ] Preserve stable MIM subjects and existing reviewed rows. Record schema/content changes through the existing receipt and complete-row review process; define legacy-profile behavior without silently interpreting missing scope as equivalence.\n- [ ] Validate declared extensions, CURIE expansion, scope/predicate compatibility and deterministic export. Include positive and negative fixtures for Rifamycin/SV, Xanthine, BSA and unresolved local materials.\n\nImplementation anchors: `src/mediaingredientmech/schema/mediaingredientmech.yaml`, `mappings/ingredient_mappings.sssom.tsv`, `MAPPING_SEMANTICS.md`, `src/mediaingredientmech/export/reviewed_sssom.py` and semantic-release validation.\n\nCoordinate with existing [#478](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/478) on composition versus granularity and [#294](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/294) on source-material annotations. This issue defines the export contract rather than duplicating their corpus curation. [#734](https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/734) owns MIM's broad-match KGX projection correction.\n\nReferences: [SSSOM schema](https://github.com/mapping-commons/sssom/blob/master/src/sssom_schema/schema/sssom_schema.yaml), [declared-extension example](https://github.com/mapping-commons/sssom/blob/master/examples/schema/extension-slots.sssom.tsv), [SKOS mapping semantics](https://www.w3.org/TR/skos-reference/#mapping).\n","number":768,"state":"closed","title":"Define a versioned SSSOM profile for ingredient scope and canonicalization","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/768"},{"body":"Found finishing #114's non-chemical disposition.\n\nThe UNMAPPED residual contained **96 records typed `STOCK_SOLUTION`** — named multi-component lab preparations (`Wolfe's vitamin mix`, `Wolfe's mineral mix`, trace-metal and amino-acid mixes). None has an ontology term and none could: a preparation is not a substance. Searching CHEBI, NCIT, MeSH, FOODON and ENVO by label and synonym returned a hit for **2 of 96, and both were false** — `PBS` → `NCIT:C122728` *'TSPO wt Allele'* (a gene allele) and `Trace minerals` → an M-prefixed MeSH concept id rather than a D/C descriptor.\n\nI minted `kgmicrobe.ingredient:` identifiers for the **61 that occur in at least one medium**, following the convention 12 existing mapped `STOCK_SOLUTION` records already use (`FALLBACK_REGISTRY`, no parent — a multi-component mix does not narrow to a single compound).\n\n**I deliberately left the other 35**, which have `total_occurrences: 0`. The reasoning was that an identifier for a preparation nothing uses is not worth the published row.\n\nThat is a judgement, not an obvious call, and it leaves the population half-done. Worth deciding explicitly:\n\n- **Finish them** — consistency, and a zero-occurrence record today may be used tomorrow; the marginal cost is 35 rows.\n- **Leave them** — the SSSOM stays proportional to what is actually used, and zero-occurrence records are the ones most likely to be import noise or duplicates that should be merged rather than minted.\n- **Triage first** — check whether any of the 35 are spelling variants of the 61 already minted (e.g. `Mc_general_salts` vs `Mc_general_salts_SO4free` are genuinely different, but others may not be). That would make some of them merges rather than mints, which is a better outcome than either option above.\n\nI would lean on the third: the zero-occurrence tail is exactly where duplicate/variant labels accumulate, and minting them would fix that population in place rather than deduplicating it.\n\nRelated: #114, #218 (duplicate identifiers), #226.","number":288,"state":"closed","title":"35 zero-occurrence STOCK_SOLUTION records left unminted — decide whether the population should be finished","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/288"},{"body":"Self-review finding on the #323 classifier (PR #328), before it merges.\n\n## The defect\n\nThe classifier has three rule families. Two are sound; one is not.\n\n| rule | assigned | reliability |\n|---|---|---|\n| chemical namespace / structure (CHEBI-with-structure, `cas:`) | 422 | sound |\n| **name pattern** (`serum`, `agar`, `digest`, `solution`, …) | 15 | sound |\n| **biological namespace** (FOODON / MICRO / BTO / ENVO primary → UNDEFINED_MIXTURE) | **10** | **~25% wrong** |\n\nThe namespace rule infers \"this is an undefined mixture\" from *which ontology\nhappened to have a term*, which is not the same question. Wrong on inspection:\n\n- **`Carboxymethyl cellulose (sodium salt)`** → UNDEFINED_MIXTURE because it is\n  FOODON-primary. CMC sodium is a **defined polymer**; the research lane\n  separately recommended `NCIT:C83596 Carboxymethylcellulose Sodium` for it. A\n  degree-of-substitution range does not make it an undefined mixture.\n- **`Bovine albumin`** → UNDEFINED_MIXTURE because MICRO-primary. BSA is a\n  purified protein. (Fraction V preparations are heterogeneous, so this one is\n  arguable — but it is being decided by namespace, not by that argument.)\n\nAlso questionable, from the *name* family rather than the namespace one:\n\n- **`Bile Acid`** → UNDEFINED_MIXTURE on the word `bile`. Bile acids are a\n  **class of steroid acids**, not an undefined biological mixture the way\n  `Bile salts` or `Horse serum` is. The pattern is right for the others and\n  wrong here.\n\n## Why it matters more than 10 records\n\n`ingredient_type` exists to tell an enrichment pass which empty\n`chemical_properties` are legitimately empty (#316). Marking a defined polymer\nUNDEFINED_MIXTURE tells every future pass **not** to enrich a record that could\nbe enriched — a silent, permanent exclusion, and the exact failure mode this\nfield was introduced to prevent, pointed the other way.\n\n## Recommendation\n\nApply the classifier's own stated principle — *a wrong value is worse than an\nabsent one* — to the rule that does not meet it: **drop the biological-namespace\ninference and leave those 10 unset**, alongside the 109 already left for review.\nA curator seeing \"unset, FOODON primary\" will make a better call than the rule\ndoes.\n\nThe name-pattern rule stays; `Bile Acid` is a single-record exception to note,\nnot a reason to drop it.\n\nThat takes PR #328 from 447 assigned / 109 unset to **437 assigned / 119 unset**,\nand removes the only rule family with a measurable error rate.","number":330,"state":"closed","title":"ingredient_type: the biological-namespace rule mis-classifies defined substances","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/330"},{"body":"Found while reviewing SSSOM delivery.\n\nTwo records for cow manure, both with 0 occurrences, both pointing at `ENVO:00003031 \"animal manure\"` — with **opposite** quality grades for the same relationship:\n\n```\n[mapped  ] Dry cow-manure   id=ENVO:00003031   MAPPED     quality=BROAD_MATCH\n[unmapped] Cow manure       id=UNMAPPED_0367   UNMAPPED   quality=NARROW_MATCH\n```\n\nThree separate problems:\n\n1. **They are duplicates.** \"Dry cow-manure\" and \"Cow manure\" are the same material modulo a preparation detail.\n2. **The grades contradict.** Cow manure cannot be both broader and narrower than animal manure. It is narrower.\n3. **`Dry cow-manure` holds `ENVO:00003031` as its identifier while grading BROAD_MATCH.** In MIM the identifier IS the ontology CURIE for a mapped record, so it claims to *be* animal manure while its grade says it is not. Per `MAPPING_SEMANTICS` §3, a record narrower than every available term should carry a registry mint.\n\n`Cow manure` is also `UNMAPPED` while carrying a populated `ontology_mapping`, which is its own inconsistency.\n\n## No more specific ontology term exists\n\nChecked before proposing a mint — `cow manure` returns nothing in ENVO/AGRO/FOODON, and `ENVO:01001116 \"bovine dairy liquid manure\"` is **liquid**, the opposite of \"dry\". So §3 step 1 does not apply and step 3 (mint) is the right instrument.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nMerge the two into one record, mint `kgmicrobe.ingredient:dry_cow_manure` (or `cow_manure`), and emit the asymmetric row to ENVO:00003031 in whichever direction #the SKOS-direction issue settles on. Both have 0 occurrences, so this is low-risk.\n\nNot done in the SSSOM-delivery PR because it is a merge plus a mint plus a direction decision, and that PR was scoped to the single row that lands backwards downstream.\n","number":391,"state":"closed","title":"Two cow-manure records with opposite grades; one holds an ENVO id it grades BROAD_MATCH against","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/391"},{"body":"The supported SSSOM review must not inherit an exact relation when the original review only supports a close parent or when catalog aliases collapse distinct formulations.\n\nPre-finalization full SSSOM SHA256: `aa328df9bab3d7a5cf555c0200d43c6987820268f2b48757d8d203b281d3f809` (3,018 rows). Four rows currently have inherited APPROVED decisions that exceed their evidence:\n\n| Source position | Exact row | Evidence conflict |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1323 | `MIM:Glucose_1-phosphate skos:exactMatch CHEBI:16077` | YAML and its positive report explicitly support CLOSE_MATCH because source stereochemistry is unspecified. |\n| 1518 | `MIM:Isocitrate skos:exactMatch CHEBI:16087` | YAML and its positive report preserve CLOSE_MATCH to a charge-specific species under an assumed pH. |\n| 2658 | `MIM:Soy_Peptone skos:exactMatch FOODON:03315720` | Source and report explicitly describe a close parent, hydrolyzed vegetable protein, not an exact soy-peptone term. |\n| 2153 | `MIM:Nutrient_Broth_No_2 skos:exactMatch MICRO:0000082` | The approved `other` field treats Nutrient Broth No.2 and BD 234000 as catalog aliases even though primary recipes differ. |\n\nFor Nutrient Broth No.2, [NITE NBRC medium 1559](https://www.nite.go.jp/nbrc/catalogue/NBRCMediumDetailServlet?NO=1559) identifies Oxoid CM0067B with 10 g/L Lab-Lemco, 10 g/L peptone and 5 g/L NaCl. [ATCC medium 3](https://www.atcc.org/-/media/product-assets/documents/microbial-media-formulations/3/atcc-medium-3.pdf?rev=7510837507e64d849c62a46b5b2197a1) identifies BD 234000 at 8 g/L, with the scratch formula 3 g/L beef extract and 5 g/L peptone. [The manufacturer](https://www.thermofisher.com/order/catalog/product/CM0067B) also distinguishes No.2 from standard Oxoid Nutrient Broth CM0001. These formulations cannot be certified as one catalog identity simply by removing the No.2 qualifier.\n\nThe original report paths are `reports/yaml_record_review/{Glucose_1-phosphate,Isocitrate,Soy_Peptone,Nutrient_Broth_No_2}.md`. Machine findings include exact assertion hashes, row payloads, report hashes/excerpts and primary-source summaries in the review evidence ledger.\n\nResolution for the present supported-only release:\n\n- Bind explicit WITHHOLD dispositions to all four final row payloads, preserve their full claims and reasons in the separate backlog, and exclude them from the supported SSSOM.\n- Reject inherited ontology `skos:exactMatch` approval when source mapping quality is CLOSE_MATCH, NARROW_MATCH, BROAD_MATCH, PLACEHOLDER or LEXICAL_MATCH unless an explicit row-specific review supports the exact relation and complete alias surface. A self-identifier equality rule is not scientific evidence. Registry rows require their own scoped review.\n- Keep the Nutrient Broth No.2 hold explicit even though its source grade is EXACT_MATCH; this case survives the grade guard.\n- Verify that rebuilding cannot silently reapprove these rows and that final row hashes/dispositions cover every complete SSSOM row. Future corrections can recover source-specific identities without inventing a formula or replacement target in this publication repair.\n\nThis closes the release-approval defect by withholding unsupported claims; it does not claim that the four source identity problems have all been scientifically corrected.\n\n## Export ownership bypass\n\nThe new standalone exporter initially accepted a nonexistent MIM subject when its label matched an existing record, and accepted an incorrect label when the subject matched. All 3,018 current subjects have canonical filename CURIE matches, so no fallback is required. Require both subject and exact preferred label to identify the same MAPPED source record. Add adversarial tests for unknown/mismatched subjects and labels and non-MAPPED owners.\n\n## Further adversarial findings addressed in this branch\n\n- The previously supported 1,2-dichloropropane and calcium chloride dihydrate rows contain concentration-qualified preparations as pure-compound synonyms; sodium nitrate includes an autoclaving instruction; SPIR solution aliases numbered formulations without composition evidence. Preserve these four current rows in the withheld backlog.\n- Resolving #724 initially preserved the original negative review fields but did not validate the historical assertion payload against its digest. A rewritten historical payload could pass. Verify the original payload hash and retain its frozen decision.\n- The graph gate initially checked mapping decision positions without requiring the standalone review to describe the identical canonical SSSOM source and payloads. Require canonical path/hash and complete ordered row equality.\n- Evidence was initially hashed and parsed in separate reads, allowing a file mutation between those operations to substitute a positive proof. Parse the exact verified bytes and recheck the complete input snapshot.\n- Partial output writes could corrupt an existing export. Stage and validate a complete generation before publication, with rollback on failure.\n- A supported asymmetric mapping could lack its required supported registry identity sibling. Validate that subset invariant without automatically promoting an unreviewed sibling.\n- Native SSSOM parsing can log and drop an invalid row before schema validation, which then accepts the empty result. Assert raw/native/manifest row-count equality and the complete identity/label/confidence multiset with duplicate multiplicity; validate numeric confidence rather than treating a quiet parser as proof of completeness.\n- Explicit changed-owner decisions must remain bound to the reviewed row and owner hashes. Freeze the input inventory and verify those fields before adoption.\n\nThese release and integrity defects are resolved by precise withholding, source correction, and regression checks. Source research still needed to rehabilitate withheld claims remains in #718 and the separate mapping backlog; closing this issue does not declare those source claims scientifically corrected.\n\n## Final integration review\n\nThe current-semantic-review link presented prior-release counts and receipts alongside updated ledgers, overstating currency. Mark the prior graph release as historical and point current mapping consumers to the completed standalone SSSOM review.\n\nThe synonym-correction batch also crossed the unified table's accumulated record-drift budget. Regenerate the table using the previously reviewed isolated-input wrapper, retain all explicit rejected-ID protections, record the input/output hashes, and stamp freshness only after a successful rebuild and rejection check.\n","number":730,"state":"closed","title":"SSSOM review: prevent implicit exact-match upgrades and invalid source ownership","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/730"},{"body":"Found while working #208.\n\n`scripts/reground_mapped_record.py` validates the destination against the local ontology adapter and refuses anything it cannot resolve:\n\n```\n$ reground_mapped_record.py --identifier CHEBI:33697 \\\n    --to kgmicrobe.compound:ribonucleic_acid_from_torula_yeast_type_vi ...\nkgmicrobe.compound:ribonucleic_acid_from_torula_yeast_type_vi has no rdfs:label in chebi.db — absent or wrong id\n```\n\nThat check is right for ontology destinations, but it makes the tool unable to express the **MAPPING_SEMANTICS.md Section 3** move — \"no exact ontology term exists, so take a registry identifier (`cas:` / `kgmicrobe.compound:`) plus a `narrowMatch` to the nearest parent\". 248 records already hold `cas:` identifiers in exactly that shape, so the destination class is well established; there is just no tool that can *move an existing mapped record into it*.\n\n## What it blocks\n\nThe #208 RNA split. `Ribonucleic acid from torula yeast type VI` — a specific commercial preparation — currently holds `CHEBI:33697 \"ribonucleic acid\"` at **EXACT_MATCH**. That over-claims (Type VI torula-yeast RNA is not the generic class) and it **occupies the CURIE that the label which genuinely IS generic RNA should hold**: residual `UNMAPPED_0842 \"RNA\"` (microbedecoder, occurrence 1).\n\nThe correct end state is:\n- Torula product → `kgmicrobe.compound:ribonucleic_acid_from_torula_yeast_type_vi`, `narrowMatch` → CHEBI:33697, plus the Rule B1 registry row;\n- `RNA` → promoted onto the freed CHEBI:33697.\n\nThe second step is exactly what `reground_mapped_record.py`'s docstring advertises (\"the freed CURIE becomes available, which is usually the point\"), but the first step cannot be run.\n\n**Not worked around.** Hand-editing is what #267 was filed about — it is how PR #266 minted two duplicate identifiers. #208's actual instruction is \"generic RNA must **not** merge onto the specific Torula-yeast product record\", and that is satisfied: no merge was performed. The record simply stays unmapped until the tool can do the split safely.\n\nSuggested: allow a registry-prefixed `--to` (validated against the known registry prefixes rather than an ontology adapter), require `--parent`/`--quality NARROW_MATCH` when the destination is a mint, and emit the Rule B1 registry row — the logic `anchor_cas_hydrate_records.py` already has for records that arrive with a `cas:` identifier.\n\nRelated: #208, #267, #239, #245.","number":273,"state":"closed","title":"reground_mapped_record.py cannot move a record onto a registry mint, which blocks the #208 RNA split","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/273"},{"body":"From the review of PR #202 (deep-research groundings for the microbedecoder residual).\n\nSix of the seven \"repo convention\" biological/complex CURIEs asserted in `mappings/microbedecoder_residual_research_decomposition.tsv` are confirmed already used in `mappings/ingredient_mappings.sssom.tsv` (Trypticase peptone MICRO:0000175, casitone MICRO:0000606, horse serum MICRO:0001235, carboxymethylcellulose CHEBI:85146, meat-cooked FOODON:03305103, D-gluconate CHEBI:18391).\n\n**The exception: `MICRO:0000520` \"clarified rumen fluid\" appears in zero repo records.** It is used in three proposed decomposition rows (`PY-glucose-rumen Fluid`, `PYG + Rumen Fluid`, `Casitone + Yeast Extract + Rumen Fluid`) and one complex-medium row (`Fermented Rumen Extract`). The research agent already flagged rumen fluid as having no exact term and recommended it as a `broadMatch` only, but the specific CURIE was not independently confirmed against OLS/MICRO.\n\n**Action before applying:** confirm `MICRO:0000520` exists in the MICRO ontology and is labelled \"clarified rumen fluid\" (or find the correct term / UBERON:0010228 ruminal fluid), and decide whether to mint a `kgmicrobe.compound:`/placeholder for rumen fluid as the report's next-steps section suggests. Same open question applies to corn steep liquor (no FOODON/CHEBI term) and the TYGVS VFA mixture.\n\nRelated: #200 (chebi.db accession-ceiling refresh, which unblocks the 6 out-of-coverage antibiotics in the same PR).","number":204,"state":"closed","title":"Verify MICRO:0000520 (clarified rumen fluid) before applying microbedecoder residual decomposition","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/204"}]
